window.QUESTIONS = [{"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 62, "stem": "An Opportunity needs to have an amount rolled up from a custom object that is not in a master-detail relationship. How can this be achieved?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the Streaming API to create real-time roll-up summaries."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Write a trigger on the Opportunity object and use an aggregate function to sum the amount for all related child objects under the Opportunity."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Write a trigger on the child object and use an aggregate function to sum the amount for all related child objects under the Opportunity."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Metadata API to create real-time roll-up summaries."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Native roll-up summary fields are only available on the master side of a master-detail relationship, so when the relationship to the custom object is a lookup instead, Salesforce provides no declarative aggregation path. The Apex Developer Guide's standard pattern for this gap is to write a trigger on the child (lookup) object's insert/update/delete/undelete events, use a SOQL aggregate function (such as SUM in a GROUP BY query, or an AggregateResult) to total the amounts for all children sharing the same Opportunity Id, and then update that total onto the parent Opportunity record. Incorrect - B: A trigger on the Opportunity object only fires when the Opportunity itself is inserted or updated; it does not fire when the unrelated child custom object records are created, updated, or deleted, so it could never notice a new child amount and recalculate the rollup - the trigger has to live on the object whose changes should trigger recalculation. Incorrect - A: The Streaming API (PushTopics, generic streaming, CDC) is designed to notify external subscribers of record changes in near-real time; it has no built-in aggregation engine and cannot write a summarized value back onto a Salesforce field by itself. Incorrect - D: The Metadata API is used to deploy and retrieve org configuration (fields, layouts, classes, and so on) as part of a release/deployment process; it has no runtime role in calculating or maintaining live aggregate values on records, so it cannot implement a rollup.", "id": 1, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 61, "stem": "A credit card company needs to implement functionality for a service agent to process damaged or stolen credit cards. When the customers call in, the service agent must gather many pieces of information. A developer is tasked to implement this functionality. What should the developer use to satisfy this requirement in the most efficient manner?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Apex trigger"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Screen-based flow"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lightning Component"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Approval process"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Flow Builder's screen flow type is purpose-built for exactly this scenario - a guided, multi-step data-collection wizard that an agent walks through live on a call, with built-in screen components, branching logic, and the ability to create/update records at the end - all declaratively and with no code, which is why Salesforce documentation recommends screen flows as the 'most efficient' choice whenever a guided UI-driven process is described. Incorrect - A: An Apex trigger only executes automatically in response to DML on a record (insert/update/delete); it has no user interface and cannot 'gather many pieces of information' interactively from an agent during a live call, so it is the wrong tool category entirely for a UI-driven data-collection task. Incorrect - C: A custom Lightning (Aura or LWC) Component could technically be built to do this, but it requires writing and maintaining JavaScript/Apex code, which is far less efficient than using the declarative, purpose-built Screen Flow feature for a straightforward guided-questions scenario - the question specifically asks for the most efficient approach. Incorrect - D: An Approval process automates a record moving through a sequence of approval steps/approvers (like a discount or expense sign-off); it is not designed to interactively collect free-form information from a user in real time and has no screen/wizard capability for that purpose.", "id": 2, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 60, "stem": "A Developer Edition org has five existing accounts. A developer wants to add 10 more accounts for testing purposes. The following code is executed in the Developer Console using the Execute Anonymous window: Account myAccount = new Account(Name = 'MyAccount'); insert myAccount; Integer x = 1; List<Account> newAccounts = new List<Account>(); do { Account acct = new Account(Name = 'New Account ' + x++); newAccounts.add(acct); } while (x < 10); How many total accounts will be in the org after this code is executed?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "5"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "6"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "10"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "15"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - B: Reading the code literally line by line, `insert myAccount;` is an actual DML statement that commits one new Account to the database, bringing the count from 5 to 6. The do-while loop that follows only ever calls `newAccounts.add(acct)`, which appends each new in-memory Account object to a List variable held in the running Apex context - at no point does the snippet call `insert newAccounts;` or any other DML statement on that list, so none of those nine constructed-but-never-inserted Account objects are ever persisted to the database. When Execute Anonymous finishes, that List and its contents simply vanish along with the rest of the transient heap state. The final count is therefore the original 5 plus the 1 that was genuinely inserted, equal to 6. Incorrect - A (the screenshot's marked answer, 5): this would only be true if `insert myAccount;` had somehow failed or not executed, but nothing in the snippet throws an exception or prevents that first insert from succeeding, so ignoring it and leaving the count at 5 is a misread of the code. Incorrect - C (10): this would require inserting exactly 10 accounts, but only 1 insert statement (for myAccount) actually exists in the code - there is no insert for the batch of 9 built in the loop, so 10 could only be reached by wrongly assuming the loop's records got saved and even then the arithmetic (5 existing + 10 = 15, not 10) wouldn't match. Incorrect - D (15): this is what the total WOULD be (5 existing + 1 inserted + 9 from the loop) if the code had included an `insert newAccounts;` statement after the loop - but it does not, which is precisely the trap the question is testing: recognizing that building a List in Apex has no effect on the database until DML is explicitly performed on it.", "id": 3, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 59, "stem": "Universal Containers wants to assess the advantages of declarative development versus programmatic customization for specific use cases in its Salesforce implementation. What are two characteristics of declarative development over programmatic customization?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Declarative development does not require Apex test classes."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Declarative development has higher design limits and query limits."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Declarative code logic does not require maintenance or review."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Declarative development can be done using the Setup menu."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: By definition, declarative customization means configuring the platform's built-in point-and-click tools (Object Manager, Flow Builder, validation rules, page layouts, and so on), all of which are reached from and built inside the Setup menu, with no code editor or IDE involved. Correct - A: Salesforce's deployment requirements (75% aggregate Apex code coverage, at least some coverage per trigger) apply specifically to Apex classes and triggers; because declarative tools like Flow and validation rules are not Apex, they carry no code-coverage or test-class obligation at all, which is a genuine efficiency advantage the question is testing. Incorrect - B: Declarative automation is still bound by the same platform governor and design limits as everything else (for example, a Flow still has SOQL query and DML limits per transaction context, and objects still have field/relationship limits) - declarative tools do not receive any special elevated limit ceiling compared to Apex, so this claim is false. Incorrect - C: This is a trap that sounds appealing but is factually wrong - Flows, validation rules, and other declarative logic absolutely still need ongoing maintenance and review as business requirements change, org complexity grows, or Salesforce deprecates/changes underlying behavior (for example, the well-documented migration effort many orgs underwent moving from Workflow Rules/Process Builder to Flow); 'no maintenance' is never true of any customization, declarative or not.", "id": 4, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 58, "stem": "What should a developer do to check the code coverage of a class after running all tests?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "View the code coverage percentage for the class using the Overall Code Coverage panel in the Developer Console Tests tab."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "View the Code Coverage column in the list view on the Apex Classes page."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "View the Class Test Percentage tab on the Apex Class list view in Salesforce Setup."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Select and run the class on the Apex Test Execution page in the Developer Console."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: After running Apex tests from the Developer Console's Test menu, the Tests tab displays an 'Overall Code Coverage' panel that lists every class touched by the run alongside its individual coverage percentage and which lines were or were not exercised - this is the documented, built-in way to inspect per-class coverage once tests have finished. Incorrect - B: The standard Apex Classes list view in Setup does not include a live 'Code Coverage' column by default; code coverage is reported through the Developer Console (or the Apex Test Execution page's results, or the Tooling/Metadata API), not as a sortable column on that Setup list view. Incorrect - C: There is no 'Class Test Percentage' tab anywhere in Salesforce Setup - this option is a fabricated UI element that does not exist, designed to sound plausible to someone unfamiliar with the actual Developer Console UI. Incorrect - D: The Apex Test Execution page (also in the Developer Console) is where you select and kick off test classes to run, and it will show a running total of overall org coverage once tests complete, but the question asks specifically how to check a particular class's coverage after tests have already run - that per-class breakdown is what the Overall Code Coverage panel on the Tests tab provides, not the Test Execution launcher screen itself.", "id": 5, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 57, "stem": "What are two characteristics related to formulas?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Formulas are calculated at runtime and are not stored in the database."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Fields that are used in a formula field can be deleted or edited without editing the formula."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Formulas can reference themselves."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Formulas can reference values in related objects."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per Salesforce Help's documentation on formula fields, a formula field's value is computed on the fly every time the record is displayed, queried, or exported - it has no dedicated column value physically stored in the underlying database, which is why formula fields cannot be used as the target of DML writes and don't count toward certain storage limits. Correct - D: Formulas can traverse relationship fields (for example, using dot notation like Account.Owner.Name, or cross-object merge fields) to pull in and calculate against values from parent records, letting a formula on a child object reference data that physically lives on a related object. Incorrect - B: If you delete or fundamentally change the type of a field that a formula currently references, Salesforce blocks the deletion (or flags the formula as broken) because the formula's compiled logic depends on that exact field existing with a compatible type - fields referenced by active formulas are protected specifically because removing them would break the formula, so this statement inverts the real, well-known dependency behavior. Incorrect - C: Formula fields cannot reference themselves, directly or indirectly through a circular chain of other formula fields - Salesforce's metadata validation explicitly detects and rejects circular formula references at save time, since a self-referencing formula would have no way to resolve to a single computed value.", "id": 6, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 56, "stem": "A developer considers the following snippet of code. Based on this code, what is the value of x?", "code": "Boolean isOK;\nInteger x;\nString theString = 'Hello';\n\nif (isOK == false && theString == 'Hello') {\n    x = 1;\n} else if (isOK == true && theString == 'Hello') {\n    x = 2;\n} else if (isOK != null && theString == 'Hello') {\n    x = 3;\n} else {\n    x = 4;\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "2"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "1"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "4"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "3"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C (x = 4): isOK is declared as a Boolean with no assigned literal, so it defaults to null rather than false - Apex reference/wrapper types (Boolean, Integer, String, sObjects) always initialize to null unless explicitly assigned. Each conditional in turn tests isOK against a concrete value: isOK == false is false because a null is not equal to the primitive false, isOK == true is likewise false, and isOK != null is false because isOK genuinely equals null. Since theString == 'Hello' is true throughout, the outcome of each branch hinges entirely on the isOK comparison, all three of which fail, so execution falls through every else-if into the final else, setting x to 4. Incorrect - A (x = 2) requires isOK == true to evaluate true, which cannot happen while isOK is null. Incorrect - B (x = 1) requires isOK == false to evaluate true, but comparing null to the primitive false in Apex yields false, not true - a classic three-state Boolean trap (true / false / null) that PD1 exams test repeatedly. Incorrect - D (x = 3) requires isOK != null to evaluate true, which is false precisely because isOK is null.", "id": 7, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 55, "stem": "A lead developer creates an Apex interface called PaymentProcessor. A developer must implement a CheckPaymentProcessor class that provides check processing payment capabilities. Which implementation is correct to use the PaymentProcessor interface class?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "public class CheckPaymentProcessor extends PaymentProcessor { public void pay(Decimal amount) { // functional code here } }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "public class CheckPaymentProcessor implements PaymentProcessor { public void pay(Decimal amount) { // functional code here } }"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "public class CheckPaymentProcessor extends PaymentProcessor { public void pay(Decimal amount); }"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "public class CheckPaymentProcessor implements PaymentProcessor { public void pay(Decimal amount); }"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per the Apex Developer Guide's rules on interfaces, a class that adopts an interface must use the implements keyword (not extends, which is reserved for inheriting from a virtual or abstract class), and it must provide a complete, concrete method body for every method declared in that interface - a class cannot be compiled with a bare method signature and no implementation. Option B correctly uses implements and supplies a full body for pay(Decimal amount), satisfying both rules. Incorrect - A and C: extends is Apex's keyword for class inheritance (subclassing a virtual/abstract class), not for adopting an interface; using extends against an interface name like PaymentProcessor is a compile error, so both A and C are invalid regardless of whether they include a method body. Incorrect - D: This option correctly uses implements, but declares pay(Decimal amount); with a semicolon and no method body - that syntax is only legal for interface or abstract method declarations, not for a concrete class implementing an interface, so it also fails to compile.", "id": 8, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 54, "stem": "A developer wrote Apex code that calls out to an external system using REST API. How should a developer write the test to prove the code is working as intended?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Write a class that extends WebServiceMock."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Write a class that implements WebServiceMock."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Write a class that implements HTTPCalloutMock."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Write a class that extends HTTPCalloutMock."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: The Apex Developer Guide documents the HttpCalloutMock interface as the mechanism for mocking any HTTP/REST-based callout in a test - a test class implements that interface's respond(HttpRequest req) method to return a synthetic HttpResponse, and the test registers it with Test.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, new MyMock()) before invoking the code under test, so the real external endpoint is never actually called during the test run (Apex tests are not allowed to make live callouts). Incorrect - B and A: WebServiceMock (and its companion Test.setMock(WebServiceMock.class, ...)) is the interface used specifically for mocking SOAP web-service callouts generated from a WSDL2Apex class, not REST/HTTP callouts - using it for a REST scenario is the wrong interface for the transport protocol involved; additionally, interfaces are adopted with implements, not extends, so option A is doubly wrong. Incorrect - D: Interfaces in Apex are always adopted with implements; extends is reserved for class inheritance, so 'extends HTTPCalloutMock' would not even compile, on top of being conceptually mismatched to the interface-adoption pattern the platform requires for mocks.", "id": 9, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 53, "stem": "A lead developer creates an Apex interface called Laptop. Consider the following code snippet. How can a developer use the Laptop interface within the SilverLaptop class?", "code": "public class SilverLaptop {\n    //code implementation\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "public class SilverLaptop extends Laptop"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "@Extends(class=\"Laptop\") public class SilverLaptop"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "@Interface(class=\"Laptop\") public class SilverLaptop"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "public class SilverLaptop implements Laptop"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Apex Developer Guide, a class adopts (uses) an interface by declaring `implements InterfaceName` in its class signature, after which it must provide concrete bodies for every method the interface declares - this is the one and only correct syntax for interface adoption in Apex. Incorrect - A: extends is the keyword for class inheritance from a virtual or abstract superclass, not for interfaces; attempting to extend an interface like Laptop produces a compile-time error because interfaces and classes are inherited/adopted through entirely different keywords. Incorrect - B and C: @Extends and @Interface are not real Apex annotations - Apex has a fixed, documented set of annotations (such as @isTest, @AuraEnabled, @InvocableMethod, @future), and neither of these appears anywhere in that list; they are fabricated distractors meant to look like plausible syntax to someone unfamiliar with the actual annotation catalog.", "id": 10, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 52, "stem": "What are two considerations for running a flow in debug mode?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Callouts to external systems are not executed when debugging a flow."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Input variables of type record cannot be passed into the flow."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Clicking Pause or executing a Pause element closes the flow and ends debugging."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "DML operations will be rolled back when the debugging ends."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per Salesforce's Flow Builder debug documentation, when you debug a flow, any Action or Apex element that would perform an external callout is skipped rather than actually executed against the live external system, since debug mode is meant to be a safe, side-effect-free way to trace a flow's internal logic without touching real integrations. Correct - D: All DML performed by the flow's elements while in debug mode (Create/Update/Delete Records) is rolled back once the debug session finishes, so a developer can safely debug flows that touch real data without permanently altering the org's records. Incorrect - B: Debug mode fully supports passing in input variables of any type, including record variables, through the debug dialog before starting the run - this is explicitly how you simulate the flow being launched with real record context (for example, simulating a record-triggered flow's $Record input). Incorrect - C: A Pause element (or clicking a Pause-equivalent action) suspends a flow interview to resume later in production use, but in debug mode Salesforce's documentation notes that pausing simply ends that debug run's step-by-step trace rather than literally 'closing' debugging as a feature - regardless, the more precisely tested/documented considerations for the exam are the callout-skipping and DML-rollback behaviors, which is why A and D are the two credited answers.", "id": 11, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 51, "stem": "Which code statement includes an Apex method named updateAccounts in the class AccountController for use in a Lightning web component?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "import updateAccounts from '@salesforce/apex/AccountController';"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "import updateAccounts from 'AccountController.updateAccounts';"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "import updateAccounts from 'AccountController';"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "import updateAccounts from '@salesforce/apex/AccountController.updateAccounts';"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide's documentation on calling Apex methods, the required import syntax is `import methodName from '@salesforce/apex/Namespace.ClassName.methodName'` - the scoped module specifier must include the '@salesforce/apex/' prefix AND the fully qualified class name AND the specific method name, all three parts together, because each individually imported Apex method is treated as its own distinct module reference by the LWC compiler. Only option D includes all three required parts. Incorrect - A (the screenshot's marked answer): this includes the correct '@salesforce/apex/' prefix and the class name AccountController, but stops short of appending '.updateAccounts' - without the method name, the LWC bundler has no way to know which specific Apex method on that class the import is supposed to bind to, so this import is incomplete and would fail to resolve. Incorrect - B: this omits the mandatory '@salesforce/apex/' scoped-module prefix entirely and instead treats 'AccountController.updateAccounts' as a bare, unscoped module specifier, which the LWC module resolver does not recognize as an Apex method reference at all. Incorrect - C: this has the same missing-method-name problem as A, but additionally also drops the required '@salesforce/apex/' prefix, making it invalid on two counts rather than one.", "id": 12, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 50, "stem": "A developer must troubleshoot to pinpoint the causes of performance issues when a custom page loads in their org. Which tool should the developer use to troubleshoot query performance?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Developer Console"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Visual Studio Code IDE"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "AppExchange"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Setup Menu"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Developer Console is Salesforce's documented built-in diagnostic tool for this exact purpose - its Query Plan feature (accessible from the query editor) shows the optimizer's cost estimate, cardinality, and which index (if any) a SOQL query will use, and its execution log Analysis/Timeline view breaks down exactly how much time each SOQL query, DML statement, and method call consumed during a page load, making it the purpose-built tool for pinpointing query-driven performance problems. Incorrect - B: VS Code with the Salesforce Extension Pack is excellent for authoring and deploying Apex, LWC, and other metadata, and can retrieve/view raw debug logs, but it has no equivalent to the Developer Console's dedicated Query Plan analyzer, so it is not the documented go-to tool for this specific diagnostic task. Incorrect - C: AppExchange is Salesforce's marketplace for browsing and installing third-party or Salesforce-built packages and apps; it has no diagnostic or performance-analysis capability of its own and is unrelated to troubleshooting an org's own custom page. Incorrect - D: The Setup Menu is where administrators configure org metadata, users, and features; while some performance-related settings live there, it does not provide query-plan analysis or execution-log breakdowns the way the Developer Console does.", "id": 13, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 49, "stem": "Which two settings must be defined in order to update a record of a junction object?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Read access on the primary relationship"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Read/Write access on the secondary relationship"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Read/Write access on the junction object"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Read/Write access on the primary relationship"}], "marked": ["B", "D"], "correct": ["B", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B and D: A junction object in a many-to-many relationship has two master-detail relationships, conventionally called the primary and secondary relationship, each pointing to one of the two objects being related. Per Salesforce Help's documentation on many-to-many relationships and sharing, because a junction record's own visibility and edit rights are controlled by its two parent relationships (not by an independent object-level permission of its own in the way a normal object works), a user must have Read/Write access on BOTH the primary relationship (D) and the secondary relationship (B) before they can successfully update a junction record - lacking write access on either side blocks the update. Incorrect - A: Read-only access on the primary relationship is insufficient for an update operation; the requirement specifically needs Read/Write (edit) access, not just Read, on that relationship, so an option offering only Read access does not satisfy the 'update a record' scenario. Incorrect - C: There is no separate, independent 'access on the junction object' setting distinct from the access granted through its two master-detail relationships - unlike a normal custom object with its own object permissions, a junction object's CRUD behavior for this purpose is governed by permissions on its two relationships to the master objects, which is precisely why B and D (not a standalone junction-object permission) are the credited pair.", "id": 14, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 48, "stem": "What are three capabilities of the <ltng:require> tag when loading JavaScript resources in Aura components?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Loading externally hosted scripts"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "One-time loading for duplicate scripts"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Specifying loading order"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Loading files from Documents"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Loading scripts in parallel"}], "marked": ["B", "C", "E"], "correct": ["B", "C", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B, C, E: Per the Lightning Aura Components Developer Guide's documentation on the <ltng:require> tag, it is used to load JavaScript and CSS static resources into a component and provides three documented conveniences: it automatically deduplicates requests so the same script referenced by multiple components in a page is only loaded once (B); it supports a 'scripts' attribute where resources listed are loaded in the specified sequential order when load order matters, such as a library needing to load before a plugin that depends on it (C); and, when order does not matter, multiple resources can be loaded in parallel for faster page performance (E). Incorrect - A: <ltng:require> is documented as a mechanism for loading static resources uploaded into the org (via the Static Resources feature), not for pulling in arbitrary externally hosted third-party scripts from outside domains, which Lightning's security model does not permit through this tag. Incorrect - D: There is no capability to load files from the legacy Salesforce Documents object through <ltng:require>; the tag is scoped specifically to static resources, not Documents, which is a different, largely deprecated storage mechanism for files.", "id": 15, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 47, "stem": "A developer has a single custom controller class that works with a Visualforce Wizard to support creating and editing multiple sObjects. The wizard accepts data from user inputs across multiple Visualforce pages and from a parameter on the initial URL. Which three statements are useful inside the unit test to effectively test the custom controller?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "insert pageRef;"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "String nextPage = controller.save().getUrl();"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "ApexPages.currentPage().getParameters().put('input', 'TestValue');"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "public ExtendedController(ApexPages.StandardController cntrl) { }"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Test.setCurrentPage(pageRef);"}], "marked": ["B", "C", "E"], "correct": ["B", "C", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - E: Per the Apex Developer Guide's Visualforce testing documentation, Test.setCurrentPage(pageRef) establishes which Visualforce PageReference the test is simulating as the 'current page,' which is required before ApexPages.currentPage() calls elsewhere in the controller will resolve correctly during the test. Correct - C: Because the wizard reads a value from a URL parameter on its initial page, the test must simulate that URL parameter by calling ApexPages.currentPage().getParameters().put('input', 'TestValue') after setting the current page, so the controller's constructor logic that reads the parameter has something to read. Correct - B: To verify the wizard's multi-page navigation logic, the test calls the controller's save() (or next()) action method and inspects the PageReference it returns via .getUrl(), confirming the wizard advances to the correct subsequent page given the test's inputs. Incorrect - D: This is a constructor signature declaration, not an executable test statement - it shows how the ExtendedController class itself is declared to accept a StandardController, but writing that signature inside a test method accomplishes nothing; the test would instead need to call `new ExtendedController(stdController)` to instantiate it. Incorrect - A: `insert pageRef;` is invalid Apex - a PageReference is not an sObject and cannot be inserted via DML; this option is a nonsensical combination of a Visualforce navigation object and a database operation that does not apply to it.", "id": 16, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 46, "stem": "A company decides to implement a new process where every time an Opportunity is created, a follow up Task should be created and assigned to the Opportunity Owner. What is the most efficient way for a developer to implement this?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Record-triggered flow on Opportunity"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Apex trigger on Task"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Auto-launched flow on Task"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Task actions"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: A record-triggered flow configured on the Opportunity object to run 'when a record is created' can, entirely declaratively, use a Create Records element to insert a new Task with OwnerId set to the Opportunity's OwnerId field - this is Flow Builder's documented pattern for 'automatically create a related record whenever X happens' and needs no code, making it the most efficient implementation among the options given. Incorrect - B: An Apex trigger on Task only executes in response to DML performed on Task records; since the actual triggering business event here is an Opportunity being created (not a Task), a trigger placed on Task would never fire as a result of that Opportunity creation, so this misplaces the automation on the wrong object entirely. Incorrect - C: An auto-launched flow has no trigering context of its own - it must be invoked by something else (Apex, Process Builder, another flow, or a record-triggered flow); simply being 'on Task' gives it no listening mechanism tied to Opportunity creation, so nothing would ever launch it under the stated requirement without still needing a separate flow or trigger on Opportunity to call it, which is strictly more complex than option A. Incorrect - D: 'Task actions' (quick actions on the Task object, such as Log a Call) are manually invoked by a user from the UI; nothing about a Task quick action fires automatically in response to an Opportunity being created, so it cannot satisfy an automatic, event-driven requirement.", "id": 17, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 45, "stem": "Universal Containers wants to back up all of the data and attachments in its Salesforce org once a month. Which approach should a developer use to meet this requirement?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Create a Schedulable Apex class."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the Data Loader command line."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Define a Data Export scheduled job."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Schedule a report."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Salesforce Help documents the native Data Export Service (Setup > Data Export) as a built-in feature that can be scheduled to run automatically on a weekly or monthly cadence, generating a full export of the org's data and attached files as a set of downloadable CSV/zip files - this exactly matches the 'back up all data and attachments once a month' requirement with zero custom code. Incorrect - A: A Schedulable Apex class could theoretically be written to run monthly and kick off some export logic, but building, testing, and maintaining custom Apex to replicate functionality the platform already provides natively is unnecessary engineering effort for a requirement the Data Export scheduled job already solves out of the box. Incorrect - B: The Data Loader command-line interface can extract data via scripted jobs, but it requires external scheduling infrastructure (like a cron job on a server, since Data Loader itself doesn't have Salesforce-native scheduling) and separate configuration per object, and it does not natively bundle attachments/files into a single org-wide backup the way the Data Export service does. Incorrect - D: A scheduled report only produces a tabular summary of records matching a report's filters and fields (subject to report row limits) - it cannot export full record data with all fields, cannot include file attachments, and is not designed as a data-backup mechanism at all.", "id": 18, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 44, "stem": "A developer must create a Lightning component that allows users to input Contact record information to create a Contact record, including a Salary__c custom field. What should the developer use, along with a lightning-record-edit-form, so that the Salary__c field functions as a currency input and is only viewable and editable by users that have the correct field-level security on Salary__c?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "<lightning-input-field field-name=\"Salary__c\"></lightning-input-field>"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "<lightning-input-currency value=\"Salary__c\"></lightning-input-currency>"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "<lightning-formatted-number value=\"Salary__c\" format-style=\"currency\"></lightning-formatted-number>"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "<lightning-input type=\"number\" value=\"Salary__c\" formatter=\"currency\"></lightning-input>"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide, lightning-input-field is the base component specifically designed to be used inside a lightning-record-edit-form; it automatically reads the target field's metadata (including its data type, so a Currency field like Salary__c is rendered as a proper currency input with the org's locale/currency formatting) and automatically enforces field-level security - if the running user lacks Read or Edit access on Salary__c, the component hides or disables the field on its own without any extra code from the developer. Incorrect - B: lightning-input-currency is not a real base Lightning component; there is no such tag in the Lightning component reference, making this a fabricated distractor. Incorrect - C: lightning-formatted-number (with format-style='currency') is a display-only component for showing a formatted currency value - it renders read-only formatted text, not an editable input field, so it cannot be used to let a user type in a new Salary__c value when creating a record. Incorrect - D: A generic lightning-input of type='number' with a 'formatter' attribute can visually format a number as currency, but it is a generic, metadata-unaware component - it does not automatically know about or enforce the field-level security permissions configured for Salary__c the way lightning-input-field does inside a record-edit-form, so it fails the 'only viewable/editable by users with correct FLS' part of the requirement.", "id": 19, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 43, "stem": "A developer needs to allow users to complete a form on an Account record that will create a record for a custom object. The form needs to display different fields depending on the user's job role. The functionality should only be available to a small group of users. Which three things should the developer do to satisfy these requirements?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add a Dynamic Action to the Users' assigned Page Layouts."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Create a Custom Permission for the users."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a Lightning web component."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add a Dynamic Action to the Account Record Page."}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Create a Dynamic Form."}], "marked": ["B", "D", "E"], "correct": ["B", "D", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: A Custom Permission is Salesforce's documented mechanism for defining a discrete, assignable permission (via permission sets or profiles) that isn't tied to a specific object or field CRUD setting; here it is used purely as a flag that can be checked in a visibility filter to restrict a feature to a small, named group of users. Correct - D: A Dynamic Action is added to the Lightning App Builder-managed Account Record Page (not a classic page layout) and can have a component-visibility filter condition that checks whether the running user has the Custom Permission from B, satisfying the 'only available to a small group' requirement declaratively. Correct - E: A Dynamic Form breaks a record's fields out into individually placed field components on the Lightning page, each of which can carry its own visibility rule - this is exactly what is needed to show different fields depending on the user's job role, since each field's visibility can be conditioned on a Role or User field value. Incorrect - A: Dynamic Actions are a Lightning App Builder record-page feature, not a Page Layout feature - 'page layouts' (the classic layout editor) do not support Dynamic Actions at all, so this option describes an unsupported combination. Incorrect - C: Building a custom Lightning web component could technically achieve the same outcome, but it requires writing and maintaining JavaScript/Apex code for something the declarative combination of Custom Permission + Dynamic Action + Dynamic Form already accomplishes with no code, so it is not the intended (or necessary) approach given the more efficient declarative tools are available and listed.", "id": 20, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 42, "stem": "Which two operations affect the number of times a trigger can fire?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "After-save record-triggered flow"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Criteria-based sharing calculations"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Roll-up summary fields"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Email messages"}], "marked": ["A", "C"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: If an after-save record-triggered flow performs an Update Records action on the very record (or a related record whose change cascades back), that update is itself a new DML operation, which invokes the object's triggers again as part of the same transaction - this is a well-documented source of a trigger firing more than once per user action, and it is why Salesforce's automation best practices warn about mixing triggers with after-save flows that write back to the triggering object. Correct - C: When a child record change causes a roll-up summary field to recalculate on its master (parent) record, that recalculation writes a new value onto the parent and constitutes a save of the parent record, which re-invokes any trigger defined on the parent object - so simply inserting/updating a child record can cause the parent's trigger to fire even though no explicit DML statement was written against the parent in the code. Incorrect - B: Criteria-based sharing rule recalculation is a background/asynchronous process that updates the org's underlying sharing tables to grant or revoke record visibility; per Salesforce's documentation on sharing recalculation, it explicitly does NOT invoke Apex triggers on the affected records, because it is not performing a conventional DML save of the record's field data. Incorrect - D: Sending an email (whether a workflow email alert, a flow email action, or Apex Messaging.sendEmail) does not perform a DML operation against the record it's related to unless the code separately updates a field (like a 'Last Emailed Date'); merely sending an email is not itself a save and therefore does not invoke or re-invoke a trigger.", "id": 21, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 41, "stem": "Which exception type cannot be caught?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "LimitException"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "NoAccessException"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "A custom exception"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "CalloutException"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "verified-web", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Apex Developer Guide's documentation on governor limits, System.LimitException - the exception thrown the instant a synchronous or asynchronous transaction exceeds a hard governor limit (such as too many SOQL queries or too many DML statements) - is explicitly documented as unable to be caught by a try/catch block; the transaction is terminated immediately and unconditionally when a limit is breached, by design, so that runaway code cannot simply swallow the error and keep consuming shared platform resources. Incorrect - B: NoAccessException (thrown when code attempts to access a field or object the running user lacks permission for, notably via WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED or similar checks) is a normal, catchable exception - a developer can wrap the offending operation in try/catch and handle the access failure gracefully. Incorrect - C: any custom exception a developer defines by extending the base Exception class is fully catchable like any other exception type; there is nothing special about governor-limit exceptions being uncatchable that extends to user-defined exceptions. Incorrect - D: CalloutException (thrown for HTTP/webservice callout failures such as timeouts or invalid endpoints) is also a standard, catchable exception - callout error handling explicitly relies on being able to catch CalloutException to implement retry or fallback logic.", "id": 22, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 40, "stem": "A team of many developers work in their own individual orgs that have the same configuration as the production org. Which type of org is best suited for this scenario?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Developer Sandbox"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Full Sandbox"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Partner Developer Edition"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Developer Edition"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per Salesforce Help's sandbox documentation, a Developer Sandbox is a full copy of the production org's metadata/configuration (objects, code, layouts, automation) with no production data, and any number of them can be created (subject to org licensing) - this makes it the natural fit for 'many developers' who each need their own isolated environment that still mirrors production's setup. Incorrect - B: a Full Sandbox copies both configuration AND all production data/attachments, is far more storage- and time-intensive to refresh, and orgs are typically limited to very few Full Sandboxes - it is reserved for final staging/performance testing, not for handing one out to every individual developer on a team. Incorrect - C and D: Developer Edition and Partner Developer Edition are standalone, free signup orgs entirely disconnected from the company's production org - they do not automatically inherit or stay in sync with production's actual configuration, so they cannot be described as 'having the same configuration as the production org' the way a refreshed Developer Sandbox does.", "id": 23, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 39, "stem": "What are two considerations for deploying from a sandbox to production?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "At least 75% of Apex code must be covered by unit tests."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Should deploy during business hours to ensure feedback can be quickly addressed."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Unit tests must have calls to the System.assert method."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "All triggers must have at least one line of test coverage."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A and D: These are the two hard, platform-enforced deployment gates documented in the Apex Developer Guide - the org-wide aggregate Apex code coverage across all classes and triggers must be at least 75% (A), and independently, every single Apex trigger must individually have greater than 0% of its lines covered (D); a deployment is blocked by the validation/deploy pipeline if either condition is not met, regardless of how good the code otherwise is. Incorrect - C: while writing meaningful assertions (System.assertEquals, Assert.areEqual, etc.) is universally recommended testing best practice, Salesforce's deployment validation does not actually inspect whether assertions exist in a test method - it only measures whether lines of the class/trigger under test were executed, so a test with zero assertions can still 'pass' coverage requirements even though it's poor practice. Incorrect - B: deploying during business hours is an operational/change-management recommendation some teams choose to follow for faster incident response, but it is not a Salesforce platform requirement or technical consideration for whether a deployment succeeds - in fact many teams deliberately deploy during low-traffic maintenance windows instead, the opposite of business hours.", "id": 24, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 38, "stem": "How is a controller and extension specified for a custom object named \"Notice\" on a Visualforce page?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "<apex:page controller=\"Notice__c\" extensions=\"myControllerExtension\">"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "<apex:page standardController=\"Notice__c\" extensions=\"myControllerExtension\">"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "<apex:page controllers=\"Notice__c, myControllerExtension\">"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "<apex:page=Notice extends=\"myControllerExtension\">"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The stem describes a scenario where 'Notice__c' itself IS the custom Apex controller class name being paired with an extension - per the Visualforce Developer Guide, the controller attribute names a custom Apex class that implements the page's core controller logic, and the extensions attribute lists one or more extension classes that add functionality on top of it; `<apex:page controller=\"Notice__c\" extensions=\"myControllerExtension\">` is the documented correct syntax for pairing a custom controller with an extension. Incorrect - B: standardController is used when the page should be driven by one of Salesforce's built-in standard controllers tied to a standard or custom sObject (e.g., standardController=\"Account\"), automatically providing standard CRUD actions for that object - it is a different attribute for a different scenario (using the platform's built-in controller, not a fully custom one), so pairing standardController with a custom-controller-named value like 'Notice__c' as if it were the object is a mismatch of attributes. Incorrect - C: there is no 'controllers' (plural) attribute in the apex:page component tag - the real attribute names are singular 'controller' plus the separate 'extensions' attribute for any additional extension classes; a comma-separated single attribute is not valid Visualforce syntax. Incorrect - D: '<apex:page=Notice extends=...>' is not valid Visualforce markup at all - apex:page does not have an '=Notice' attribute pattern, and 'extends' is not a recognized apex:page attribute (extends is an Apex class-inheritance keyword, unrelated to page controller wiring).", "id": 25, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 37, "stem": "What should a developer use to fix a Lightning web component bug in a sandbox?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Execute Anonymous"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Force.com IDE"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "VS Code"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Developer Console"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per Salesforce's Lightning Web Components documentation, LWC source files (JavaScript, HTML templates, CSS, and the *.js-meta.xml configuration) are authored and edited using VS Code with the Salesforce Extension Pack (or the browser-based Code Builder, which is also VS Code-based) - these are the officially supported tooling for creating and modifying LWC bundles, including fixing bugs in them. Incorrect - D: the Developer Console is explicitly documented as supporting Apex classes, triggers, Visualforce pages, and (legacy) Aura components, but it has no editor support for Lightning Web Components at all - you cannot create or edit an LWC bundle from within the Developer Console. Incorrect - B: the Force.com IDE was a legacy Eclipse-based plugin that has been officially retired/deprecated by Salesforce in favor of the Salesforce Extensions for VS Code; it also predates LWC's existence as a framework and never supported editing LWC bundles. Incorrect - A: Execute Anonymous (in the Developer Console) is used to run one-off Apex code snippets for testing or data manipulation - it has no relationship to editing front-end LWC source files and cannot be used to fix a component bug.", "id": 26, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 36, "stem": "What are two benefits of using External IDs?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "An External ID can be used with Salesforce Mobile to make external data visible."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "An External ID field can be used to reference an ID from another external system."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "An External ID is indexed and can improve the performance of SOQL queries."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "An External ID can be a formula field to help create a unique key from two fields in Salesforce."}], "marked": ["B", "C"], "correct": ["B", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per Salesforce Help's field-type documentation, marking a custom field as an External ID is specifically meant to hold a unique record identifier that originates from an outside system (an ERP, legacy database, etc.), which is what enables upsert operations to match incoming records to existing Salesforce records using that foreign system's key rather than the Salesforce record Id. Correct - C: External ID fields are automatically indexed by the platform (alongside standard indexed fields like Name, Id, OwnerId, and CreatedDate), so SOQL queries filtering on that field benefit from the selective, indexed query optimizer path instead of a full table scan, meaningfully improving performance for lookups keyed on the external identifier. Incorrect - D: the External ID checkbox is only available on text, number, email, and auto-number field types - Salesforce Help explicitly does not allow a formula field to be flagged as an External ID, because formula fields are computed/derived and not stored the way a true external-key value needs to be for upsert matching. Incorrect - A: there is no documented capability where marking a field as an External ID changes what data is visible or surfaced within the Salesforce Mobile app - visibility in mobile is governed by standard field-level security, page layouts, and mobile navigation settings, none of which are affected by the External ID flag.", "id": 27, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 35, "stem": "Universal Containers wants to ensure that all new leads created in the system have a valid email address. They have already created a validation rule to enforce this requirement, but want to add an additional layer of validation using automation. What would be the best solution for this requirement?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use an Approval Process to enforce the completion of a valid email address using an outbound message action."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use a custom Lightning Web component to make a callout to validate the fields on a third party system."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use a before-save Apex trigger on the Lead object to validate the email address and display an error message if it is invalid."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Submit a REST API Callout with a JSON payload and validate the fields on a third party system."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: A before insert/update Apex trigger on Lead runs in the same save transaction as the existing validation rule and can implement additional custom logic (such as a regex pattern check on the email format) beyond what a declarative validation rule formula can express, and calling addError() on the record from a before-save trigger blocks the save exactly the way a validation rule does - giving a genuine 'additional layer' of programmatic validation stacked on top of the existing rule. Incorrect - B and D: both of these route email validation through a synchronous callout to a third-party system; per the Apex Developer Guide, callouts are explicitly NOT allowed from a before-save trigger context (or any synchronous DML trigger context) because a callout requires releasing the lock on the current transaction, which conflicts with an open DML operation - attempting a synchronous callout during a record save throws a runtime CalloutException, so neither approach could actually execute as described. Incorrect - A: an Approval Process routes a record through a sequence of approval steps and can send an outbound message as a side effect of an approval action, but it does not run inline during the initial save/insert of the Lead and has no mechanism to block record creation the way a validation rule or before-save trigger does, so it cannot provide the same kind of blocking 'additional layer' of validation at creation time.", "id": 28, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 34, "stem": "Given the following Apex statement: Account myAccount = [SELECT Id, Name FROM Account]; What occurs when more than one Account is returned by the SOQL query?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The first Account returned is assigned to myAccount."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "An unhandled exception is thrown and the code terminates."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The variable, myAccount, is automatically cast to the List data type."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The query fails and an error is written to the debug log."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Apex allows an inline SOQL query to be assigned directly to a single sObject variable only when the result set contains zero or exactly one row; per this documented behavior, if more than one row comes back, the runtime throws a System.QueryException with the message 'List has more than 1 row for assignment to SObject' at the moment of assignment - if that exception is not caught in a surrounding try/catch, it propagates up and halts execution of the transaction (an unhandled exception). Incorrect - A: Apex performs no implicit 'take the first row and discard the rest' behavior for this assignment pattern; that kind of leniency would silently hide bugs, which is why Salesforce chose to throw an explicit exception instead - if a developer genuinely wants only the first row, they must add LIMIT 1 to the query themselves. Incorrect - C: the variable's type (a single Account, not a List<Account>) is fixed at compile time by its declaration; Apex is statically typed and does not dynamically widen a variable's type at runtime based on how many rows a query happens to return. Incorrect - D: this is not a query-level failure that gets silently logged - the SOQL statement itself runs successfully and returns valid rows; the problem is specifically in the assignment to an incompatible variable shape, and it manifests as a thrown runtime exception, not merely a debug log entry.", "id": 29, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 33, "stem": "A developer creates a new Apex trigger with a helper class, and writes a test class that only exercises 95% coverage of the new Apex helper class. Change Set deployment to production fails with the test coverage warning: 'Test coverage of selected Apex Trigger is 0%, at least 1% test coverage is required.' What should the developer do to successfully deploy the new Apex trigger and helper class?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Run the tests using the 'Run All Tests' method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Increase the test class coverage on the helper class."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Remove the failing test methods from the test class."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Create a test class and methods to cover the Apex trigger."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The 95% coverage the test achieves is entirely on the helper class, which means the existing test method(s) are calling the helper's methods directly rather than performing DML that would actually cause the trigger itself to execute - since the trigger's own lines of code are never run by any test, its coverage is genuinely 0%, and per Salesforce's deployment requirements every trigger individually needs at least some (>0%) coverage regardless of overall org or class coverage elsewhere. The fix is to add test methods that perform an actual insert/update/delete on the trigger's object, causing the trigger body itself to execute and accumulate coverage. Incorrect - A: simply re-running the same tests via 'Run All Tests' does not change what code those tests actually exercise - if no test performs DML that invokes the trigger, running the identical tests again still produces 0% trigger coverage. Incorrect - B: the helper class is already at 95% coverage, which is well above the 75% requirement - increasing it further does nothing for the specific, separate 0%-trigger-coverage error being reported, since trigger coverage and helper-class coverage are tracked independently. Incorrect - C: removing test methods would only reduce coverage further (or leave the trigger's 0% coverage completely unaddressed) - it moves in the opposite direction of what's needed and does nothing to make the trigger itself execute during a test.", "id": 30, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 32, "stem": "For which three items can a trace flag be configured?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Flow"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Apex Trigger"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Apex Class"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "User"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Visualforce"}], "marked": ["B", "C", "D"], "correct": ["B", "C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B, C, D: Per Salesforce Help's documentation on debug logs and trace flags, a trace flag can be created against a User (capturing everything that user's actions generate, including the Automated Process user for background jobs), an Apex Class, or an Apex Trigger - the last two letting a developer scope detailed logging to a specific class or trigger's execution regardless of which user invokes it. Incorrect - A: Flow does not have its own trace-flag entity type in Setup's Debug Logs configuration - flow interview debugging/logging is handled through Flow Builder's own debug mode and the 'Paused and Waiting Interviews'/flow error email mechanisms, not through the trace flag system used for Apex and users. Incorrect - E: Visualforce likewise has no dedicated trace-flag target of its own; Visualforce page execution details show up in the debug log generated by a User- or Apex-scoped trace flag (since a VF page's controller Apex code is what actually gets logged), but 'Visualforce' itself is not one of the entity types you can directly attach a trace flag to.", "id": 31, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 31, "stem": "An org has an existing flow that edits an Opportunity with an Update Records element. A developer must update the flow to also create a Contact and store the created Contact's ID on the Opportunity. Which update must the developer make in the flow?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add a new Get Records element."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add a new Roll Back Records element."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add a new Update Records element."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add a new Create Records element."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per Flow Builder's documented data element behavior, a Create Records element inserts a new record (here, the Contact) and automatically populates the flow's record variable for that element with the newly assigned Id once the create succeeds - that Id can then be referenced later in the same flow (for example, in the existing Update Records element that edits the Opportunity) to store it on a custom lookup/text field on the Opportunity. Incorrect - A: a Get Records element only queries for EXISTING records matching filter criteria - it has no ability to create a brand-new Contact record, so it cannot satisfy the 'create a Contact' half of the requirement. Incorrect - C: an Update Records element only modifies fields on records that already exist and are already identified/referenced in the flow - it cannot insert a new Contact record from scratch, since 'update' presupposes the target record already exists. Incorrect - B: 'Roll Back Records' is not a real, documented Flow Builder data element - Flow's actual transaction-control element for undoing changes is a Rollback element for fault-path error handling, but it does not create or update records at all, and rollback is not what this requirement (creating and referencing a new Contact) calls for in any case.", "id": 32, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 30, "stem": "A PrimaryId__c custom field exists on the Candidate__c custom object. The field is used to store each candidate's id number and is marked as Unique in the schema definition. As part of a data enrichment process, Universal Containers has a CSV file that contains updated data for all candidates in the system, including each candidate's primary id. Universal Containers wants to upload this information into Salesforce while ensuring all data rows are correctly mapped to a candidate. Which technique should the developer implement to streamline the data upload?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Create a before insert trigger to correctly map the records."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Upload the CSV into a custom object related to Candidate__c."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a before save flow to correctly map the records."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Update the PrimaryId__c field definition to mark it as an External Id."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: PrimaryId__c is already Unique, and per Salesforce Help, marking it as an External ID as well unlocks the ability to perform an upsert operation (via Data Loader, the Bulk API, or Apex) keyed on that field - the platform then automatically matches each incoming CSV row to its existing Candidate__c record by that external identifier and updates it in place, rather than requiring any custom code to figure out the mapping. This is the documented, purpose-built, zero-code solution for exactly this kind of 'match incoming rows to existing records by an external key' data-enrichment scenario. Incorrect - A and C: both a before-insert trigger and a before-save flow only run on records that are already in the process of being inserted through Salesforce's normal save pipeline - they do nothing to help the actual bulk data-loading process find and match the right existing Candidate__c record from a flat CSV file in the first place; building custom mapping logic here duplicates functionality upsert-on-External-ID already provides natively. Incorrect - B: uploading the CSV into a separate, related custom object does not update the existing Candidate__c records at all - it would create a parallel data set that would still need to be correlated back to the original candidates through some other process, adding complexity rather than streamlining the upload.", "id": 33, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 29, "stem": "A developer is tasked to perform a security review of the ContactSearch Apex class that exists in the system. Within the class, the developer identifies the following method as a security threat: List<Contact> performSearch(String lastName){ return Database.query('SELECT Id, FirstName, LastName FROM Contact WHERE LastName Like %'+lastName+'%'); } What are two ways the developer can update the method to prevent a SOQL injection attack?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use variable binding and replace the dynamic query with a static SOQL."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the @ReadOnly annotation and the with sharing keyword on the class."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use a regular expression on the parameter to remove special characters."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the escapeSingleQuotes method to sanitize the parameter before its use."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Apex Developer Guide's secure coding documentation, replacing a dynamically built Database.query() string with a static, inline SOQL statement that uses a bind variable (e.g., WHERE LastName LIKE :searchTerm) is the recommended defense, because bind variables are passed to the query engine strictly as data values - they can never be interpreted as SOQL syntax, no matter what characters the user typed, which structurally eliminates the injection vector rather than trying to filter it. Correct - D: String.escapeSingleQuotes() is Salesforce's documented sanitizer specifically for cases where dynamic SOQL construction with Database.query() genuinely is needed - it escapes any single-quote characters in the input (the character an attacker would use to break out of a string literal and inject additional query logic) before that string is concatenated into the query text, closing off the same vulnerability from a different angle. Incorrect - B: @ReadOnly relaxes certain governor limits for long-running read queries, and with sharing enforces the running user's record-level sharing rules - neither has anything to do with sanitizing or safely parameterizing untrusted input in a dynamic SOQL string, so this pairing does nothing to prevent injection. Incorrect - C: manually writing a custom regular expression to strip 'special characters' is an unreliable, ad hoc, blacklist-style approach that Salesforce's own secure coding guidance advises against in favor of the two purpose-built mechanisms (bind variables and escapeSingleQuotes) - it's easy to miss an edge case or an encoding trick that a hand-rolled regex doesn't anticipate.", "id": 34, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 28, "stem": "A developer is alerted to an issue with a custom Apex trigger that is causing records to be duplicated. What is the most appropriate debugging approach to troubleshoot the issue?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add system.debug statements to the code to track the execution flow and identify the issue."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Review the Historical Event logs to identify the source of the issue."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Disable the trigger in production and test to see if the issue still occurs."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Apex Interactive Debugger to step through the code and identify the issue."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Salesforce's Apex Interactive Debugger (available through VS Code with the Salesforce Extensions, using the Apex Replay Debugger or the live Interactive Debugger against a debug log) lets a developer set breakpoints, step line-by-line through the trigger's actual execution, and inspect variable state at each point - this is documented as the most direct, purpose-built way to pinpoint exactly where and why duplicate records are being created, without guessing. Incorrect - A: adding System.debug() statements can work, but it requires the developer to guess in advance which points in the code are worth instrumenting, then redeploy and reproduce the issue, and re-read verbose logs after the fact - it is a slower, more indirect technique compared to stepping through the code live with the interactive debugger, which is why it is not the 'most appropriate' choice among these options. Incorrect - C: disabling the trigger in production to test is a risky, disruptive experiment performed directly against the live environment that could itself cause data problems or break dependent processes - it is not a safe or appropriate diagnostic technique, and it doesn't actually explain the root cause even if it confirms the trigger is involved. Incorrect - B: 'Historical Event logs' generally refers to Field History Tracking / Setup Audit Trail-style records of past field or configuration changes, not a tool for tracing live code execution logic - it would not show the step-by-step reasoning inside the trigger that caused the duplication.", "id": 35, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 27, "stem": "What are three ways for a developer to execute tests in an org?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Setup Menu"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Salesforce DX"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Metadata API"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Bulk API"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Tooling API"}], "marked": ["A", "B", "E"], "correct": ["A", "B", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A, B, E: Per Salesforce's testing documentation, Apex tests can be launched from the Apex Test Execution page in Setup (A, a point-and-click UI for selecting and running test classes); via the Salesforce CLI/Salesforce DX (B, using commands like sf apex run test, which is the standard way to run tests from a scripted or CI/CD pipeline); and through the Tooling API (E, which exposes objects like ApexTestQueueItem and methods to programmatically enqueue and monitor test runs, used by IDEs and custom tooling). Incorrect - D: the Bulk API is designed for high-volume record CRUD operations (inserting, updating, querying, or deleting large numbers of records) - it has no concept of Apex test classes or test execution and cannot be used to run tests. Incorrect - C: the Metadata API is used to deploy, retrieve, and manage org configuration (classes, objects, layouts, and so on) as part of a release process; while a deployment through the Metadata API can trigger tests to run as a side effect of validating that deployment, the Metadata API itself is not a mechanism a developer uses to directly 'execute tests' on demand the way the other three options are.", "id": 36, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 26, "stem": "A developer wants to get access to the standard price book in the org while writing a test class that covers an OpportunityLineItem trigger. Which method allows access to the price book?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use @TestVisible to allow the test method to see the standard price book."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use Test.getStandardPricebookId() to get the standard price book ID."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use @IsTest(SeeAllData=true) and delete the existing standard price book."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use Test.loadData() and a static resource to load a standard price book."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per the Apex Developer Guide's documentation on testing Pricebook-related code, Test.getStandardPricebookId() is the purpose-built system method that returns the Id of the org's one standard Pricebook, letting a test insert a PricebookEntry against it (which is required before an OpportunityLineItem can reference a Product/PricebookEntry) - and it works without needing SeeAllData, keeping the test properly isolated from real org data. Incorrect - A: @TestVisible is used to expose a private Apex method or member variable to test-context code within the same org's codebase; it has no relationship to Pricebooks or org data visibility and cannot 'reveal' a standard price book that isn't otherwise accessible in tests. Incorrect - C: @IsTest(SeeAllData=true) would let a test see real org data including the actual standard Pricebook, but Salesforce explicitly recommends against SeeAllData as a testing anti-pattern (it makes tests fragile and dependent on org data state), and 'deleting the existing standard price book' as part of a test is both unnecessary and dangerous - there is no reason or mechanism suggesting a test should delete the org's one standard Pricebook. Incorrect - D: Test.loadData() combined with a static resource is used to load arbitrary custom test data (typically custom object records) from a CSV bundled as a static resource - it is not the documented mechanism for accessing the org's single, special standard Pricebook, which has its own dedicated system method precisely because it's a singleton, not a bulk-loadable record set.", "id": 37, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 25, "stem": "A developer must perform a complex SOQL query that joins two objects in a Lightning component. How can the Lightning component execute the query?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Create a flow to execute the query and invoke from the Lightning component."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Invoke an Apex class with the method annotated as @AuraEnabled to perform the query."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the Salesforce Streaming API to perform the SOQL query."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Write the query in a custom Lightning web component wrapper and invoke from the Lightning component."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - B: SOQL is exclusively an Apex/server-side query language - per the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide, any data access beyond what Lightning Data Service's wire adapters natively provide must go through an Apex method decorated @AuraEnabled, which the component then calls (imperatively or via @wire) to run the actual query on the server and return the results as serializable data back to the client. This is the only supported path for a component to get server-queried data, complex joins included. Incorrect - D (the screenshot's marked answer): a 'Lightning web component wrapper' still runs entirely as client-side JavaScript in the browser - JavaScript has no ability to execute SOQL directly against the Salesforce database under any circumstance, no matter how the component is structured or wrapped; this option describes something that is not technically possible on the platform. Incorrect - A: while a screen flow could technically retrieve records via a Get Records element and be embedded/invoked from a Lightning component, that is a far more roundabout path for a 'complex SOQL query' than simply calling an Apex method built for that exact query, and Flow's Get Records element itself doesn't support arbitrary raw SOQL joins the way a hand-written Apex query can. Incorrect - C: the Streaming API (PushTopics, generic/platform events, Change Data Capture) is a publish/subscribe mechanism for receiving near-real-time notifications about data changes - it is not a query mechanism at all and cannot execute an ad hoc SOQL join.", "id": 38, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 24, "stem": "Universal Containers wants a list button to display a Visualforce page that allows users to edit multiple records. Which Visualforce feature supports this requirement?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Standard Controller with Custom List Controller extension"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Standard controller and the recordSetVar page attribute"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Controller Extension and <apex:listButton> tag"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Custom List Controller with recordSetVar page attribute"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Visualforce Developer Guide's documentation on mass-action list buttons, a custom list controller class (one whose constructor accepts an ApexPages.StandardSetController) paired with the recordSetVar attribute on <apex:page> is the documented pattern for building a page that operates on a set of selected records passed in from a list-view button - the recordSetVar attribute is what tells Visualforce to populate the StandardSetController with the list of record Ids the user selected before clicking the button. Incorrect - B: a plain standardController (not a custom list controller) combined with recordSetVar is not a valid/supported combination for this purpose - the recordSetVar pattern specifically requires a controller (custom or an extension) built to accept and work with a StandardSetController of multiple records, not the single-record standard controller. Incorrect - A: 'Standard Controller with Custom List Controller extension' garbles the actual required pattern - the correct approach uses a custom controller (or extension) constructed to accept a StandardSetController via recordSetVar, not a standard single-record controller extended in this way. Incorrect - C: there is no <apex:listButton> tag in Visualforce - list buttons are configured declaratively in Setup on the object's list view button configuration (specifying the target Visualforce page and its recordSetVar handling), not through a Visualforce component tag of that name.", "id": 39, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 23, "stem": "How does the Lightning Component framework help developers implement solutions faster?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "By providing code review standards and processes"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "By providing an Agile process with default steps"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "By providing device-awareness for mobile and desktops"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "By providing change history and version control"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: The Lightning Component framework documentation highlights built-in device-awareness/responsiveness as a core benefit - a single component automatically adapts its layout and behavior across desktop, tablet, and phone form factors without the developer having to build and maintain separate versions for each, which meaningfully speeds up delivery compared to hand-building responsive behavior from scratch. Incorrect - A: code review standards are a team process choice (often supported by external tools like static analyzers or pull-request workflows) rather than something the UI framework itself supplies; the framework has no built-in review mechanism. Incorrect - B: the framework does not prescribe or provide an Agile methodology with defined steps - project management approach is an organizational decision entirely independent of which front-end framework is used. Incorrect - D: change history and version control (tracking who changed what code and when) come from tools like Git and Salesforce's source-tracking features in scratch orgs/DX projects, not from the Lightning Component framework itself, which is concerned with rendering and component architecture, not source management.", "id": 40, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 22, "stem": "A developer is designing a new application on the Salesforce platform and wants to ensure it can support multiple tenants effectively. Which design framework should the developer consider to ensure scalability and maintainability?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Model-View-Controller (MVC)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Agile Development"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Waterfall Model"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Flux (view, action, dispatcher, and store)"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Model-View-Controller pattern is the architectural pattern that underlies Salesforce's own UI frameworks (Visualforce's controller/page separation, Aura's Controller/Helper/Renderer split, and LWC's JS-controller/HTML-template/Apex-model separation) - by cleanly separating data (Model), presentation (View), and logic (Controller), MVC makes an application's codebase easier to scale, test, and maintain as complexity grows, which is exactly the documented rationale Salesforce gives for building on it. Incorrect - B and C: Agile and Waterfall are software development lifecycle/project-management methodologies concerned with how a team plans and delivers work over time - they are not architectural or design patterns for how an application's code itself is structured, so neither answers a question about a 'design framework' for scalability and maintainability of the application's architecture. Incorrect - D: Flux (with its view/action/dispatcher/store unidirectional data flow) is a JavaScript application architecture popularized by React/Redux-style front-end applications - it is not a pattern natively used or promoted by Salesforce's own platform UI frameworks, making it an architecture foreign to this platform-native context even though it's a legitimate pattern elsewhere.", "id": 41, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 21, "stem": "What should a developer use to obtain the Id and Name of all the Leads, Accounts, and Contacts that have the company name \"Universal Containers\"?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "SELECT lead(id, name), account(id, name), contact(id, name) FROM Lead, Account, Contact WHERE Name = 'Universal Containers'"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "SELECT Lead.id, Lead.Name, Account.Id, Account.Name, Contact.Id, Contact.Name FROM Lead, Account, Contact WHERE CompanyName = 'Universal Containers'"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "FIND 'Universal Containers' IN CompanyName Fields RETURNING lead(id, name), account(id, name), contact(id, name)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "FIND 'Universal Containers' IN Name Fields RETURNING lead(id, name), account(id, name), contact(id, name)"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The requirement to search across three different, unrelated object types (Lead, Account, Contact) for a matching company/name value is a textbook SOSL use case, per the Apex Developer Guide's guidance that SOSL, not SOQL, is the right tool whenever you need to search text across multiple objects in one operation. The documented 'NAME FIELDS' scope in SOSL specifically targets the fields Salesforce designates as name-type fields on each searched object - which includes Account.Name, Contact.Name, and Lead.Company (Lead's company field is categorized as a Name-type field for SOSL purposes) - letting one FIND statement match 'Universal Containers' across all three objects' relevant name fields and RETURN the Id/Name pairs from each. Incorrect - A and B: SOQL cannot SELECT FROM multiple unrelated object types in a single query the way these options attempt to - a single SOQL statement always targets exactly one primary object (plus optional related-object subqueries/joins along actual relationships), so listing 'Lead, Account, Contact' after FROM as if it were a valid multi-object query is not legal SOQL syntax. Incorrect - C: 'CompanyName Fields' is not a real, documented SOSL field-group scope - the valid scopes are ALL FIELDS, NAME FIELDS, EMAIL FIELDS, and PHONE FIELDS; 'CompanyName' does not exist as one of them, making this syntactically invalid.", "id": 42, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 20, "stem": "If Apex code executes inside the execute() method of an Apex class when implementing the Batchable interface, which statements are true regarding governor limits?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The Apex governor limits cannot be exceeded due to the asynchronous nature of the transaction."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The Apex governor limits will use the asynchronous limit levels."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The Apex governor limits are omitted while calling the constructor of the Apex class."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The Apex governor limits are reset for each iteration of the execute() method."}], "marked": ["B", "D"], "correct": ["B", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per the Apex Developer Guide's documentation on Batch Apex, because a batch job runs asynchronously, each transaction inside it is subject to the higher asynchronous Apex governor limits (for example, the SOQL query row limit and heap size limits documented for asynchronous contexts) rather than the tighter synchronous limits that apply to code running directly in a user's request. Correct - D: the Batchable interface's documented execution model treats each invocation of execute() (once per batch/chunk of scope records) as its own separate transaction with its own fresh set of governor limits - limits consumed in one batch's execute() call do not carry over or accumulate into the next batch's execute() call. Incorrect - A: governor limits absolutely still apply to asynchronous Apex - being asynchronous only changes which specific limit values apply (the higher async ceilings), it does not make the code immune to hitting limits altogether; a poorly written batch execute() method can still throw a LimitException. Incorrect - C: there is no documented carve-out where governor limits are 'omitted' while the Batchable class's constructor runs - any code executed anywhere in the transaction, including a constructor, is still subject to the limits in effect for that context.", "id": 43, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 19, "stem": "When importing and exporting data into Salesforce, which two statements are true?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Data import wizard is an application that is installed on your computer."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Bulk API can be used to bypass the storage limits when importing large data volumes in development environments."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Developer and Developer Pro sandboxes have different storage limits."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Bulk API can be used to import large data volumes in development environments without bypassing the storage limits."}], "marked": ["C", "D"], "correct": ["C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per Salesforce's sandbox documentation, a Developer sandbox has a smaller data/file storage allocation (roughly 200MB) than a Developer Pro sandbox (roughly 1GB), even though both copy only metadata (no production data) when refreshed - so the same import volume that fits comfortably in a Developer Pro sandbox might exhaust a plain Developer sandbox's storage far sooner. Correct - D: the Bulk API is simply a faster, chunked, asynchronous transport mechanism for moving large volumes of records into or out of Salesforce - every record it inserts is still a real row subject to the same storage-limit accounting as any other insert method, so importing through it does not exempt those records from counting against the org's storage ceiling. Incorrect - A: the Data Import Wizard is a point-and-click tool that runs entirely inside the browser from Setup - nothing is installed on the developer's machine; the tool that actually installs as a desktop application is Data Loader, a separate product, so this statement confuses the two. Incorrect - B: no Salesforce API - Bulk, REST, SOAP, or otherwise - bypasses storage limits; all of them write to the same underlying database subject to the same per-org storage ceiling, making the premise of 'bypassing' storage limits false on its face regardless of API choice.", "id": 44, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 18, "stem": "A developer at AW Computing is tasked to create the supporting test class for a programmatic customization that leverages records stored within the custom object, Pricing_Structure__c. AW Computing has a complex pricing structure for each item on its store, spanning more than 500 records. Which two approaches can the developer use to ensure Pricing_Structure__c records are available when the test class is executed?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use without sharing on the class declaration."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the @IsTest(SeeAllData=true) annotation."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use a Test Data Factory class."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Test.loadTest() method."}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["C", "D"], "confidence": "verified-web", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - C: Per Apex testing best practices documented in the Apex Developer Guide, a Test Data Factory class - a dedicated utility class whose static methods build and return the needed Pricing_Structure__c test records with realistic sample data - is the recommended, reusable way to make sure test data exists inside the isolated test transaction, without depending on any data that happens to already be in the org. Correct - D: for a genuinely large or complex data set (over 500 records, as described here), Test.loadTest() lets the test load records from a CSV file bundled as a static resource, giving the developer a way to seed a large, realistic Pricing_Structure__c data set with minimal test-method code rather than generating every record programmatically in a loop. Incorrect - B (one of the screenshot's marked answers): @IsTest(SeeAllData=true) makes the test rely on whatever Pricing_Structure__c records already happen to exist in the org's real data - Salesforce's own documentation calls this an anti-pattern precisely because it makes tests fragile, order-dependent, and liable to fail unexpectedly if the underlying org data changes, which is why it is not considered a correct approach here even though it is technically possible. Incorrect - A (the other marked answer): without sharing changes whether the running class enforces record-level sharing rules for the current user - it has nothing to do with whether Pricing_Structure__c records exist or are visible as test data; sharing and data-availability are entirely different concerns, so this option does not address the requirement at all.", "id": 45, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 17, "stem": "Universal Containers decides to use exclusively declarative development to build out a new Salesforce application. Which three options should be used to build out the database layer for the application?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Custom objects and fields"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Roll-up summaries"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Relationships"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Flows"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Triggers"}], "marked": ["A", "B", "C"], "correct": ["A", "B", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A, B, C: the 'database layer' of a Salesforce application is its schema/data model - custom objects and fields (A) define what data is stored and in what shape, relationships (C, lookup or master-detail) define how those objects connect to one another, and roll-up summary fields (B) declaratively aggregate values from child records onto a master, all configured entirely through Setup's Object Manager with no code, which is exactly the 'exclusively declarative' constraint the stem specifies. Incorrect - D: Flows implement business process/automation logic (what happens in response to events or user actions) - they operate on top of the data model rather than defining the schema itself, so they belong to the business-logic layer, not the database layer being asked about. Incorrect - E: Triggers are Apex code, which directly contradicts the 'exclusively declarative development' constraint in the stem - even setting that aside, triggers, like Flows, implement logic that reacts to data changes rather than defining the underlying schema/database structure itself.", "id": 46, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 16, "stem": "A team of developers is working on a source-driven project that allows them to work independently, with many different org configurations. Which type of Salesforce orgs should they use for their development?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Developer sandboxes"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Scratch orgs"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Developer orgs"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Full Copy sandboxes"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per the Salesforce DX Developer Guide, a scratch org's entire configuration is defined by a scratch-org-definition JSON file and reproduced from source-controlled metadata each time it is created - this makes it inherently 'source-driven' (the org's state comes from files in the repo, not from copying an existing org) and lets each developer spin up their own disposable org, potentially with a different definition file/feature set, entirely independent of what any other developer on the team is working in. Incorrect - A: Developer sandboxes all derive their configuration from copying the one shared production org's metadata - every developer's sandbox reflects that same baseline, which does not provide the 'many different org configurations' flexibility scratch orgs offer via distinct definition files. Incorrect - D: Full Copy sandboxes copy both configuration and all production data, take much longer to refresh, and orgs are typically limited to very few of them at once - they are reserved for staging or performance testing, not as a lightweight, per-developer, disposable workspace. Incorrect - C: standalone Developer Edition orgs are not tied to any source-controlled project definition or reproducible build process - they are just independent, permanent signup orgs, which is the opposite of the disposable, source-driven model the scenario calls for.", "id": 47, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 15, "stem": "A developer needs to prevent the creation of Request__c records when certain conditions exist in the system. A RequestLogic class exists that checks the conditions. What is the correct implementation?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "trigger RequestTrigger on Request__c (before insert) { RequestLogic.validateRecords(trigger.new); }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "trigger RequestTrigger on Request__c (after insert) { if (RequestLogic.isValid(Request__c)) Request.addError('Your request cannot be created at this time.'); }"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "trigger RequestTrigger on Request__c (before insert) { if (RequestLogic.isValid(Request__c)) Request.addError('Your request cannot be created at this time.'); }"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "trigger RequestTrigger on Request__c (after insert) { RequestLogic.validateRecords(trigger.new); }"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Apex Developer Guide's documentation on SObject.addError(), calling addError on a record only prevents that record from being saved when it is invoked during a before-save trigger context; this option correctly declares before insert and passes the actual list of new record instances (trigger.new) into RequestLogic.validateRecords, which is presumably implemented to call addError internally on any record that fails validation - blocking creation exactly as required. Incorrect - B and D: both declare after insert, meaning the Request__c records have already been committed to the database with real Ids by the time this code runs - calling addError in an after-trigger context throws a runtime error rather than preventing the row from being saved, since the save has already happened; neither option can satisfy a 'prevent creation' requirement. Incorrect - C: this option passes `Request__c` - the SObject type name/token itself, not an actual record or a collection of records - into isValid(), which is not valid Apex (you cannot pass a bare sObject type as if it were an instance), and it also calls `Request.addError(...)` on `Request`, which is not a declared record variable in scope (the object's API name is Request__c, and addError must be called on a specific record instance, such as an element of trigger.new) - this code would not compile.", "id": 48, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 13, "stem": "Universal Containers wants to automatically assign new cases to the appropriate support representative based on the case origin. They have created a custom field on the Case for the representative name. What is the best solution to assign the case to the appropriate support representative?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use a formula field on the Case object."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use a trigger on the Case object."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use a validation rule on the Case object."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use an Assignment Flow element."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: An Apex trigger on Case (before insert) can inspect the incoming record's Origin value, run whatever conditional or lookup logic is needed to determine the right representative, and directly write that value onto the custom representative-name field before the record commits - this is a straightforward, supported programmatic assignment pattern for a field-driven routing requirement that isn't natively covered by a simpler declarative feature (standard Case Assignment Rules route to queues/owners based on criteria, not to an arbitrary custom text field the way this scenario needs). Incorrect - A: a formula field is always read-only and recalculated live purely from other field values on the record (or, cross-object, from a single parent's fields) - it cannot be 'assigned' a value by automation and, more fundamentally, it cannot itself perform a lookup against a separate mapping of origins to representatives, so it structurally cannot satisfy an assignment requirement. Incorrect - C: a validation rule can only evaluate a formula against a record's values and either allow the save or block it with an error - it has no mechanism to write or assign a value to any field, so it cannot populate the representative field at all. Incorrect - D: 'Assignment Flow element' is not an actual Salesforce automation tool or a named Flow Builder element - standard Case assignment rules exist as a distinct, separate Setup feature, but they are not referred to by this name, making this option a distractor built from mismatched/fabricated terminology.", "id": 49, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 12, "stem": "What is the result of the following code snippet?", "code": "public void doWork(Account acct) {\n    for (Integer i = 0; i <= 200; i++) {\n        insert acct;\n    }\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "0 Accounts are inserted."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "1 Account is inserted."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "200 Accounts are inserted."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "201 Accounts are inserted."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: the same acct variable (a single Account instance) is passed to `insert` repeatedly inside the loop rather than a new Account being constructed each iteration. Per Apex's DML documentation, the first `insert acct;` call succeeds and assigns acct a database Id; every subsequent `insert acct;` call in the loop then attempts to insert a record that already has an Id, which Apex treats as invalid for an insert operation and throws a DmlException ('Cannot specify Id in an insert call' or a duplicate-Id-related error) on the second iteration. Since this exception is not caught anywhere in the method, it propagates up unhandled and rolls back the entire transaction - including that first, otherwise-successful insert - leaving 0 Accounts actually committed to the database. Incorrect - B (1 Account): this would be the result only if the loop somehow stopped after the first successful insert without attempting a second one, or if the exception on the second insert did not roll back the first - but an unhandled exception rolls back the whole transaction, undoing the first insert too. Incorrect - C (200) and D (201): reaching either of these counts would require 200 or 201 distinct Account records to be successfully inserted, but only one Account object exists in memory throughout the entire loop (acct is never reassigned to a new Account), so there is no way multiple distinct records could ever be created by this code even before the exception occurs.", "id": 50, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 11, "stem": "What is the result of the following code?", "code": "Account a = new Account();\nDatabase.insert(a, false);", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The record will be created and no error will be reported."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The record will not be created and an exception will be thrown."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The record will be created and a message will be in the debug log."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The record will not be created and no error will be reported."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Account has a required Name field, and this code constructs an Account with no fields set at all, so the insert will fail server-side validation. Per the Apex Developer Guide's documentation on Database methods, calling Database.insert(record, false) with the second boolean parameter (allOrNone) explicitly set to false tells the platform to perform a partial-success-style operation and return a Database.SaveResult rather than throwing a DmlException - the failure is captured in that returned SaveResult's isSuccess()/getErrors() rather than as a thrown exception, so nothing is created and no exception propagates unless the developer's own code chooses to inspect the SaveResult and throw one. Incorrect - A: the record cannot be created because Name is required and was never set - this option incorrectly assumes the insert silently succeeds despite the missing required field. Incorrect - B: this describes the behavior of the simple DML statement `insert a;` (or Database.insert(a, true), or Database.insert(a) with the default allOrNone=true) - either of those WOULD throw an unhandled DmlException on this same failure, but that is not what the code shown does, since allOrNone is explicitly false here. Incorrect - C: nothing in Database.insert's documented behavior automatically writes a message to the debug log on failure - the error information is returned programmatically in the SaveResult object for the calling code to handle (or ignore), not auto-logged as a side effect.", "id": 51, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 10, "stem": "The Account object in an organization has a master-detail relationship to a child object called Branch. The following automations exist: Roll-up summary fields; Custom validation rules; Duplicate rules. A developer created a trigger on the Account object. Which two things should the developer consider while testing the trigger code?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The trigger may fire multiple times during a transaction."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Duplicate rules are executed once all DML operations commit to the database."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The validation rules will cause the trigger to fire again."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Rollup summary fields can cause the parent record to go through Save."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: whenever a Branch (child) record is inserted, updated, or deleted, Salesforce recalculates any roll-up summary field defined on the parent Account and writes that new value back onto the Account - that write is itself a save of the Account record, meaning the Account's own trigger runs again as a side effect of a change that happened on a completely different object. Correct - A: because of chains like that roll-up-driven save, plus any workflow/flow field updates and possible recursive DML inside the trigger's own logic, the same trigger can be invoked more than once within a single overall transaction - a well-written trigger must be bulk-safe and idempotent across these repeated invocations, and tests should specifically exercise multi-invocation scenarios rather than assuming the trigger only ever runs once. Incorrect - B: duplicate rules run synchronously as part of the save pipeline, evaluating potential duplicates before the record is committed to the database (during the 'Duplicate Rules' step of the documented order of execution) - not after commit - so describing them as executing 'once all DML operations commit' misstates their actual timing. Incorrect - C: validation rules only evaluate a formula against the record's field values and either allow the save to proceed or block it by throwing an error; they have no mechanism to invoke a trigger again, since a blocked save means no DML operation ever completes in the first place, and only a completed DML operation (not a rejected one) is what fires a trigger.", "id": 52, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 9, "stem": "What are three considerations when using the @InvocableMethod annotation in Apex?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "A method using the @InvocableMethod annotation must define a return value."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "A method using the @InvocableMethod annotation can have multiple input parameters."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Only one method using the @InvocableMethod annotation can be defined per Apex class."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "A method using the @InvocableMethod annotation can be declared as Public or Global."}, {"letter": "E", "text": "A method using the @InvocableMethod annotation must be declared as static."}], "marked": ["C", "D", "E"], "correct": ["C", "D", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - E: per the Apex Developer Guide, an invocable method must be declared static, because Flow Builder and Process Builder invoke it directly against the class itself rather than first constructing an instance of it. Correct - C: the documentation also caps a class at exactly one @InvocableMethod - if a developer needs multiple distinct invocable actions, each one must live in its own separate Apex class. Correct - D: the method's access modifier is flexible and can be either public (usable within the same namespace/org) or global (required if the method needs to be callable from a subscriber org that has installed a managed package containing it). Incorrect - A: a return value is explicitly optional - many invocable methods are declared void because their entire purpose is to perform a side effect, such as a DML update, and they have nothing that needs to be handed back to the calling Flow. Incorrect - B: an invocable method can only accept a single parameter, and that parameter must be a List (of primitives, sObjects, or a custom Apex wrapper class) so Flow can invoke it in bulk across many records at once - the annotation does not support multiple, separately named parameters the way an ordinary method can.", "id": 53, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 8, "stem": "A lead developer creates a virtual class called \"OrderRequest\". Consider the following code snippet. How can a developer use the OrderRequest class within the CustomerOrder class?", "code": "public class CustomerOrder{\n    //code implementation\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "public class CustomerOrder extends OrderRequest"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "@Extends(class=\"OrderRequest\") public class CustomerOrder"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "public class CustomerOrder implements Order"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "@Implements(class=\"OrderRequest\") public class CustomerOrder"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - A: per the Apex Developer Guide's class inheritance documentation, a class inherits from a virtual (or abstract) parent class using the `extends` keyword in its class declaration - `public class CustomerOrder extends OrderRequest` is the one and only correct, compilable syntax for this relationship, after which CustomerOrder can override any virtual methods OrderRequest declares. Incorrect - B (the screenshot's marked answer): @Extends is not a real Apex annotation - Apex has a fixed, documented set of annotations (@isTest, @AuraEnabled, @InvocableMethod, @future, @TestVisible, and a handful of others), and @Extends does not appear anywhere in that list; class inheritance is always expressed with the extends keyword in the class signature, never through an annotation. Incorrect - D: @Implements is likewise not a real Apex annotation, and even setting that aside, 'implements' as a concept is reserved for adopting an interface's method contract, not for inheriting a virtual class's implementation - the two relationships (extends vs implements) are fundamentally different mechanisms in Apex. Incorrect - C: `implements Order` uses the interface-adoption keyword against a type named 'Order,' but the class the developer actually needs to use is the virtual class OrderRequest, not some separate interface called Order - this option targets the wrong type entirely and uses the wrong keyword for inheriting a virtual class.", "id": 54, "bullets": []}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 7, "stem": "Since Aura application events follow the traditional publish-subscribe model, which method is used to fire an event?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "fireEvent()"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "fire()"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "registerEvent()"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "emit()"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: per the Lightning Aura Components Developer Guide, once an event is created with $A.get('e.c:MyEvent') (or a component/application event registered on the component), calling its fire() method is the documented API that actually dispatches the event to any registered handlers/subscribers - this is the standard publish step in Aura's publish-subscribe event model. Incorrect - A: fireEvent() is not a real method on Aura's Event object - it is a plausible-sounding but fabricated name; the actual API is simply fire(). Incorrect - C: registerEvent is a component markup element (<aura:registerEvent>) used to DECLARE that a component can fire a particular component event, as part of setting up the event's definition and access - it is not the runtime call used to actually dispatch/publish an already-instantiated event object. Incorrect - D: emit() is not part of Aura's documented event API at all - it resembles method names from other JavaScript event systems (such as Node's EventEmitter) but is not how events are fired in the Aura framework.", "id": 55, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 6, "stem": "A Salesforce administrator used Flow Builder to create a flow named \"accountOnboarding\". The flow must be used inside an Aura component. Which tag should a developer use to display the flow in the component?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "aura:flow"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "lightning-flow"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "aura-flow"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "lightning:flow"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: per the Lightning Aura Components Developer Guide's base component reference, lightning:flow is the documented Aura base component specifically built to embed and run a Flow interview inside an Aura component - you set its flowName attribute to the API name of the target flow (here, accountOnboarding) and it renders the flow's screens in place. Incorrect - B: lightning-flow (hyphenated, lowercase-with-dash) is the equivalent base component's tag syntax for Lightning Web Components, not Aura - Aura components use the colon-namespaced 'lightning:flow' syntax instead, so using the hyphenated LWC-style tag inside an Aura markup file would not resolve correctly. Incorrect - A and C: 'aura:flow' and 'aura-flow' are not real, documented Aura base components - there is no tag by either of these names in the Aura component reference; they are fabricated distractors built from the aura: namespace prefix but do not correspond to an actual flow-embedding component.", "id": 56, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 5, "stem": "What should a developer use to script the deployment and unit test execution as part of continuous integration?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "VS Code"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Developer Console"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Salesforce CLI"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Execute Anonymous"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: the Salesforce CLI (sf/sfdx) is documented as a scriptable, headless command-line tool - commands to deploy source metadata and run Apex tests (e.g., sf project deploy start, sf apex run test) can be invoked non-interactively from any CI system (Jenkins, GitHub Actions, CircleCI, and so on), which is exactly the scriptable interface a CI pipeline needs. Incorrect - A: VS Code with the Salesforce Extension Pack is an interactive desktop IDE meant for a human developer to write and deploy code by hand; while it uses the Salesforce CLI internally for many of its commands, a CI server does not launch a graphical code editor - it calls the underlying CLI directly. Incorrect - B: the Developer Console is a browser-based, point-and-click tool for a human to manually run tests, inspect logs, and execute anonymous Apex; it has no command-line or scriptable API surface that a CI pipeline could invoke automatically. Incorrect - D: Execute Anonymous is a feature of the Developer Console (or the Tooling API's executeAnonymous endpoint) for running one-off ad hoc Apex snippets - it is not a deployment or test-orchestration mechanism and has no built-in role in a CI pipeline's deploy-and-test workflow.", "id": 57, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 4, "stem": "A developer must provide custom user interfaces when users edit a Contact in either Salesforce Classic or Lightning Experience. What should the developer use to override the Contact's Edit button and provide this functionality?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "A Visualforce page in Salesforce Classic and a Lightning component in Lightning Experience"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "A Lightning page in Salesforce Classic and a Visualforce page in Lightning Experience"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "A Visualforce page in Salesforce Classic and a Lightning page in Lightning Experience"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "A Lightning component in Salesforce Classic and a Lightning component in Lightning Experience"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: per Salesforce's documented standard-button-override capabilities, the two supported override content types for a button like Edit are a Visualforce page (which is fully compatible with and renders in Salesforce Classic) and, as a separate configuration specifically for Lightning Experience, a Lightning (Aura) component that implements the lightning:actionOverride interface - configuring both together lets a Salesforce org present a custom Contact edit interface correctly regardless of which UI the user is in. Incorrect - B and C: Lightning pages (built in App Builder as record/app/home page containers) are not a supported override type for a standard button at all, in either Classic or Lightning Experience - the override configuration screen only accepts a Visualforce page or a Lightning component as content, never a 'Lightning page' as such. Incorrect - D: Lightning (Aura) components are not supported in Salesforce Classic - Classic does not have a Lightning component runtime to render them in, so a Lightning component override only ever takes effect in Lightning Experience, making 'a Lightning component in Salesforce Classic' an impossible configuration.", "id": 58, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 3, "stem": "Managers at Universal Containers want to ensure that only decommissioned containers are able to be deleted in the system. To meet the business requirement a Salesforce developer adds \"Decommissioned\" as a picklist value for the Status__c custom field on the Container__c object. Which two approaches could a developer use to enforce only Container records with a status of \"Decommissioned\" can be deleted?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Apex trigger"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "After record-triggered flow"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Validation rule"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Before record-triggered flow"}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "verified-web", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - A: an Apex trigger declared on the before delete event runs before the deletion is committed and can inspect the incoming record's Status__c value on Trigger.old, then call addError() to block the deletion outright if the status is not 'Decommissioned' - per the Apex Developer Guide, addError only prevents the operation from completing when it is called in a before context, which before delete is. Correct - D: since Spring '22, Salesforce's Flow documentation confirms that a record-triggered flow can be configured to run before a record is deleted, and such a flow can use a Custom Error element (typically inside a fault/decision path checking the status) to block the deletion declaratively - functionally mirroring what the before-delete trigger does, but without code. Incorrect - B (the screenshot's marked answer): an after-delete flow, by definition, only runs once the deletion has already been committed to the database - at that point there is nothing left to block, so an after-delete flow structurally cannot prevent a deletion from happening, no matter what logic it contains; this makes it the wrong choice for an enforcement requirement like this one. Incorrect - C: per Salesforce's documented validation rule behavior, validation rules only evaluate and fire during insert and update (save) operations - they are never evaluated during a delete operation at all, so a validation rule has no way to block a delete regardless of its formula logic.", "id": 59, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 2, "stem": "A developer must write an Apex method that will be called from a Lightning component. The method may delete an Account stored in the accountRec variable. Which method should a developer use to ensure only users that should be able to delete Accounts can successfully perform deletions?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Schema.sObjectType.Account.isDeletable()"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Account.isDeletable()"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "accountRec.sObjectType.isDeletable()"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "accountRec.isDeletable()"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: per the Apex Developer Guide's documentation on CRUD/object-permission checks, Schema.sObjectType.Account.isDeletable() calls the describe-result method that reports whether the running user's profile/permission sets grant Delete access on the Account object as a whole - this is the documented, correct syntax for programmatically enforcing object-level delete permission before performing a DML delete from Apex. Incorrect - B: 'Account.isDeletable()' is not valid Apex - isDeletable() is a method on a DescribeSObjectResult (obtained via the sObjectType describe chain), not something you can call directly on the sObject type token itself without going through Schema.sObjectType first. Incorrect - D: 'accountRec.isDeletable()' also does not compile - isDeletable() is not an instance method available on a concrete sObject record variable; CRUD-permission checks are describe-level operations tied to the object's Schema metadata, not to any particular record instance. Incorrect - C: 'accountRec.sObjectType' is likewise not valid syntax for reaching the describe information in this way - the correct, documented entry point is the static Schema.sObjectType.Account reference (or accountRec.getSObjectType().getDescribe() as an alternative valid pattern), not chaining '.sObjectType' directly off a record variable followed by isDeletable().", "id": 60, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 1, "stem": "A custom picklist field, Food_Preference__c, exists on a custom object. A developer must ensure a value is populated every time a record is created or updated. What is the optimal way to ensure a value is selected every time a record is saved?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Write an Apex trigger to ensure a value is selected."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Set \"Use the first value in the list as the default value\" to True."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Mark the field as Required on the field definition."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Mark the field as Required on the object's page layout."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: per Salesforce Help's documentation on field properties, marking a field Required at the field-definition level (in Object Manager) enforces that requirement universally - across every page layout the field appears on, every API-based insert/update (Data Loader, integrations, Apex DML), and every other entry point into the record - because it is a schema-level constraint rather than a UI-only setting. Incorrect - D: marking a field Required only on a specific page layout enforces that requirement solely for users editing the record through that particular layout in the UI; any insert or update performed via the API, a different layout, or Apex code entirely bypasses that layout-level requirement, so a value could still be saved as blank. Incorrect - B: setting a default value (even 'use the first value in the list') only pre-populates the field when a new record form is first opened - it does not stop a user or integration from clearing that value before saving, and it does nothing at all for updates to an existing record where the field might later be blanked out, so it cannot guarantee the field is always populated 'every time a record is created or updated.' Incorrect - A: an Apex trigger checking for a blank value and calling addError could technically enforce this, but it is unnecessary custom code (with its own testing and maintenance burden) for a requirement the platform's native, declarative field-level Required setting already solves completely and more robustly - it is not the 'optimal' (simplest, most maintainable) solution when the built-in option exists.", "id": 61, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 65, "stem": "Which two are best practices when it comes to Aura component and application event handling?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Try to use application events as opposed to component events."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use component events to communicate actions that should be handled at the application level."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Reuse the event logic in a component bundle, by putting the logic in the helper."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Handle low-level events in the event handler and re-fire them as higher-level events."}], "marked": ["C", "D"], "correct": ["C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: the Lightning Aura Components Developer Guide's best-practices documentation recommends placing reusable event-handling logic in a component bundle's Helper resource rather than duplicating it across multiple Controller handler functions, since any handler in the bundle can call into the Helper, improving maintainability and making the logic independently testable. Correct - D: that same guidance recommends catching low-level, generic events (such as a raw click or change event) in a component's handler and translating/re-firing them as a higher-level, semantically meaningful custom event (like 'itemSelected') - this way, consumers of the component only need to reason about business-meaningful events rather than low-level implementation details. Incorrect - A: this inverts Salesforce's actual documented guidance - application events broadcast to every listening component regardless of relevance and are explicitly recommended to be used sparingly, precisely because they are harder to reason about and scale less well than component events, which follow a defined bubbling path from child to parent. Incorrect - B: component events are documented for parent-child communication within a specific containment hierarchy, not for broadcasting concerns that should be 'handled at the application level' - that description is actually the correct use case for an application event, so this option mismatches the event type to the scenario it describes.", "id": 62, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 64, "stem": "A custom Visualforce controller calls the ApexPages.addMessage() method, but no messages are rendering on the page. Which component should be added to the Visualforce page to display the message?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "<apex:pageMessages />"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "<apex:message for=\"info\"/>"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "<apex:pageMessage severity=\"info\" />"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "<apex:facet name=\"messages\" />"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: per the Visualforce Component Reference, <apex:pageMessages /> is specifically documented to render every message currently queued on the page, including any added programmatically via ApexPages.addMessage() in the controller - it requires no binding to a specific field or component, making it the correct, general-purpose component for surfacing controller-added messages. Incorrect - B: <apex:message for=\"...\"> is documented to render validation/error messages associated with one specific input component identified by its 'for' id - it does not display arbitrary messages added generically via ApexPages.addMessage() unless that message happens to be explicitly associated with the referenced component. Incorrect - C: <apex:pageMessage> (singular) is documented as a way to manually render one specific, hard-coded message you define directly on the tag (via its 'summary'/'severity' attributes) - it is not wired to automatically display messages that already exist in the ApexPages message collection from addMessage(). Incorrect - D: <apex:facet name=\"messages\"> defines a named facet placeholder used inside certain data-bound components like <apex:pageBlock> (for header/footer content) - it has no relationship to Apex's page-message system at all and would not render controller messages.", "id": 63, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "PD1-1-24", "number": 63, "stem": "A developer is creating a page that allows users to create multiple Opportunities. The developer is asked to verify the current user's default Opportunity record type, and set certain default values based on the record type before inserting the record. How can the developer find the current user's default record type?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the Schema.userInfo.Opportunity.getDefaultRecordType() method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Query the Profile where the ID equals userInfo.getProfileID() and then use the profile.Opportunity.getDefaultRecordType() method."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create the opportunity and check the opportunity.recordType, which will have the record ID of the current user's default record type, before inserting."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use Opportunity.sObjectType.getDescribe().getRecordTypeInfos() to get a list of record types, and iterate through them until isDefaultRecordTypeMapping() is true."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: per the Apex Developer Guide's Schema describe documentation, calling Opportunity.sObjectType.getDescribe().getRecordTypeInfos() returns a list of RecordTypeInfo objects representing every record type available to the running user for that object, and each RecordTypeInfo exposes an isDefaultRecordTypeMapping() method that returns true for the one record type the current user's profile is mapped to as their default - iterating this list to find that flagged entry is the documented, correct way to determine the running user's default record type. Incorrect - A: 'Schema.userInfo.Opportunity.getDefaultRecordType()' is not real Apex syntax - there is no 'userInfo' member on the Schema namespace structured this way, and no such method exists; this option is a fabricated API path. Incorrect - B: while Profile does have a DefaultOpportunityRecordType-style relationship queryable via more involved SOQL against RecordTypeInfo-adjacent objects in newer API contexts, there is no simple 'profile.Opportunity.getDefaultRecordType()' method - dot-referencing an object name off of a queried Profile record like this is not valid Apex, making this option syntactically invalid regardless of the underlying idea. Incorrect - C: a brand-new Opportunity() constructed in memory does not automatically populate its recordType field with the current user's default record type Id - Apex does not perform this kind of implicit lookup on object construction, so inspecting an uninitialized opportunity.recordType would not yield the needed information at all.", "id": 64, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 9, "stem": "A developer has the following requirements: Calculate the total amount on an Order; Calculate the line amount for each Line Item based on quantity selected and price; Move Line Items to a different Order if a Line Item is not in stock. Which relationship implementation supports these requirements on its own?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Order has a re-parentable master-detail field to Line Item."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Line Item has a re-parentable master-detail field to Order."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Order has a re-parentable lookup field to Line Item."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Line Item has a re-parentable lookup field to Order."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Line Item is logically the child of Order (many Line Items belong to one Order), so the master-detail field must live on Line Item and point to Order. A master-detail relationship is the only relationship type that supports roll-up summary fields, which is what lets the parent Order automatically total the line amounts declaratively without Apex. Per Salesforce Help on master-detail relationships, enabling the 'Allow reparenting' setting on that same master-detail field is what then permits a Line Item's parent Order to be changed programmatically or via the UI when a line item is out of stock - without that flag, master-detail children are locked to their original parent. Incorrect - A: A master-detail field must be defined on the child object referencing the parent; placing it on Order (the parent) referencing Line Item (the child) is backwards and is not how master-detail relationships are declared in the schema, so this configuration is not valid for this requirement. Incorrect - C and D: A lookup relationship, from either direction, does not support roll-up summary fields at all, so it cannot satisfy the 'calculate the total amount on an Order' requirement on its own - roll-up summaries are exclusively a master-detail feature.", "id": 65, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 8, "stem": "The following code snippet is executed by a Lightning web component in an environment with more than 2,000 lead records. Which governor limit will likely be exceeded within the Apex transaction?", "code": "@AuraEnabled\npublic static void updateLeads() {\n    for (Lead thisLead : [SELECT Origin__c FROM Lead]) {\n        thisLead.LeadSource = thisLead.Origin__c;\n        update thisLead;\n    }\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Total number of records processed as a result of DML statements"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Total number of SOQL queries issued"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Total number of records retrieved by SOQL queries"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Total number of DML statements issued"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The `update thisLead;` statement sits inside the for-loop body, so it executes once per Lead returned by the query rather than once for the whole set. Per the Apex governor limits documented in the Apex Developer Guide, a synchronous Apex transaction is capped at 150 total DML statements; with 2,000+ Leads, the code issues 2,000+ individual DML statements and blows past that 150 ceiling almost immediately - specifically it is the count of DML statements (not the number of rows they touch) that is exhausted here first. Incorrect - A: 'Total number of records processed as a result of DML statements' is a separate, much higher limit (10,000 rows per transaction); since this loop fails on the 150-statement ceiling long before it could process 10,000 rows, that limit is never the one actually hit. Incorrect - B: Only a single SOQL query is issued (the one in the for-loop's collection expression, which runs once, not once per row) - Apex for-loop-over-a-query syntax like `for (Lead l : [SELECT ...])` performs one query and streams the results in batches, so the 100-SOQL-queries limit is nowhere close to being exceeded. Incorrect - C: The query returns 2,000+ rows, which is well under the 50,000-row SOQL query limit, so that limit is not the bottleneck; the actual failure happens on the DML statement count long before row-retrieval limits would ever matter.", "id": 66, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 7, "stem": "A developer must troubleshoot to pinpoint the causes of performance issues when a custom page loads in their org. Which tool should the developer use to troubleshoot query performance?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Visual Studio Code IDE"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Setup Menu"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "AppExchange"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Developer Console"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Developer Console is Salesforce's built-in, browser-based diagnostic tool and includes the Query Plan feature specifically documented for analyzing SOQL query performance (showing the cost, cardinality, and index usage of a query), plus a full execution log viewer/Analysis tab that lets a developer see exactly how long each SOQL query and DML operation took during a page load - this combination makes it the purpose-built tool for this troubleshooting task. Incorrect - A: VS Code with the Salesforce Extension Pack is excellent for writing and deploying Apex/LWC code and can view logs, but it does not have a built-in Query Plan analyzer equivalent to the Developer Console's, so it is not the primary tool Salesforce documentation points to for query performance diagnosis. Incorrect - B: The Setup Menu is where you configure org features, permissions, and metadata; it has no query-plan or execution-log analysis capability of its own. Incorrect - C: AppExchange is Salesforce's marketplace for installing third-party and Salesforce-built apps/packages; it is not a diagnostic tool at all and has no relationship to query performance troubleshooting.", "id": 67, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 6, "stem": "Consider the following code snippet. Given the multi-tenant architecture of the Salesforce platform, what is a best practice a developer should implement to ensure successful execution of the method?", "code": "public static List<Lead> obtainAllFields(Set<Id> leadIds) {\n    List<Lead> result = new List<Lead>();\n    for (Id leadId : leadIds) {\n        result.add([SELECT FIELDS(STANDARD) FROM Lead WHERE Id = :leadId]);\n    }\n    return result;\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Avoid executing queries without a limit clause."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Avoid performing queries inside for loops."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Avoid returning an empty List of records."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Avoid using variables as query filters."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: The SOQL query is placed inside the for-loop, so it runs once per element of leadIds - with more than 100 IDs in the set, this issues more than 100 separate SOQL queries in the transaction and throws 'System.LimitException: Too many SOQL queries: 101', a governor limit that exists precisely because Salesforce's multi-tenant architecture must protect shared database resources from any single org's runaway code. The documented best-practice fix (from the Apex Developer Guide's bulkification guidance) is to move the query outside the loop entirely and use a single query with a WHERE Id IN :leadIds bind-variable clause, retrieving all needed Leads in one round trip. Incorrect - A: A missing LIMIT clause is not the problem here - a query with WHERE Id = :leadId naturally returns at most one row per execution, so row-count limits are not what fails; the failure is purely about the number of separate query invocations. Incorrect - C: Returning an empty list is a legitimate, harmless outcome (for example, if leadIds is empty) and has no bearing on multi-tenant governor limits or execution success. Incorrect - D: Using a variable (leadId) as a bind variable in a WHERE clause is actually the recommended, safe way to filter a query (it also protects against SOQL injection) - the problem is not that a variable is used as a filter, but that the query executes repeatedly inside the loop.", "id": 68, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 49, "stem": "Which two characteristics are true for Lightning Web Component custom events?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "By default a custom event only propagates to its immediate container and to its immediate child component."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Data may be passed in the payload of a custom event using @wire decorated properties."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Data may be passed in the payload of a custom event using a property called detail."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "By default a custom event only propagates to it's immediate container."}], "marked": ["C", "D"], "correct": ["C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide, when you construct a CustomEvent you pass an options object whose `detail` property carries the event's payload - for example `new CustomEvent('mycustomevent', { detail: dataToPass })` - and listeners read that payload via `event.detail`. This is the documented, and only, mechanism for attaching data to a custom event. Correct - D: LWC custom events default to `bubbles: false` and `composed: false`, meaning that unless a component explicitly opts in with those flags set to true, the event is dispatched only to the component's own immediate container/parent listener and does not bubble further up the DOM or cross shadow boundaries. Incorrect - A: This overstates default propagation by adding 'and to its immediate child component' - events flow upward from the component that fires them to a listening ancestor, never automatically downward to a child, so no default LWC event propagates to a child. Incorrect - B: The @wire decorator is used to provision (read) data reactively from Apex methods or the UI API into a component's properties - it has nothing to do with attaching a payload to a custom event, which is exclusively done through the `detail` property described in C.", "id": 69, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 48, "stem": "A credit card company needs to implement the functionality for a service agent to process damaged or stolen credit cards. When the customers call in, the service agent must gather many pieces of information. A developer is tasked to implement this functionality. What should the developer use to satisfy this requirement in the most efficient manner?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lightning Component"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Apex trigger"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Approval process"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Screen-based flow"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: A screen flow, built declaratively in Flow Builder, is Salesforce's purpose-built tool for guiding a user (here, the service agent) through a multi-step, multi-field data-collection process with branching logic, input validation, and a polished guided UI - all without writing code, which is exactly what 'gather many pieces of information' during a live call describes, and it is the most efficient (lowest-effort, fastest-to-build and maintain) option of the four. Incorrect - A: A custom Lightning component (Aura or LWC) could technically be built to collect this information, but it would require writing markup, JavaScript, and possibly Apex from scratch to replicate what a screen flow already provides out of the box, making it a far less efficient choice for a purely declarative data-gathering wizard. Incorrect - B: An Apex trigger only executes in response to a DML operation on a record (insert/update/delete) - it has no user interface and cannot 'gather' information interactively from an agent on a live call, so it is not applicable to this UI-driven requirement at all. Incorrect - C: An approval process automates routing a record through a sequence of approval steps (e.g., manager sign-off); it is not designed for open-ended, guided data collection from a user and has no free-form multi-screen input capability like a screen flow does.", "id": 70, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 47, "stem": "A developer needs to prevent the creation of Request__c records when certain conditions exist in the system. A RequestLogic class exists that checks the conditions. What is the correct implementation?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "trigger RequestTrigger on Request__c (after insert) { RequestLogic.validateRecords(trigger.new); }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "trigger RequestTrigger on Request__c (before insert) { if (RequestLogic.isValid(Request__c)) Request.addError('Your request cannot be created at this time.'); }"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "trigger RequestTrigger on Request__c (after insert) { if (RequestLogic.isValid(Request__c)) Request.addError('Your request cannot be created at this time.'); }"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "trigger RequestTrigger on Request__c (before insert) { RequestLogic.validateRecords(trigger.new); }"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Apex Developer Guide's trigger order-of-execution documentation, calling SObject.addError() on a record only prevents that record from being committed to the database when it is called during a before trigger; calling it after the DML has already completed (an after trigger context) has no effect on whether the record was created, since the insert has already happened. This option correctly runs in a before insert context and hands trigger.new (the actual list of new record instances) to RequestLogic.validateRecords, which is presumably implemented to call addError internally on the offending records. Incorrect - A and C: Both declare the trigger as after insert, meaning by the time RequestLogic runs, the Request__c records already exist in the database with committed IDs - calling addError at this point throws a runtime error rather than blocking creation, so neither can satisfy a 'prevent creation' requirement regardless of what logic runs inside. Incorrect - B: This option passes `Request__c` itself - the SObject Type token/class name - into isValid(), not an actual record or collection of records, which does not compile as valid Apex; it also calls `Request.addError(...)` on `Request`, which is not a declared variable in scope (the SObject type is Request__c, and addError is an instance method that must be called on a specific record, such as an element of trigger.new, not on a bare type name), making this a syntactically invalid implementation.", "id": 71, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 46, "stem": "A company decides to implement a new process where every time an Opportunity is created, a follow up Task should be created and assigned to the Opportunity Owner. What is the most efficient way for a developer to implement this?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Task actions"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Record-triggered flow on Opportunity"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Apex trigger on Task"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Auto-launched flow on Task"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: A record-triggered flow configured to run on Opportunity when 'A record is created' can, entirely declaratively, use a Create Records element to insert a new Task with its OwnerId set to the triggering Opportunity's OwnerId - this is the documented Flow Builder pattern for 'when X happens, automatically create related record Y' and requires no Apex, making it the most efficient (fastest to build, easiest to maintain) implementation of the four options. Incorrect - A: 'Task actions' is not a Salesforce automation trigger point; quick actions on Task let a user manually log a call or create a follow-up from the Task itself, but nothing about a Task action fires automatically when an Opportunity is created, so it cannot satisfy an automatic, event-driven requirement. Incorrect - C: An Apex trigger on Task would only execute in response to DML performed against Task records; since the triggering event here is the creation of an Opportunity, not a Task, a Task-based trigger is never invoked by this business process at all - the trigger would need to be on Opportunity, and even then Apex is more effort than the declarative flow. Incorrect - D: An auto-launched flow on Task suffers the same fundamental problem as C - an auto-launched flow must be invoked by something (Apex, another flow, or a Process Builder/flow-based trigger); being 'on Task' does not give it a listening/triggering context tied to Opportunity creation, so it would never run when an Opportunity is created unless additional automation on Opportunity explicitly invoked it, which is more complex and less direct than simply making the flow trigger on Opportunity itself.", "id": 72, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 45, "stem": "Given the following Apex statement: Account myAccount = [SELECT Id, Name FROM Account]; What occurs when more than one Account is returned by the SOQL query?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The query fails and an error is written to the debug log."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The variable, myAccount, is automatically cast to the List data type."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "An unhandled exception is thrown and the code terminates."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The first Account returned is assigned to myAccount."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: In Apex, assigning the result of an inline SOQL query directly to a single sObject variable (rather than a List<Account>) is valid syntax only when the query returns exactly zero or one row; per Apex's documented behavior for this pattern, if the query returns more than one row at runtime, the platform throws a System.QueryException with the message 'List has more than 1 row for assignment to SObject' - this is a genuine runtime exception, and if the surrounding code has no try/catch around it, execution of the transaction halts at that point (an unhandled exception). Incorrect - A: This is not a compile-time or query-syntax failure that merely logs an error and continues - the query itself executes successfully and returns rows; the failure is specifically in the assignment step, and it is a thrown runtime exception, not a silently logged error. Incorrect - B: Apex does not perform implicit type-widening or auto-casting of a query result into a List merely because multiple rows came back; the variable's declared type (Account, a single sObject) is fixed at compile time, and a multi-row result cannot be shoehorned into it - hence the exception rather than a cast. Incorrect - D: Apex has no 'take the first row' fallback behavior for this assignment pattern (unlike, for example, LIMIT 1 explicitly added to a query, which would guarantee a single row); silently picking the first result would hide bugs, so Salesforce deliberately throws an exception instead.", "id": 73, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 44, "stem": "A developer wants to mark each Account in a List<Account> as either Active or Inactive, based on the value in the LastModifiedDate field of each Account being greater than 90 days in the past. Which Apex technique should the developer use?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "An if-else statement, with a for loop inside"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "A switch statement, with a for loop inside"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "A for loop, with a switch statement inside"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "A for loop, with an if or if/else statement inside"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The requirement is to examine every Account in the list individually, so the outer control structure must be a for loop that iterates the collection; for each Account, the decision of Active versus Inactive depends on a computed boolean condition (whether LastModifiedDate is more than 90 days old, e.g. `acct.LastModifiedDate < System.now().addDays(-90)`), which is exactly the kind of open-ended comparison an if or if/else statement is designed to evaluate. Incorrect - A: Putting the for loop inside the if-else (rather than the reverse) would mean the branch decision is made once, before ever looking at individual Accounts, so it could not evaluate each Account's own LastModifiedDate separately - the iteration must be the outer structure, not the inner one. Incorrect - B and C: A switch statement (Apex's `switch on`) is designed to match a single expression against a fixed, finite set of discrete literal values or types (like specific strings, enums, or sObject types) - it has no built-in way to express an open-ended date/time range comparison such as 'more than 90 days ago,' so it cannot implement this condition regardless of whether it wraps or is wrapped by the loop.", "id": 74, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 43, "stem": "The OrderHelper class is a utility class that contains business logic for processing orders (public class without sharing OrderHelper). A developer needs to create a constant named DELIVERY_MULTIPLIER with a value of 4.15. The value of the constant should not change at any time in the code. How should the developer declare the DELIVERY_MULTIPLIER constant to meet the business objectives?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "constant decimal DELIVERY_MULTIPLIER = 4.15;"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "static decimal DELIVERY_MULTIPLIER = 4.15;"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "decimal DELIVERY_MULTIPLIER = 4.15;"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "static final decimal DELIVERY_MULTIPLIER = 4.15;"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Apex Developer Guide's variable declaration rules, Apex has no dedicated 'constant' keyword; a true constant is expressed by combining `static` (the variable belongs to the class itself rather than to each instance, and is shared across the class) with `final` (the variable can be assigned a value exactly once - here, at declaration - and any subsequent attempt to reassign it is a compile-time error). Together, `static final Decimal DELIVERY_MULTIPLIER = 4.15;` guarantees the value can never change anywhere in the code, satisfying the stated business objective. Incorrect - A: Apex simply does not recognize `constant` as a language keyword; code written this way will not compile. Incorrect - B: `static` alone only controls whether the variable is class-level versus instance-level (shared across all uses of the class) - without `final`, nothing stops later code in the class from reassigning DELIVERY_MULTIPLIER to a different value, so immutability is not guaranteed. Incorrect - C: A plain instance member declared with neither `static` nor `final` is both mutable and tied to individual instances of OrderHelper (a new copy per object), which is the opposite of a single, unchangeable, shared constant.", "id": 75, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 42, "stem": "Universal Containers recently transitioned from Classic to Lightning Experience. One of its business processes requires certain values from the Opportunity object (Name, Amount, Account) to be sent via an HTTP REST callout to its external order management system when the user presses a custom button on the Opportunity detail page. Which two methods should the developer implement to fulfill the business requirement?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Create an after update trigger on the Opportunity object that calls a helper method using @Future(Callout=true) to perform the HTTP REST callout."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Create a Remote Action on the Opportunity object that executes an Apex immediate action to perform the HTTP REST callout whenever the Opportunity is updated."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a Lightning component quick action that performs the HTTP REST callout, and use a Lightning Action to expose the component on the Opportunity detail page."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Create a custom Visualforce quick action that performs the HTTP REST callout, and use a Visualforce quick action to expose the component on the Opportunity detail page."}], "marked": ["C", "D"], "correct": ["C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C and D: The stated trigger for the callout is explicitly 'when the user presses a custom button on the Opportunity detail page' - a user-initiated, on-demand action, not a data-change event. Salesforce's supported way to put a custom button on a Lightning Experience record page that runs custom logic on click is a quick action: either a Lightning Web/Aura Component quick action wired up via a Lightning Action (C), or a Visualforce-based quick action (D, still supported for this purpose in Lightning Experience). Both let the developer's code perform the HTTP callout only when the button is actually clicked, matching the requirement exactly. Incorrect - A: An after update trigger fires automatically whenever any Opportunity update occurs, regardless of whether the user pressed the specific custom button - this would send the callout on every ordinary field edit to the Opportunity, not just the intended button press, misrepresenting the actual business trigger. Incorrect - B: 'Remote Action' refers to JavaScript Remoting used from Visualforce pages/buttons in Classic, and this option additionally describes it as firing 'whenever the Opportunity is updated,' which - like A - ties the callout to a record update event rather than a deliberate button press, so it does not match the stated user-driven requirement even setting aside that Remote Actions are a Classic-era construct being phased out in favor of quick actions in Lightning Experience.", "id": 76, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 41, "stem": "The sales management team at Universal Containers requires that the Lead Source field of the Lead record be populated when a Lead is converted. What should be done to ensure that a user populates the Lead Source field prior to converting a Lead?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Create an after trigger on Lead."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use a validation rule."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use Lead Conversion field mapping."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use a formula field."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: A validation rule on Lead that fires an error when `ISBLANK(LeadSource)` (or equivalent) is true will run as part of the standard save/convert pipeline and prevent the Lead conversion from completing until the user actually populates Lead Source, which directly satisfies 'ensure that a user populates the field prior to converting.' Incorrect - A: An after trigger runs only once the record has already been saved/converted, so by the time an after-trigger's logic executes it is too late to force the user to fill in the field beforehand - it could react to a missing value (e.g., by logging it) but cannot block the conversion from having already happened. Incorrect - C: Lead Conversion field mapping controls which Lead field values get copied into which fields on the newly created Account/Contact/Opportunity during conversion; it has no validation or enforcement capability and does not require any field to be populated before conversion proceeds. Incorrect - D: A formula field is read-only and calculated automatically from other fields - a user cannot manually populate it, and it cannot itself block a Lead conversion, so it cannot be used to enforce that a person enters a Lead Source value.", "id": 77, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 40, "stem": "A developer creates a custom exception as shown below: public class ParityException extends Exception {}. What are two ways the developer can fire the exception in Apex?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "throw new ParityException('parity does not match');"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "new ParityException('parity does not match');"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "new ParityException();"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "throw new ParityException();"}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A and D: Per the Apex Developer Guide's exception-handling chapter, an exception object must be raised with the `throw` statement - simply constructing it does nothing until `throw` hands it to the runtime, which then unwinds the call stack looking for a matching catch block (or terminates the transaction if none exists). Because ParityException extends the built-in Exception class, it automatically inherits multiple constructors, including a no-argument constructor and one that takes a String message, so both `throw new ParityException('parity does not match');` (with a custom message) and `throw new ParityException();` (with no message) are valid ways to fire it. Incorrect - B and C: Both of these merely instantiate a new ParityException object with `new` and then discard it - without the `throw` keyword, the object is just an ordinary object sitting in memory; it is never raised, no stack unwinding occurs, and no catch block anywhere will ever see it, so these lines have no effect on program flow at all.", "id": 78, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 39, "stem": "What should a developer do to check the code coverage of a class after running all tests?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "View the code coverage percentage for the class using the Overall Code Coverage panel in the Developer Console Tests tab."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "View the Class Test Percentage tab on the Apex Class list view in Salesforce Setup."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "View the Code Coverage column in the list view on the Apex Classes page."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Select and run the class on the Apex Test Execution page in the Developer Console."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: After tests are executed in the Developer Console, the Tests tab displays an 'Overall Code Coverage' panel that lists every class along with the percentage of its lines exercised by the tests that were run, which is exactly the documented way to inspect per-class coverage results following a test run. Incorrect - B: There is no 'Class Test Percentage' tab anywhere in Setup's Apex Classes area - this is a fabricated UI element that does not exist in Salesforce. Incorrect - C: The standard Apex Classes list view in Setup does not include a live 'Code Coverage' column that reflects results of tests you just ran; code coverage reporting for a specific run is surfaced through the Developer Console (or the Tooling API/ApexCodeCoverage object), not through that static Setup list view. Incorrect - D: The Apex Test Execution page is where you select and run tests (or re-run them) - it is the action of running tests, not the report of the resulting code coverage; you still have to go to the Tests tab's Overall Code Coverage panel (or similar) to actually see the coverage numbers.", "id": 79, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 38, "stem": "What are two ways a developer can get the status of an enqueued job for a class that implements the queueable interface?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Query the AsyncApexJob object"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "View the Apex Jobs page"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "View the Apex Flex Queue"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "View the Apex Status page"}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Apex Developer Guide's asynchronous Apex documentation, every batch, queueable, scheduled, and future job is tracked as a row in the AsyncApexJob system object, exposing fields like Status, NumberOfErrors, JobItemsProcessed, and TotalJobItems - querying this object with SOQL (optionally filtered on the Id returned by System.enqueueJob()) is the documented, programmatic way to check a Queueable job's status. Correct - B: The Apex Jobs page in Setup (Setup > Apex Jobs, or Apex Jobs under Environment > Jobs) is the point-and-click UI view of the same AsyncApexJob records, letting an admin or developer see queued, processing, completed, or failed jobs including Queueable ones without writing SOQL. Incorrect - C: The Apex Flex Queue specifically holds Batch Apex jobs that are queued and waiting to run (allowing up to 100 holding jobs and reordering), not Queueable jobs - Queueable jobs go straight into execution once resources are available and are not managed through the Flex Queue. Incorrect - D: There is no 'Apex Status page' in Salesforce; this is not a real feature, and the actual job status information lives in AsyncApexJob / the Apex Jobs page as described above.", "id": 80, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 37, "stem": "A developer is alerted to an issue with a custom Apex trigger that is causing records to be duplicated. What is the most appropriate debugging approach to troubleshoot the issue?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Review the Historical Event logs to identify the source of the issue."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add system.debug statements to the code to track the execution flow and identify the issue."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Disable the trigger in production and test to see if the issue still occurs."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Apex Interactive Debugger to step through the code and identify the issue."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "verified-web", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Apex Interactive Debugger (accessed via the Developer Console's checkpoints or VS Code's Apex Replay Debugger) lets a developer set checkpoints/breakpoints inside the trigger, execute it against a real scenario, and step line by line while inspecting variable and collection state at each point - this directly reveals where duplicate records are being created (for example, a second insert being issued inside a loop) far more precisely than any of the other options. Incorrect - A: 'Historical Event logs' is not a Salesforce feature for diagnosing Apex code behavior; even where org-level Setup Audit Trail or Field History Tracking exist, they log configuration/field changes, not the runtime execution path of a trigger. Incorrect - C: Disabling a trigger in a live production org to 'test' is both risky (it changes production behavior for all users while diagnosing) and inconclusive on its own - at best it confirms the trigger is involved, but tells you nothing about where inside the trigger the duplication logic is going wrong, so it isn't a debugging approach in the sense the question is asking about. Incorrect - B: Adding System.debug statements can help narrow down a problem over several iterations of deploy-test-check logs, but it is a slower, more indirect technique compared to interactively stepping through the actual execution with the debugger, which is why the purpose-built debugger is the more appropriate first choice here.", "id": 81, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 36, "stem": "A developer has a single custom controller class that works with a Visualforce Wizard to support creating and editing multiple sObjects. The wizard accepts data from user inputs across multiple Visualforce pages and from a parameter on the initial URL. Which three statements are useful inside the unit test to effectively test the custom controller?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "public ExtendedController(ApexPages.StandardController cntrl) { }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "ApexPages.currentPage().getParameters().put('input', 'TestValue');"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "insert pageRef;"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "String nextPage = controller.save().getUrl();"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Test.setCurrentPage(pageRef);"}], "marked": ["B", "D", "E"], "correct": ["B", "D", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - E: Per the Visualforce/Apex testing documentation, Test.setCurrentPage(pageRef) establishes which PageReference the test is simulating as the 'current page,' which is a prerequisite for ApexPages.currentPage() to return a meaningful object inside the test - without it, code relying on the current page context in the controller cannot be exercised correctly. Correct - B: Because the wizard reads a parameter from the initial page URL, the test must simulate that URL parameter; ApexPages.currentPage().getParameters().put('input', 'TestValue') injects a key/value pair into the current page's parameter map exactly as if it had been passed on the query string, letting the test drive the controller's URL-parameter-dependent logic. Correct - D: Calling the controller's own save() (or next()/save-equivalent) method and capturing the PageReference it returns via getUrl() is the standard pattern for verifying the wizard's navigation logic - i.e., confirming the controller redirects to the page the business logic says it should after each step. Incorrect - A: This is merely a constructor signature/method stub, not an executable statement that does anything inside a test method - it shows how the controller *could* be declared, but writing it in a test body accomplishes nothing on its own. Incorrect - C: 'insert pageRef;' is invalid Apex - PageReference is a Visualforce navigation object, not an sObject, and cannot be passed to the DML `insert` statement; this option combines two unrelated concepts into a line that will not compile.", "id": 82, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 35, "stem": "Universal Containers has a large number of custom applications that were built using a third-party JavaScript framework and exposed using Visualforce pages. The company wants to update these applications to apply styling that resembles the look and feel of Lightning Experience. What should the developer do to fulfill the business request in the quickest and most effective manner?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Enable Available for Lightning Experience, Lightning Communities, and the mobile app on Visualforce pages used by the custom application."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Rewrite all Visualforce pages as Lightning components."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Set the attribute enableLightning to true in the definition."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Incorporate the Salesforce Lightning Design System CSS stylesheet into the JavaScript applications."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Salesforce Lightning Design System (SLDS) is Salesforce's own published CSS framework (available as a static resource or via CDN reference) that provides the exact classes, colors, spacing, and components needed to visually match Lightning Experience; because the existing pages already work functionally (they just need restyling), simply linking the SLDS stylesheet into the existing Visualforce/JavaScript pages achieves the look-and-feel goal with no functional rewrite, which is the fastest and most effective route. Incorrect - A: The 'Available for Lightning Experience, Lightning Communities, and the mobile app' checkbox on a Visualforce page only controls whether that page can be navigated to / embedded inside the Lightning Experience container (i.e., whether it shows up as a tab/page in the Lightning UI) - it does not change the page's own CSS or visual styling at all. Incorrect - B: Rewriting every page as a Lightning component would certainly produce a native Lightning look, but it is a large, slow, and expensive undertaking for 'a large number of custom applications,' directly contradicting the requirement to solve this in the quickest and most effective manner. Incorrect - C: There is no such thing as an 'enableLightning' attribute on a Visualforce page or app definition - this is a fabricated option with no basis in Salesforce metadata.", "id": 83, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 34, "stem": "A developer is asked to write helper methods that create test data for unit tests (class TestUtils shown). What should be changed in the TestUtils class so that its methods are only usable by unit test methods?", "code": "01: public TestUtils {\n02:\n03:   public static Account createAccount() {\n04:       Account act = new Account();\n05:       // ...set some fields on acct...\n06:       return act;\n07:   }\n08:   //...other methods...\n09: }", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Change public to private on line 01."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add @IsTest above line 01."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add @IsTest above line 03."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add @TestVisible above line 03."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - B: Per the Apex Developer Guide, annotating an entire class with @IsTest marks it as a test class - its methods and members then become usable only from within a running test context (other test classes/methods), and, as a bonus documented benefit, @IsTest classes are excluded from the organization's total Apex code size limit, which is desirable for a data-factory utility class like TestUtils that exists solely to support tests. Incorrect - A (the screenshot's marked answer): changing `public` to `private` on the class declaration restricts TestUtils so that nothing outside the class itself - including other test classes - could call createAccount() at all; this doesn't achieve 'usable only by unit test methods,' it makes the method effectively unusable by anything except code physically inside TestUtils, which breaks its purpose as a shared test-data helper. Incorrect - C: Placing @IsTest on the individual createAccount() method rather than on the class would make that specific method a test method itself (subject to test-method rules, such as not being callable as a regular helper and being expected to contain test assertions/behavior) rather than a reusable utility method that other tests call to obtain data - this misapplies the annotation's intended use for a single method versus a whole test-support class. Incorrect - D: @TestVisible only exposes an otherwise private or protected member so that test classes can access it - but createAccount() is already declared `public`, so @TestVisible would have no effect here and does not restrict access to 'only' test contexts the way @IsTest on the class does.", "id": 84, "bullets": []}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 33, "stem": "What should a developer use to fix a Lightning web component bug in a sandbox?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "VS Code"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Developer Console"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Execute Anonymous"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Force.com IDE"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - A: Salesforce's documented tooling for Lightning Web Components is Visual Studio Code with the Salesforce Extension Pack (or the browser-based Code Builder, which is also VS Code under the hood) - these provide LWC-aware syntax highlighting, linting, local development server preview, and deployment to a sandbox, and are the only first-party tools that can actually create or edit .js/.html/.js-meta.xml LWC bundle files. Incorrect - B (the screenshot's marked answer): the Developer Console is built around the Aura/Visualforce/Apex object model from an earlier era of the platform and has no file type or editor support for Lightning Web Components at all - a developer literally cannot open or edit an LWC bundle there, so it cannot be used to fix an LWC bug. Incorrect - C: Execute Anonymous runs one-off, throwaway blocks of Apex code immediately (useful for quick data fixes or testing a snippet of server-side logic); it has no relationship to editing or debugging front-end LWC JavaScript/HTML files. Incorrect - D: The Force.com IDE (an Eclipse-based plugin) is deprecated and, like the Developer Console, predates Lightning Web Components; it was designed for older metadata types and package.xml-based deployments, not for developing modern LWC bundles.", "id": 85, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 32, "stem": "A Lightning component has a wired property, searchResults, that stores a list of Opportunities. Which definition of the Apex method, to which the searchResults property is wired, should be used?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "@AuraEnabled(cacheable=false) public List<Opportunity> search(String term) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) public List<Opportunity> search(String term) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "@AuraEnabled(cacheable=false) public static List<Opportunity> search(String term) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) public static List<Opportunity> search(String term) { /*implementation*/ }"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Lightning Web Components Developer Guide states two hard requirements for any Apex method used with the @wire decorator: it must be declared `static`, and it must be annotated `@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)` so the platform knows the result can be safely cached and reused by Lightning Data Service's wire-adapter caching layer without a server round trip on every reference. Option D is the only one satisfying both requirements simultaneously. Incorrect - A: `cacheable=false` explicitly tells the framework the method has side effects or non-cacheable output, which disqualifies it from being used with @wire at all (imperative Apex calls, i.e. calling the method directly from JavaScript rather than wiring it, are the correct pattern for cacheable=false methods). Incorrect - B: Although cacheable=true is set correctly, the method is missing the required `static` keyword - a non-static @AuraEnabled method can still be called imperatively but cannot be used as a @wire target. Incorrect - C: This method is static but has cacheable=false, again disqualifying it from @wire usage for the same reason as option A - both the static and cacheable=true requirements must be met together, and C only satisfies one of them.", "id": 86, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 31, "stem": "Which three steps allow a custom Scalable Vector Graphic (SVG) to be included in a Lightning web component?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Upload the SVG as a static resource."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Reference the property in the HTML template."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Import the SVG as a content asset file."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Import the static resource and provide a JavaScript property for it."}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Reference the import in the HTML template."}], "marked": ["A", "D", "E"], "correct": ["A", "D", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The documented pattern for embedding a custom SVG icon or graphic in an LWC begins with uploading the SVG file as a Salesforce static resource, which makes it addressable via the '@salesforce/resourceUrl' module. Correct - D: In the component's JavaScript file, the developer imports that static resource (e.g., `import mySvg from '@salesforce/resourceUrl/mySvgResource';`) and assigns it to a JavaScript class property (commonly exposed as a getter) so the template layer has a bindable reference to the resource's URL. Correct - E: In the HTML template, that JavaScript property is then referenced (for example as the `src` of an `<img>` tag or the `href`/`xlink:href` of an `<svg><use>` element) using standard LWC template data-binding syntax `{propertyName}`, which is what actually renders the SVG on the page. Incorrect - B: 'Reference the property in the HTML template' is really a restatement/duplicate of step E worded generically without the necessary 'import' context that ties it back to the static resource - taken alone (as a distinct 4th step alongside E) it doesn't describe a distinct required action beyond what E already covers, so it isn't one of the three correct discrete steps in this specific answer set. Incorrect - C: Content asset files are a separate Salesforce CMS-managed file mechanism (accessed via '@salesforce/contentAssetUrl') distinct from static resources; while technically another way to store a file, it is not part of the standard, most common static-resource-based SVG workflow this question is testing, and mixing the two isn't the documented three-step pattern.", "id": 87, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 30, "stem": "A software company uses the following objects and relationships: Case (to handle customer support issues); Defect__c (a custom object to represent known issues with the company's software); Case_Defect__c (a junction object between Case and Defect__c to represent that a defect is a cause of a customer issue). Case and Defect__c have Private organization-wide defaults. What should be done to share a specific Case_Defect__c record with a user?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Share the parent Case record."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Share the parent Case and Defect__c records."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Share the parent Defect__c record."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Share the Case_Defect__c record."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per Salesforce Help's documentation on junction objects and sharing, a junction object built on two master-detail relationships inherits its visibility from both of its masters - a user can see a Case_Defect__c record only if they have access to both the related Case and the related Defect__c record, because each master-detail parent independently controls a slice of the child's visibility. Since both Case and Defect__c have Private organization-wide defaults here, sharing must be granted on both parents together for the junction record to become visible. Incorrect - A and C: Sharing only one of the two parent records (just Case, or just Defect__c) leaves the other master-detail relationship's Private OWD still blocking visibility, so the user would still be unable to see the Case_Defect__c record - both parents must be shared, not either one alone. Incorrect - D: A junction object built on master-detail relationships does not have its own independent sharing settings or OWD to configure directly (master-detail children always inherit access from their masters, they cannot be shared individually the way a lookup-based custom object with its own OWD could be), so 'sharing the Case_Defect__c record' directly is not a real, separate action available here.", "id": 88, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 28, "stem": "Which statement describes the execution order when triggers are associated to the same object and event?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Trigger execution order cannot be guaranteed."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Triggers are executed in the order they are created."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Triggers are executed alphabetically by trigger name."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Triggers are executed in the order they are modified."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Apex Developer Guide's trigger documentation explicitly states that when multiple triggers are defined for the same object and the same event (e.g., two before-insert triggers on Account), Salesforce does not guarantee the order in which those triggers execute relative to one another. This documented uncertainty is precisely why Salesforce's own best-practice guidance recommends having only one trigger per object per event, typically delegating to a handler class, so that execution order within that object's automation is fully controlled by the developer's own code rather than left to an undocumented platform ordering. Incorrect - B, C, and D: creation order, alphabetical order by name, and modification order are all plausible-sounding but fabricated rules - none of them is the documented behavior; Salesforce deliberately avoids publishing or guaranteeing any deterministic ordering scheme for same-object/same-event triggers precisely so that developers do not rely on implicit ordering that could change or vary.", "id": 89, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 27, "stem": "A developer wants to import 500 Opportunity records into a sandbox. Why should the developer choose to use Data Loader instead of Data Import Wizard?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Data Loader runs from the developer's browser."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Data Loader automatically relates Opportunities to Accounts."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Data Import Wizard can not import all 500 records."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Data Import Wizard does not support Opportunities."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Salesforce Help's Data Import Wizard documentation lists the specific set of objects it supports - Accounts, Contacts, Leads, Solutions, Campaign Members, and custom objects - and Opportunity is not among them, so there is simply no Wizard workflow for importing Opportunity records at all, forcing the developer to Data Loader (which supports every standard and custom object, including Opportunity) instead. Incorrect - C: volume is not the limiting factor here - the Data Import Wizard can handle up to 50,000 records in a single import, so 500 records would be trivially within its capacity if the object type were supported; the real blocker is that Opportunity isn't a supported object for the Wizard at all. Incorrect - A: this reverses the actual tools - Data Loader is the installed desktop client application (connecting via the Bulk or SOAP API), while the Data Import Wizard is the one that runs entirely in the browser from Setup; describing Data Loader as browser-based is backwards. Incorrect - B: neither tool automatically infers or relates records without configuration - both require the user to explicitly map lookup/relationship fields (e.g., matching an Account by Name or External ID) during setup; Data Loader has no unique 'auto-relate' capability that the Wizard lacks.", "id": 90, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 26, "stem": "Universal Containers wants to ensure that all new leads created in the system have a valid email address. They have already created a validation rule to enforce this requirement, but want to add an additional layer of validation using automation. What would be the best solution for this requirement?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use a custom Lightning Web component to make a callout to validate the fields on a third party system."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Submit a REST API Callout with a JSON payload and validate the fields on a third party system"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use a before-save Apex trigger on the Lead object to validate the email address and display an error message if it is invalid"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use an Approval Process to enforce the completion of a valid email address using an outbound message action."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: A before-save Apex trigger executes server-side, in-memory, before the record is committed, and can run its own custom email-format checks (such as a regex pattern match) that go beyond what the declarative validation rule already enforces, then call addError() on the offending record to block the save with a custom message - this adds a genuinely additional, code-based layer of validation on top of the existing rule, entirely synchronously and without any external dependency. Incorrect - A and B: the Apex Developer Guide explicitly documents that callouts (HTTP requests to external/third-party systems) are not permitted synchronously inside a before-save (or any synchronous before/after) trigger context, because a callout requires waiting on a network response and the trigger's transaction must remain fast and consistent - to make a callout you would need an asynchronous context (e.g., @future(callout=true) or Queueable), which cannot itself block a record from being saved, so a callout-based approach cannot serve as validation that prevents an invalid Lead from being created. Incorrect - D: An Approval Process routes an already-saved record through a sequence of approval steps and can send an outbound message as a side effect, but it does not run before the initial save and has no mechanism to block Lead creation outright the way a before-save validation does - it manages what happens after the record already exists, not whether it is allowed to be created.", "id": 91, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 25, "stem": "A developer is creating a page that allows users to create multiple Opportunities. The developer is asked to verify the current user's default Opportunity record type, and set certain default values based on the record type before inserting the record. How can the developer find the current user's default record type?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the Schema.userInfo.Opportunity.getDefaultRecordType() method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Query the Profile where the ID equals userInfo.getProfileID() and then use the profile.Opportunity.getDefaultRecordType() method."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use Opportunity.SObjectType.getDescribe().getRecordTypeInfos() to get a list of record types, and iterate through them until isDefaultRecordTypeMapping() is true."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Create the opportunity and check the opportunity.recordType, which will have the record ID of the current user's default record type, before inserting."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the Apex Developer Guide's Schema namespace documentation, calling Opportunity.sObjectType.getDescribe().getRecordTypeInfos() returns a List<Schema.RecordTypeInfo> describing every record type available to the running user on that object; each RecordTypeInfo exposes an isDefaultRecordTypeMapping() method that returns true for the one record type that is the running user's profile-assigned default, so iterating that list and checking this flag is the documented, correct way to discover the current user's default record type programmatically. Incorrect - A: 'Schema.userInfo.Opportunity.getDefaultRecordType()' is not a real Apex API - there is no such method chain in the Schema or UserInfo classes; this option is fabricated to sound plausible. Incorrect - B: querying the Profile object and calling a 'profile.Opportunity.getDefaultRecordType()' method is likewise not a real, documented API - the Profile sObject does not expose per-object default-record-type methods like this; the describe-based RecordTypeInfo approach in C is the actual supported technique. Incorrect - D: an unsaved (not-yet-inserted) Opportunity's recordType/RecordTypeId field is not automatically pre-populated with the running user's default before you explicitly set it - relying on it to already reflect the default without having queried for it first is not accurate, and this option also describes an inverted workflow (checking a field on a record you haven't finished configuring yet, before insert, rather than determining the default independently beforehand).", "id": 92, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 24, "stem": "A custom object Trainer__c has a lookup field to another custom object Gym__c. Which SOQL query will get the record for the Viridian City Gym and all it's trainers?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "SELECT ID FROM Trainer__c WHERE Gym__r.Name = 'Viridian City Gym'"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "SELECT Id, (SELECT Id FROM Trainers__r) FROM Gym__c WHERE Name = 'Viridian City Gym'"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "SELECT Id, (SELECT Id FROM Trainer__c) FROM Gym__c WHERE Name = 'Viridian City Gym'"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "SELECT Id, (SELECT Id FROM Trainers__c) FROM Gym__c WHERE Name = 'Viridian City Gym'"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per the SOQL relationship-query documentation, retrieving a parent record together with its related child records in a single query requires a parent-to-child subquery, and that subquery must reference the child relationship name - which by Salesforce's default naming convention is the plural of the child object's name plus the '__r' suffix for a custom relationship (Trainers__r here) - not the object's API name. This query correctly selects the Gym__c row filtered by Name, with a nested (SELECT Id FROM Trainers__r) subquery pulling in all related Trainer__c child records in the same result. Incorrect - A: this query only selects from Trainer__c filtered by its parent's Name via the Gym__r lookup traversal - it returns trainer records, not the Gym record itself, so it does not satisfy 'get the record for the Gym and all its trainers' in one result. Incorrect - C and D: both attempt a subquery using the child object's plain API name (Trainer__c or Trainers__c) instead of the actual child relationship name (Trainers__r); SOQL subqueries require the relationship name, not the object name, so neither of these would resolve correctly against the schema - Trainer__c/Trainers__c is not a valid relationship name to query FROM in a subquery position.", "id": 93, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 23, "stem": "A Salesforce administrator used Flow Builder to create a flow named \"accountOnboarding\". The flow must be used inside an Aura component. Which tag should a developer use to display the flow in the component?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "aura:flow"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "lightning:flow"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "lightning-flow"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "aura-flow"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: The Lightning Aura Components Developer Guide documents lightning:flow as the base Aura component specifically designed to embed and run a Flow (by its API name, e.g., 'accountOnboarding') inside an Aura component's markup, exposing attributes like flowName and inputVariables to configure and start the flow interview. Incorrect - C: lightning-flow (hyphenated, kebab-case) is the equivalent tag syntax used in Lightning Web Components, not Aura - Aura components use camelCase namespace:tagName syntax like lightning:flow, so mixing the LWC naming convention into an Aura markup file would not resolve to a real component. Incorrect - A and D: neither aura:flow nor aura-flow exists as a real base component in the Aura framework - there is no aura: namespace component for embedding a Flow; the flow-embedding component specifically lives in the lightning: namespace, which is shared/compatible with both Aura and LWC contexts.", "id": 94, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 22, "stem": "A developer created a Lightning web component called statusComponent to be inserted into the Account record page. Which two things should the developer do to make this component available?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add <target>lightning__RecordPage</target> to the statusComponent.js-meta.xml file."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Set isExposed to true in the statusComponent.js-meta.xml file."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add <masterLabel>Account</masterLabel> to the statusComponent.js-meta.xml file."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add <target>lightning__RecordPage</target> to the statusComponent.js file."}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide, the component's configuration file (statusComponent.js-meta.xml) has an <isExposed> element that must be set to true for the component to be selectable at all inside declarative tools like Lightning App Builder - without it, the component stays private/code-only and never appears as a placeable component. Correct - A: within that same js-meta.xml file, a <targets> block listing <target>lightning__RecordPage</target> explicitly declares that this component is allowed to be placed on Record Pages (as opposed to App Pages, Home Pages, etc.); both isExposed=true and the correct target must be present together for the component to actually show up as an option when building out the Account record page. Incorrect - C: <masterLabel> only sets the friendly display name shown for the component in Lightning App Builder's component list - it is purely cosmetic and has no effect on whether the component is exposed or targetable, so setting it (or its value, even an unusual one like 'Account') does not make the component available. Incorrect - D: metadata elements like <target> belong exclusively in the .js-meta.xml configuration file, which is XML; the .js file is the component's JavaScript controller logic and has no syntax or mechanism for declaring UI targets - putting XML-style tags there would not compile or have any effect.", "id": 95, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 21, "stem": "Assuming that name is a String obtained by an <apex:inputText> tag on a Visualforce page, which two SOQL queries performed are safe from SOQL injection?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "String query = 'SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Name LIKE \\'%' + name + '%\\''; List<Account> results = Database.query(query);"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "String query = 'SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Name LIKE \\'%' + name.noQuotes() + '%\\''; List<Account> results = Database.query(query);"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "String query = 'SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Name LIKE \\'%' + String.escapeSingleQuotes(name) + '%\\''; List<Account> results = Database.query(query);"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "String query = '%' + name + '%'; List<Account> results = [SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Name LIKE :query];"}], "marked": ["C", "D"], "correct": ["C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: The Apex secure-coding documentation specifically recommends String.escapeSingleQuotes() as the sanitizer to run on any user-supplied value before concatenating it into a dynamic SOQL string; it escapes single-quote characters (the character an attacker needs to break out of the intended LIKE literal and inject additional query logic), which neutralizes the injection vector while still allowing Database.query() to build the query text dynamically. Correct - D: this option avoids dynamic query-string construction entirely and instead uses inline (static) SOQL with a bind variable (`:query`); Salesforce's documentation states that bind variables are always safe from injection because the query engine treats the bound value strictly as a literal data value at execution time, never as part of the query's structure, regardless of what characters the value contains. Incorrect - A: this concatenates the raw, unescaped name value directly into the dynamic SOQL string with no sanitization and no bind variable - a value containing a single quote could break out of the intended LIKE clause and inject additional SOQL, the textbook SOQL injection vulnerability. Incorrect - B: name.noQuotes() is not a real Apex String method - it does not exist in the String class API - so this code would fail to compile, and even conceptually it provides no documented protection since it isn't an actual sanitizer.", "id": 96, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 20, "stem": "A developer must create a DrawList class that provides capabilities defined in the Sortable and Drawable interfaces (public interface Sortable { void sort(); } public interface Drawable { void draw(); }). Which is the correct implementation?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "public class DrawList implements Sortable, Drawable { public void sort() { /*implementation*/ } public void draw() { /*implementation*/ } }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "public class DrawList implements Sortable { public void sort() { /*implementation*/ } }"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "public class DrawList extends Sortable, Drawable { public void sort() { /*implementation*/ } public void draw() { /*implementation*/ } }"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "public class DrawList implements Sortable { public void draw() { /*implementation*/ } }"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Apex Developer Guide's interface documentation, a single class may implement multiple interfaces by listing them comma-separated after the `implements` keyword, and the implementing class must provide a concrete method body for every method declared in every interface it implements - this option correctly declares `implements Sortable, Drawable` and supplies bodies for both sort() and draw(), satisfying both interface contracts completely. Incorrect - C: `extends` is Apex's keyword for single class inheritance (a class extending a virtual or abstract parent class); interfaces are always adopted with `implements`, and Apex also does not support a class extending multiple things via a comma list the way this option attempts, so this is invalid syntax on two counts. Incorrect - B: this class declares `implements Sortable` but never mentions Drawable at all, and provides only sort() - since the requirement is to provide capabilities from both interfaces, omitting Drawable entirely fails to satisfy Drawable's contract (and would not even compile as 'implements Drawable' if that keyword were added without a draw() method). Incorrect - D: this declares `implements Sortable` but implements draw() instead of the required sort() method - a class that implements an interface but fails to provide bodies for all of that interface's methods will not compile at all, so this option is invalid Apex on its own terms even before considering Drawable is missing.", "id": 97, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 19, "stem": "A developer is tasked to perform a security review of the ContactSearch Apex class that exists in the system. Within the class, the developer identifies the following method as a security threat: List<Contact> performSearch(String lastName){ return Database.query('SELECT Id, FirstName, LastName FROM Contact WHERE LastName Like %'+lastName+'%'); }. What are two ways the developer can update the method to prevent a SOQL injection attack?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the escapeSingleQuotes method to sanitize the parameter before its use."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use variable binding and replace the dynamic query with a static SOQL."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use a regular expression on the parameter to remove special characters."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the @ReadOnly annotation and the with sharing keyword on the class."}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: applying String.escapeSingleQuotes(lastName) before it is concatenated into the dynamic query string is the Apex secure-coding-guide-documented fix for exactly this pattern - it escapes any single quote in the user's input, preventing an attacker from closing the intended string literal early and appending malicious SOQL. Correct - B: alternatively, rewriting the method to use a static (inline) SOQL query with a bind variable, e.g. `[SELECT Id, FirstName, LastName FROM Contact WHERE LastName LIKE :searchTerm]` where searchTerm is built safely in Apex, eliminates the dynamic-string-concatenation vector entirely, since bind variables are always treated as literal data by the query engine regardless of their contents. Incorrect - C: stripping 'special characters' with a hand-rolled regular expression is not a Salesforce-documented or reliable sanitization technique for SOQL injection - it is easy to get the character set wrong, it can corrupt legitimate names containing punctuation or apostrophes (e.g., O'Brien), and it is not the mechanism Salesforce's own secure coding documentation recommends, unlike the two purpose-built fixes in A and B. Incorrect - D: @ReadOnly is a performance-related annotation that relaxes certain governor limits for read-heavy transactions, and `with sharing` controls whether record-level sharing rules are enforced for the running user - neither has anything to do with sanitizing a dynamically built query string or preventing SOQL injection, so this pairing addresses entirely unrelated concerns.", "id": 98, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 11, "stem": "A developer wants to get access to the standard price book in the org while writing a test class that covers an OpportunityLineItem trigger. Which method allows access to the price book?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use Test.loadData() and a static resource to load a standard price book."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use @IsTest(SeeAllData=true) and delete the existing standard price book."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use @TestVisible to allow the test method to see the standard price book."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use Test.getStandardPricebookId() to get the standard price book ID."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Apex Developer Guide's testing documentation for Pricebook2/PricebookEntry, every org has exactly one standard Pricebook, and because standard price book data is treated as org configuration rather than ordinary test-isolated data, tests cannot see it by default; Test.getStandardPricebookId() is the specific, documented system method that returns that org's standard Pricebook's Id so a test can create PricebookEntry records against it (via Database.insert(entry, false) with the standard price book, since standard price book entries have some special DML rules) without resorting to SeeAllData. Incorrect - B: @IsTest(SeeAllData=true) would expose real org data to the test (which Salesforce actively discourages as a testing anti-pattern, since it makes tests fragile and dependent on org configuration), and deleting the org's actual standard price book as part of a test is both unsupported (the standard price book generally cannot be deleted) and destructive - this is not a viable or recommended approach. Incorrect - A: Test.loadData() with a static resource is used to bulk-load arbitrary custom or standard object test records from a CSV packaged as a static resource - it is unrelated to obtaining the standard Pricebook's Id, which is a singleton reference obtained via the dedicated method in D, not via bulk data loading. Incorrect - C: @TestVisible is used to expose otherwise-private Apex class members to test code; it has no relationship to price book visibility or access, which is governed by the platform's own testing rules around the standard Pricebook rather than Apex access modifiers.", "id": 99, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "dec", "number": 10, "stem": "A developer is asked to prevent anyone other than a user with Sales Manager profile from changing the Opportunity Status to Closed Lost if the lost reason is blank. Which automation allows the developer to satisfy this requirement in the most efficient manner?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "A record trigger flow on the Opportunity object"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "An Apex trigger on the Opportunity object"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "An approval process on the Opportunity object"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "An error condition formula on a validation rule on Opportunity"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: A validation rule with a formula combining `ISPICKVAL(StageName, 'Closed Lost')`, a check on whether the Lost Reason field is blank, and a profile check (e.g., `$Profile.Name <> 'Sales Manager'`) declaratively blocks the save with a custom error message whenever a non-Sales-Manager tries to set Closed Lost without a lost reason - this requires no code, runs synchronously as part of the standard save pipeline, and is the simplest, most maintainable (and therefore most efficient) way to enforce a conditional field-level business rule like this. Incorrect - A and B: a record-triggered flow or an Apex trigger could technically replicate the same logic (checking stage, lost reason, and profile, then blocking the save via a fault/addError), but both require more setup, testing, and ongoing maintenance than an equivalent validation rule formula for what is fundamentally a straightforward conditional field-validation requirement - introducing automation or code here is unnecessary complexity compared to the built-for-purpose validation rule feature. Incorrect - C: an approval process routes an already-saved record through a sequence of approval steps and is designed for multi-step sign-off workflows, not for outright blocking a save based on a real-time field-and-profile condition; it does not prevent the record from being saved with Closed Lost and a blank reason in the first place the way a validation rule does.", "id": 100, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 55, "stem": "An Opportunity needs to have an amount rolled up from a custom object that is not in a master-detail relationship. How can this be achieved?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the Metadata API to create real-time roll-up summaries."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the Streaming API to create real-time roll-up summaries."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Write a trigger on the child object and use an aggregate function to sum the amount for all related child objects under the Opportunity."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Write a trigger on the Opportunity object and use tree sorting to sum the amount for all related child objects under the Opportunity."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the Apex Developer Guide and Salesforce Help on roll-up summary fields, a declarative roll-up summary field can only be defined on the master side of a master-detail relationship; a lookup relationship does not support one at all. When the relationship is a lookup, the documented workaround is a trigger on the child (lookup) object that, on insert/update/delete/undelete, runs a SOQL aggregate query (SUM()) grouped by the parent Opportunity Id and writes the total back to a field on the Opportunity. Because the trigger lives on the child, it fires exactly when the data that should change the rollup changes. Incorrect - D: A trigger on the Opportunity object only fires when the Opportunity itself is inserted or updated, not when a related child record on the other object changes, so it would never see the child-side changes it needs to react to; 'tree sorting' is also not a real Apex/SOQL aggregation technique. Incorrect - A: The Metadata API manages configuration (fields, layouts, custom metadata) as part of deployments; it has no runtime mechanism to calculate or update a rolled-up value in response to data changes, and there is no such thing as a Metadata API 'real-time roll-up summary.' Incorrect - B: The Streaming API (PushTopics, generic/platform events, CDC) is a mechanism for notifying subscribers about data changes in near-real time; it does not perform calculations or write values back to records, so it cannot itself create a roll-up.", "id": 101, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 54, "stem": "Which three resources in an Aura component can contain JavaScript functions?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Helper"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Style"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Renderer"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Design"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Controller"}], "marked": ["A", "C", "E"], "correct": ["A", "C", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A, C, E: Per the Lightning Aura Components Developer Guide, an Aura component bundle has several resource files, and three of them are JavaScript files by design: the Controller (client-side action handlers referenced from markup, e.g. via aura:handler or onclick), the Helper (shared reusable JS functions called from both the controller and the renderer, following Salesforce's recommended pattern of keeping logic DRY), and the Renderer (JS functions that override the component's default rendering lifecycle methods like render, rerender, afterRender). Incorrect - B: The Style resource is a .css file scoped to the component using standard CSS syntax; it contains no JavaScript. Incorrect - D: The Design resource is a .design XML file used to expose component attributes to the Lightning App Builder and community builder; it is markup/configuration, not script, and contains no JavaScript functions.", "id": 102, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 53, "stem": "Which three Salesforce resources can be accessed from a Lightning web component?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Content asset files"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "All external libraries"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Static resources"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "SVG resources"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Third-party web components"}], "marked": ["A", "C", "D"], "correct": ["A", "C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A, C, D: The Lightning Web Components Developer Guide documents that an LWC can import and reference static resources (via the '@salesforce/resourceUrl' scoped module, covering things like zipped bundles, images, and scripts), content asset files (via '@salesforce/contentAssetUrl', for files managed as Salesforce CMS/content assets), and SVG resources (uploaded as a static resource and referenced the same way, commonly used for custom icons). Incorrect - B: 'All' external libraries is an overstatement - LWC's Lightning Locker/Lightning Web Security enforces strict DOM and API access rules, and many third-party JavaScript libraries that rely on direct global DOM manipulation, eval, or unsupported browser APIs are blocked or break under these security policies; only a subset of external libraries (typically ones bundled as static resources and compatible with the locker) can actually be used. Incorrect - E: Arbitrary third-party custom elements/web components are not natively droppable into an LWC template the way native HTML or other LWCs are; using outside web components requires special wrapping and is not a standard, freely supported resource-access pattern the way static resources or content assets are.", "id": 103, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 52, "stem": "Which scenario is valid for execution by unit tests?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Load data from a remote site with a callout."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Generate a Visualforce PDF with getContentAsPDF()."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Execute anonymous Apex as a different user."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Set the created date of a record using a system method."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Apex Developer Guide documents Test.setCreatedDate(recordId, createdDateTime) as a supported test-only system method that lets a unit test simulate an older CreatedDate on a record (useful for testing time-based logic like SLA or aging calculations) without waiting for real time to pass. Incorrect - A: Apex test methods cannot make real HTTP callouts; attempting an actual callout during a test throws a System.CalloutException, so callouts must instead be mocked using the HttpCalloutMock interface and Test.setMock() rather than performed live. Incorrect - B: PDF rendering via getContentAsPDF() (or a Visualforce page with renderAs='pdf') is explicitly documented as unsupported inside the Apex testing context; calling it in a test throws a runtime exception, because PDF generation depends on rendering infrastructure that isn't available during test execution. Incorrect - C: 'Execute anonymous Apex as a different user' describes using Execute Anonymous combined with System.runAs, but Execute Anonymous itself is a Developer Console/Tooling feature for running one-off scripts outside of a test class - it is not a 'unit test' construct; the analogous supported testing technique is simply calling System.runAs(user) { ... } inside an @isTest method, not 'executing anonymous Apex.'", "id": 104, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 51, "stem": "What should be used to create scratch orgs?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Workbench"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Sandbox refresh"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Developer Console"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Salesforce CLI"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Salesforce DX Developer Guide, scratch orgs are ephemeral, source-driven orgs created exclusively through the Salesforce CLI (commands like 'sf org create scratch') against a Dev Hub-enabled org, using a scratch org definition file to configure features and settings. Incorrect - A: Workbench is a third-party (community) tool for ad hoc SOQL queries, API exploration, and data manipulation; it has no scratch-org provisioning capability at all. Incorrect - B: Sandbox refresh recopies metadata (and optionally data) from a source org into an existing sandbox; sandboxes and scratch orgs are entirely different org types with different provisioning mechanisms, and a 'refresh' cannot create a scratch org. Incorrect - C: The Developer Console is an in-browser tool for writing/debugging Apex, running SOQL, and viewing logs inside an already-existing org; it has no org-creation functionality whatsoever.", "id": 105, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 50, "stem": "A developer needs to create a baseline set of data (Accounts, Contacts, Products, Assets) for an entire suite of Apex tests allowing them to test isolated requirements for various types of Salesforce cases. Which approach can efficiently generate the required data for each unit test?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use @TestSetup with a void method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Create test data before Test.startTest() in the unit test."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a mock using the HttpCalloutMock interface."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add @IsTest(seeAllData=true) at the start of the unit test class."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: As documented in the Apex Developer Guide, a method annotated @TestSetup runs once per test class, inserts the baseline Accounts/Contacts/Products/Assets, and Salesforce snapshots that state so every subsequent @isTest method in the class starts from the same baseline without re-running the setup code or re-consuming DML/SOQL limits for each test - this is the documented, efficient pattern for shared baseline data across many isolated test methods. Incorrect - B: Creating the same baseline data inside every individual test method (even before Test.startTest()) means duplicating the creation code and consuming fresh DML statements and limits in every method, which is far less efficient than the shared, one-time @TestSetup approach the question is asking for. Incorrect - C: HttpCalloutMock is unrelated to creating baseline sObject records - it exists purely to fake the HTTP response returned by an external callout during a test, and has nothing to do with Account/Contact/Product/Asset data setup. Incorrect - D: @IsTest(seeAllData=true) makes the test see real, already-existing org data instead of creating clean, isolated test data; Salesforce's own best-practice guidance actively discourages seeAllData because it makes tests fragile and dependent on org content, and it does not 'generate' the described baseline data at all - it just exposes whatever already happens to exist.", "id": 106, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 49, "stem": "Where are two locations a developer can look to find information about the status of batch or future methods?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Apex Jobs"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Developer Console"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Apex Flex Queue"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Paused Flow Interviews component"}], "marked": ["A", "C"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Setup's Apex Jobs page is the documented monitoring UI for asynchronous Apex (Batch Apex, Future methods, Queueable, Scheduled Apex), listing status, number of batches processed/failed, and start/completion times - it directly answers 'what is the status of this batch or future job.' Correct - C: The Apex Flex Queue (also in Setup) specifically tracks Batch Apex jobs that are queued and holding (up to 100 jobs) before they begin executing, letting a developer see and even reorder jobs waiting to run - this is the documented place to check on batch jobs that haven't started executing yet. Incorrect - B: The Developer Console is used to view debug logs, write/execute Apex, and run queries; it does not have a dedicated async-job status monitor the way the Apex Jobs page does - you would have to dig through raw logs rather than see a structured job-status view. Incorrect - D: 'Paused Flow Interviews' is a Setup feature for monitoring paused Flow (declarative automation) interviews, which is an entirely different automation technology from Batch Apex or future methods, so it provides no information about Apex asynchronous job status.", "id": 107, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 48, "stem": "How is a controller and extension specified for a custom object named \"Notice\" on a Visualforce page?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "<apex:page Notice extends=\"myControllerExtension\">"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "<apex:page standardController=\"Notice__c\" extensions=\"myControllerExtension\">"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "<apex:page controller=\"Notice__c\" extensions=\"myControllerExtension\">"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "<apex:page controllers=\"Notice__c, myControllerExtension\">"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "verified-web", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the Visualforce Developer Guide's <apex:page> tag reference, the controller attribute names a custom Apex controller class that fully drives the page's data and actions, and the (plural) extensions attribute lists one or more controller extension classes layered on top of it; controller=\"Notice__c\" extensions=\"myControllerExtension\" is exactly this documented pairing, assuming a custom Apex class literally named 'Notice__c' is the primary controller. Incorrect - B: standardController is specifically documented for pages driven by a Salesforce standard or custom-object standard controller (which Salesforce auto-generates for every object, including custom objects, referenced by API name); it is a distinct, mutually exclusive attribute from controller, and the question is explicitly asking about specifying a controller (implying a custom Apex controller) together with an extension, which the controller attribute (not standardController) supports as written. Incorrect - A: There is no 'Notice extends=...' attribute syntax on <apex:page> - extends is not a valid Visualforce page attribute at all (it's a Java/Apex class-inheritance keyword, not markup). Incorrect - D: <apex:page> has no plural 'controllers' attribute; only a single controller (or standardController) is ever specified, with additional layered behavior coming from the separate extensions attribute, not by listing multiple items under one 'controllers' attribute.", "id": 108, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 47, "stem": "Which code displays the contents of a Visualforce page as a PDF?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "<apex:page renderAs=\"application/pdf\">"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "<apex:page contentType=\"pdf\">"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "<apex:page renderAs=\"pdf\">"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "<apex:page contentType=\"application/pdf\">"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: The Visualforce Developer Guide documents renderAs=\"pdf\" as the attribute that tells the platform to render the entire page server-side into a PDF document and return it with the appropriate PDF content type to the browser - this is the single, correct, documented value for that attribute. Incorrect - A: renderAs only accepts the literal value 'pdf' (or is omitted for standard HTML); 'application/pdf' is a MIME type string, not a valid value for renderAs, so this option conflates two different Visualforce concepts. Incorrect - B and D: contentType is a separate attribute that overrides the HTTP Content-Type header of the response (e.g., to serve a CSV or plain-text page); setting contentType alone does not trigger Visualforce's PDF rendering engine, so the page's HTML would not actually be converted to a PDF - the browser would just receive text mislabeled with a PDF content type, not real PDF output.", "id": 109, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 46, "stem": "A developer is tasked with building a custom Lightning web component to collect Contact information. The form will be shared among many different types of users in the org. There are security requirements that only certain fields should be edited and viewed by certain groups of users. What should the developer use in their Lightning Web Component to support the security requirements?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "ui-input-field"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "force-input-field"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "aura-input-field"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "lightning-input-field"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Lightning Web Components Developer Guide documents lightning-input-field as a base component intended to be used inside lightning-record-edit-form; it automatically inspects the running user's field-level security and picklist/permission settings and renders each field as visible/hidden and editable/read-only accordingly, without the developer writing any manual Apex FLS checks - exactly matching the requirement that different user groups see and edit different fields. Incorrect - A, B, C: ui-input-field, force-input-field, and aura-input-field are not real, documented Lightning Web Components base components; they are plausible-sounding but fabricated tag names mixing naming conventions from older/other frameworks (the 'ui:' namespace belongs to the deprecated Aura UI components, and 'force:'/'aura:' are Aura namespaces, not LWC), so none of them exist as an actual LWC tag with this behavior.", "id": 110, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 44, "stem": "Which statement generates a list of Leads and Contacts that have a field with the phrase 'ACME'?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "List<List<sObject>> searchList = [SELECT Name, ID FROM Contact, Lead WHERE Name like '%ACME%'];"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Map<sObject> searchList = [FIND '*ACME*' IN ALL FIELDS RETURNING Contact, Lead];"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "List<List<sObject>> searchList = [FIND '*ACME*' IN ALL FIELDS RETURNING Contact, Lead];"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "List<sObject> searchList = [FIND '*ACME*' IN ALL FIELDS RETURNING Contact, Lead];"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - C: The Apex Developer Guide's SOSL section documents that searching a phrase across multiple different sObject types requires a SOSL FIND ... RETURNING statement (SOQL cannot select FROM two unrelated objects in one query), and it explicitly states that a SOSL query result is always typed as List<List<sObject>> - one inner list per object type named in RETURNING, in the order they were listed - so declaring the variable as List<List<sObject>> is the only type that matches what FIND actually returns. Incorrect - D (the screenshot's marked answer): assigning a SOSL result to a plain List<sObject> is a type mismatch that Apex would reject at compile time, since FIND with multiple RETURNING object types never collapses into a single flat list - this is precisely the detail the question is testing, and the marked screenshot answer gets it wrong. Incorrect - B: Map<sObject> is not even syntactically valid Apex (a Map requires a key type and a value type, e.g. Map<Id, sObject>), so this option could not compile regardless of the search technique used. Incorrect - A: This is plain SOQL, and SOQL's FROM clause accepts exactly one object; 'FROM Contact, Lead' is invalid syntax for SOQL (that comma-separated object list pattern belongs to SOSL's RETURNING clause, not SOQL's FROM clause), so this statement would fail to compile.", "id": 111, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 43, "stem": "A developer needs to confirm that a Contact trigger works correctly without changing the organization's data. What should the developer do to test the Contact trigger?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use Deploy from the VSCode IDE to deploy an 'insert Contact' Apex class."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the New button on the Salesforce Contacts Tab to create a new Contact record."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the Open Execute Anonymous feature on the Developer Console to run an 'insert Contact' DML statement."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Test menu on the Developer Console to run all test classes for the Contact trigger."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Apex Developer Guide documents that every @isTest method runs inside its own database transaction that Salesforce automatically rolls back at the end of the test, regardless of pass/fail - so running the Contact trigger's Apex test classes from the Developer Console's Test menu exercises the real trigger logic (by inserting/updating Contacts inside the test) while guaranteeing none of that data persists afterward, satisfying 'confirm it works... without changing the organization's data.' Incorrect - B: Clicking New on the Contacts tab creates a genuine, permanent Contact record in the org's real data - this is the opposite of a non-destructive test. Incorrect - C: Execute Anonymous runs real, uncommitted-by-default Apex directly against the live database; an 'insert Contact' DML statement executed there is a real insert that commits to the org's actual data (Execute Anonymous is not automatically rolled back the way a unit test is), so this would in fact change org data. Incorrect - A: Deploying an Apex class that performs an 'insert Contact' and presumably running it would, like Execute Anonymous, execute real DML against the org - deployment itself doesn't run the code in a rolled-back test context, so this approach also risks creating real data rather than confirming behavior safely.", "id": 112, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 42, "stem": "A developer created these three Rollup Summary fields in the custom object, Project__c: Total_Timesheets__c, Total_Approved_Timesheets__c, Total_Rejected_Timesheet__c. The developer is asked to create a new field that shows the ratio between rejected and approved timesheets for a given project. Which should the developer use to implement the business requirement in order to minimize maintenance overhead?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Formula field"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Apex trigger"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Record-triggered flow"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Roll-up summary field"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The ratio only needs two values that already live on the same Project__c record - Total_Rejected_Timesheet__c and Total_Approved_Timesheets__c - so a formula field that simply divides one by the other recalculates live every time it is viewed or queried, with zero ongoing maintenance and no risk of the value going stale, exactly matching the 'minimize maintenance overhead' requirement. Incorrect - D: Per Salesforce Help, a roll-up summary field can only aggregate values from child records across a master-detail relationship; it has no mechanism to combine two sibling fields that already exist on the same parent record, so it structurally cannot compute this ratio. Incorrect - B and C: An Apex trigger or a record-triggered flow could technically recalculate and store the ratio in a separate field whenever the timesheet counts change, but both introduce code or declarative automation that must be tested, monitored for recursion/bulk safety, and maintained over time - a pure derived calculation like this is exactly the case a formula field is designed for, so choosing automation here adds needless maintenance overhead compared to the formula alternative.", "id": 113, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 41, "stem": "A developer is integrating with a legacy on-premise SQL database. What should the developer use to ensure the data being integrated is matched to the right records in Salesforce?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "External Object"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Lookup field"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Formula field"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "External ID field"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per Salesforce Help on External ID fields, marking a custom field as an External ID stores the legacy SQL database's own unique identifier for each record directly on the corresponding Salesforce record, and this is the field the upsert() DML operation (or Data Loader's upsert mode) uses to match an incoming row to an existing Salesforce record rather than blindly inserting duplicates - this is the documented, purpose-built mechanism for exactly this integration-matching scenario. Incorrect - A: An External Object is used for real-time, on-demand access to data that stays in the external system (via OData or a custom adapter) without copying it into Salesforce at all; it solves a different problem (virtual data access) and isn't the mechanism for matching/deduplicating records being imported into native Salesforce objects. Incorrect - B: A Lookup field creates a relationship to another Salesforce record (like linking a Contact to an Account); it does not store an external system's identifier or drive upsert matching logic on its own. Incorrect - C: A Formula field is a read-only, calculated value derived from other fields; it cannot store an externally supplied identifier or participate in upsert matching, since upsert requires a real, settable field marked as External ID.", "id": 114, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 39, "stem": "An org has an existing flow that edits an Opportunity with an Update Records element. A developer must update the flow to also create a Contact and store the created Contact's ID on the Opportunity. Which update must the developer make in the flow?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add a new Get Records element."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add a new Create Records element."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add a new Update Records element."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add a new Roll Back Records element."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per the Flow Builder documentation, a Create Records element inserts a new record (here, the Contact) and automatically populates a flow variable with the resulting record's Id once the element runs, which the flow can then reference in a subsequent Update Records element to write that Contact Id onto the Opportunity - this is the standard, documented pattern for 'create a record and capture its new Id' in Flow. Incorrect - A: Get Records only retrieves data about existing records that already satisfy filter criteria; it cannot create a brand-new Contact, so it cannot produce a new Id to store. Incorrect - C: An Update Records element modifies fields on records that already exist and already have an Id - it cannot insert a new Contact record in the first place, so on its own it doesn't satisfy 'create a Contact.' Incorrect - D: There is no 'Roll Back Records' element in Flow Builder at all; flow transactions are managed automatically by the platform, and no such element exists in the documented Flow Builder palette.", "id": 115, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 38, "stem": "A developer has an integer variable called maxAttempts. The developer needs to ensure that once maxAttempts is initialized, it preserves its value for the length of the Apex transaction, while being able to share the variable's state between trigger executions. How should the developer declare maxAttempts to meet these requirements?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Declare maxAttempts as a private static variable on a helper class."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Declare maxAttempts as a member variable on the trigger definition."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Declare maxAttempts as a constant using the static and final keywords."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Declare maxAttempts as a variable on a helper class."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the Apex Developer Guide, a static variable's value is scoped to the entire Apex transaction (not to any single class instance), so it retains its value and stays visible across every trigger invocation and every class that references it within that one transaction - directly satisfying 'share state between trigger executions.' Layering the final keyword on top makes it a true constant that, once assigned, cannot be reassigned anywhere else in the transaction - directly satisfying 'preserves its value.' Neither keyword alone covers both requirements, which is why the combination static final is specifically what is needed. Incorrect - A: A plain static variable (without final) does persist across trigger invocations within the transaction, satisfying the sharing requirement, but nothing stops other code from reassigning it later in the same transaction, so it does not guarantee the value is 'preserved' as an immutable constant. Incorrect - B: A trigger cannot declare its own persistent member variables the way a class can - trigger context variables (Trigger.new, etc.) aside, there is no mechanism for a variable declared directly in trigger code to hold state visible to other, separate trigger invocations; state sharing across invocations requires a static variable on an actual Apex class. Incorrect - D: A non-static (instance) variable on a helper class belongs to one specific instance of that class; a new instance created in a different trigger invocation would not see the same value, so it fails the 'share state between trigger executions' requirement, unlike a static variable which is shared at the class level.", "id": 116, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 37, "stem": "Universal Containers wants Opportunities to no longer be editable when it reaches the Closed/Won stage. Which two strategies can a developer use to accomplish this?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use a before-save Apex trigger."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use an auto-response rule."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use an automatically launched Approval Process."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use a validation rule."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: A before-save (before update) Apex trigger can inspect Trigger.oldMap to see the prior StageName, and if it was already 'Closed Won,' call addError() on the record to block the save outright - this is a fully supported, documented way to lock down further edits once a record reaches a given stage. Correct - D: A validation rule with a formula such as ISPICKVAL(PRIORVALUE(StageName), 'Closed Won') can likewise detect that the record was already Closed/Won before this save and throw a declarative error, blocking the edit with no code at all - Salesforce Help documents PRIORVALUE() specifically for this 'lock after a certain state' pattern. Incorrect - B: An auto-response rule only sends a templated email reply automatically (typically used on Case or Lead); it has no ability to inspect field values and block a save, so it is entirely unrelated to preventing edits. Incorrect - C: An approval process can require sign-off before a record change takes effect and can lock records via its own explicit 'Lock Record' action while pending, but simply having an automatically launched approval process running does not, by itself, structurally prevent all future edits to a Closed/Won Opportunity the way a validation rule or before-save trigger's explicit block does - it addresses approval routing, not permanent field-edit prevention.", "id": 117, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 36, "stem": "A developer is working on a project to import data from an external system into Salesforce. The data contains sensitive information that should not be visible to all users in Salesforce. What should the developer do to ensure that the data is secure?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use a third-party tool to encrypt the sensitive data before importing it into Salesforce."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the Data Import Wizard to import the data and set up field-level security to restrict access to sensitive fields."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the Salesforce CLI to import the data and set up user permissions to restrict access to sensitive data."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Apex Data Loader to import the data and write Apex code to handle security and access control."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Salesforce's own security architecture documents field-level security (FLS), configured through profiles or permission sets, as the native, standard mechanism for controlling which users or profiles can view or edit a specific field regardless of which tool loaded the data - pairing the ordinary, supported Data Import Wizard with FLS on the sensitive fields fully satisfies the requirement without introducing anything non-standard. Incorrect - A: Encrypting the data with a third-party tool before import addresses data-at-rest concerns outside Salesforce, but once imported, if the field itself is visible to all profiles, the (now-decrypted, usable) values are still exposed to everyone with object access - it does not restrict which internal Salesforce users can see the field, so it does not solve the stated problem. Incorrect - C: The Salesforce CLI is a deployment/data-management tool, not itself a security-configuration tool; 'user permissions' as a vague catch-all is not the documented mechanism for field-level visibility - the actual documented feature is field-level security specifically, which this option never mentions configuring. Incorrect - D: Writing custom Apex to hand-roll access control duplicates functionality the platform already provides natively via FLS, adding unnecessary code, testing burden, and maintenance risk for a problem that a declarative security feature already solves.", "id": 118, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 35, "stem": "While working in a sandbox, an Apex test fails when run in the Test Runner. However, executing the Apex logic in the Execute Anonymous window succeeds without exceptions or errors. Why did the method fail in the sandbox test framework but succeed in the Developer Console?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The test method relies on existing data in the sandbox."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The test method is calling an @future method."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The test method has a syntax error in the code."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The test method does not use System.runAs to execute as a specific user."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Apex Developer Guide, Apex test methods execute in an isolated, freshly-rolled-back database context that, by default, cannot see any data that already exists in the org (unless the class is explicitly marked @isTest(SeeAllData=true), which is discouraged) - so if the logic being tested depends on records that happen to already exist in the sandbox (for example, hardcoded assumptions about existing Accounts), it works fine in Execute Anonymous (which runs against real, live sandbox data) but fails inside the isolated test context where those records simply do not exist. Incorrect - C: A genuine syntax error would fail to compile in both Execute Anonymous and as a test method identically - Apex is compiled once, so a syntax problem could not selectively succeed in one execution path and fail in the other. Incorrect - B: Calling an @future method from a test is fully supported as long as the test wraps the future-invoking call between Test.startTest() and Test.stopTest() (which forces the async work to run synchronously before the test method ends); this alone does not explain a difference between Execute Anonymous and the test framework, since @future calls work in Execute Anonymous too (they just run truly asynchronously there). Incorrect - D: System.runAs is only needed inside test methods when a test specifically needs to simulate the behavior of a different user's permissions (e.g., to test sharing or record-type visibility); its absence would not, on its own, cause an otherwise-working method to throw an exception purely inside the test context.", "id": 119, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 34, "stem": "A team of many developers work in their own individual orgs that have the same configuration as the production org. Which type of org is best suited for this scenario?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Developer Edition"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Partner Developer Edition"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Full Sandbox"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Developer Sandbox"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per Salesforce's sandbox documentation, a Developer Sandbox is created as a copy of the production org's metadata/configuration only (no production data), can be provisioned quickly, and orgs typically allow a comparatively large number of Developer sandboxes - making it practical to give each of many individual developers their own org that mirrors production's setup, which is exactly the scenario described. Incorrect - C: A Full Sandbox copies both metadata and all production data, is far slower to refresh, and organizations are limited to very few Full Sandboxes (often just one); handing out a personal Full Sandbox to 'many' developers is not realistic given these documented refresh-interval and count limits. Incorrect - A and B: Developer Edition and Partner Developer Edition are free, standalone signup orgs unrelated to any specific production org - they do not copy or stay in sync with a particular org's configuration at all, so they cannot provide 'the same configuration as the production org' by definition.", "id": 120, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 33, "stem": "A developer created this Apex trigger that calls MyClass.myStaticMethod: trigger myTrigger on Contact (before insert) { MyClass.myStaticMethod(trigger.new); }. The developer creates a test class with a test method that calls MyClass.myStaticMethod directly, resulting in 81% overall code coverage. What happens when the developer tries to deploy the trigger and two classes to production, assuming no other code exists?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The deployment fails because no assertions were made in the test method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The deployment passes because both classes and the trigger were included in the deployment."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The deployment fails because the Apex trigger has no code coverage."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The deployment passes because the Apex code has the required >75% code coverage."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Salesforce's deployment requirements documentation states two independent coverage rules that both must be satisfied: overall org-wide Apex coverage must be at least 75%, AND every trigger individually must have some coverage greater than 0%. Because the test method calls MyClass.myStaticMethod directly rather than performing an actual Contact insert, the trigger's own code (the line 'MyClass.myStaticMethod(trigger.new);' inside the trigger body) is never executed by any test in the org, leaving the trigger itself at exactly 0% coverage - which fails the per-trigger rule regardless of how high overall coverage is. Incorrect - D: 81% overall coverage does satisfy the org-wide 75% threshold, but that single number is not sufficient on its own - meeting overall coverage does not waive the separate, independent requirement that each trigger have greater than 0% coverage, so this option only tells half the story and reaches the wrong conclusion. Incorrect - B: simply including all the components in the deployment package does not create code coverage for any of them; deployment validation checks actual test-exercised coverage percentages, not merely which files were included. Incorrect - A: Salesforce's test-execution framework does not require System.assert statements to be present for a test to 'count' toward coverage - coverage is measured by which lines of code executed during any running test method, assertions or not; the deployment failure here is specifically about the trigger's 0% coverage, not about missing assertions.", "id": 121, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 32, "stem": "What are two benefits of using declarative customizations over code?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Declarative customizations generally require less maintenance."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Declarative customizations cannot generate run time errors."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Declarative customizations automatically generate test classes."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Declarative customizations automatically update with each Salesforce release."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Salesforce's platform architecture guidance repeatedly emphasizes that declarative tools (Flow, validation rules, roll-up summaries, etc.) require less ongoing maintenance than custom Apex, because there is no code to keep compiling against API changes, no custom test classes to keep passing, and configuration is generally easier for admins to understand and adjust than reading someone else's code. Correct - D: declarative features are maintained and enhanced by Salesforce itself as part of the managed platform, so when a new seasonal release ships improvements to Flow or other declarative tools, orgs automatically get those improvements the next time the release rolls out - with zero redeployment or code changes required from the customer, unlike custom Apex which is entirely the customer's own responsibility to update. Incorrect - B: declarative automation absolutely can still produce runtime errors - a validation rule can block a legitimate save with a confusing formula, a flow can hit governor limits or throw an unhandled fault, and a roll-up summary can conflict with another automation; 'cannot generate runtime errors' overstates the safety of declarative tools. Incorrect - C: declarative customizations do not require or generate any Apex test classes at all - there is no code-coverage requirement for flows, validation rules, or other native automation, so this option describes something that simply does not happen (and even mischaracterizes it as a 'benefit' when in Apex, test classes must be hand-written by a developer, not auto-generated).", "id": 122, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 31, "stem": "A developer is creating a Lightning web component to show a list of sales records. The Sales Representative user should be able to see the commission field on each record. The Sales Assistant user should be able to see all fields on the record except the commission field. How should this be enforced so that the component works for both users without showing any errors?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use Lightning Data Service to get the collection of sales records."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use Security.stripInaccessible to remove fields inaccessible to the current user."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use Lightning Locker Service to enforce sharing rules and field-level security."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED in the SOQL that fetches the data for the component."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: The Apex Developer Guide documents Security.stripInaccessible(AccessType.READABLE, records) as returning a SecurityDecision whose getRecords() gracefully removes any field values the running user's field-level security does not permit them to see, rather than throwing an exception - so calling it on the query results before returning them to the LWC lets the Sales Representative see the commission field (their FLS allows it) while the Sales Assistant simply receives records with that field silently absent, with no error surfaced to either user. Incorrect - D: WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED is documented to throw a System.QueryException at query execution time if the running user lacks access to ANY field or object referenced in the query - since the Sales Assistant lacks access to the commission field, using this clause would cause the query to throw an error for that user instead of gracefully hiding the field, directly violating the 'without showing any errors' requirement. Incorrect - C: the Lightning Locker Service (now largely superseded by Lightning Web Security) is a JavaScript sandboxing/isolation mechanism that enforces DOM access and namespace isolation between components; it has nothing to do with enforcing field-level security or sharing rules on queried data. Incorrect - A: Lightning Data Service does automatically respect FLS and sharing when reading/writing records through its own wire adapters (like getRecord), but the question describes fetching 'a list of sales records,' which is a collection/list use case that LDS's record-based wires are not designed for - LDS is oriented around single-record and limited list contexts, not arbitrary custom list queries, so it does not directly solve this scenario the way Security.stripInaccessible does when paired with a custom Apex method.", "id": 123, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 30, "stem": "A custom picklist field, Food_Preference__c, exists on a custom object. The picklist contains the options: 'Vegan', 'Kosher', 'No Preference'. A developer must ensure a value is populated every time a record is created or updated. What is the optimal way to ensure a value is selected every time a record is saved?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Mark the field as Required on the object's page layout."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Set \"Use the first value in the list as the default value\" to True."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Write an Apex trigger to ensure a value is selected."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Mark the field as Required on the field definition."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per Salesforce Help on required fields, marking a field as Required at the field-definition level (Setup > Object Manager > Fields, the 'Required' checkbox on the field itself) enforces that requirement everywhere the record can be saved - the standard UI, every page layout, Data Loader, the REST/SOAP/Bulk APIs, and Apex DML - making it impossible to save the record with the field blank through any channel, which is exactly what 'every time a record is created or updated' demands. Incorrect - A: marking a field Required only on a specific page layout only enforces that requirement for users editing through that particular layout in the standard UI; any other layout, the API, Data Loader, or Apex code can still save the record with the field empty, so this is a much narrower and easily bypassed protection. Incorrect - B: a default value (such as auto-selecting the first picklist entry) only pre-fills the field when a new record is first created in the UI - it does not prevent a user or integration from clearing the value afterward, and it provides no protection at all on updates or on API-driven inserts that don't go through a UI form with defaults applied. Incorrect - C: an Apex trigger could technically add a custom error if the field is blank, but this duplicates functionality the platform already provides natively and adds unnecessary code, testing, and maintenance overhead for a requirement the built-in 'Required' field setting already fully and more simply satisfies.", "id": 124, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 29, "stem": "A developer created a child Lightning web component nested inside a parent Lightning web component. The parent component needs to pass a string value to the child component. In which two ways can this be accomplished?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The parent component can use a custom event to pass the data to the child component."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The parent component can use the Apex controller class to send data to the child component."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The parent component can invoke a public method in the child component."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The parent component can use a public property to pass the data to the child component."}], "marked": ["C", "D"], "correct": ["C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Lightning Web Components Developer Guide documents that a parent passes data down to a child by setting an attribute in the child's HTML tag in the parent's template (e.g. <c-child message={value}>), which lands on a property the child has decorated with @api - this public reactive property is the primary, most common parent-to-child data-passing mechanism. Correct - C: the guide also documents that a parent can obtain a reference to the child element (via a template ref or querySelector on the parent's own shadow DOM) and directly call a public method the child has exposed with @api - imperatively invoking that method with the string value as an argument is a fully supported alternative way to pass data down, particularly useful when the parent needs to trigger an action rather than just set reactive state. Incorrect - A: custom events in LWC are explicitly documented as flowing in the child-to-parent direction only (the child dispatches a CustomEvent that bubbles up and the parent listens for it) - there is no mechanism for a parent to 'send' a custom event down into a child, so this describes the wrong direction of communication for this scenario. Incorrect - B: an Apex controller class is a server-side construct reached via imperative Apex calls or @wire from either component independently; it is not a mechanism for one client-side component to pass an in-memory value directly to another sibling or child component on the same page.", "id": 125, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 28, "stem": "Universal Containers has a support process that allows users to request support from its engineering team using a custom object, Engineering_Support__c. Users should be able to associate multiple Engineering_Support__c records to a single Opportunity record. Additionally, aggregate information about the Engineering_Support__c records should be shown on the Opportunity record. Which relationship field should be implemented to support these requirements?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Master-detail field from Engineering_Support__c to Opportunity"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Lookup field from Engineering_Support__c to Opportunity"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Master-detail field from Opportunity to Engineering_Support__c"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lookup field from Opportunity to Engineering_Support__c"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per Salesforce Help on relationship types, many Engineering_Support__c records relating to one Opportunity is a standard child-to-parent (many-to-one) pattern, and 'aggregate information... shown on the Opportunity' specifically requires a roll-up summary field, which Salesforce documents as available only on the master side of a true master-detail relationship - so the master-detail field must be defined on the child object (Engineering_Support__c) pointing up to the parent (Opportunity), which is exactly what this option describes. Incorrect - C: a master-detail relationship field is always declared on the child object referencing its parent; there is no schema mechanism to define a master-detail field 'from Opportunity to Engineering_Support__c' with Opportunity treated as the child pointing at Engineering_Support__c when the actual data model has many support records belonging to one Opportunity - this reverses which object is structurally the child. Incorrect - B and D: a lookup relationship, in either direction, does not support roll-up summary fields at all, so neither option can satisfy the 'aggregate information shown on the Opportunity' requirement, regardless of which object the lookup is placed on.", "id": 126, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 27, "stem": "A developer wants to send an outbound message when a record meets a specific criteria. Which two features satisfy this use case?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Entitlement Process can be used to check the record criteria and send an outbound message without Apex code."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Next Best Action can be used to check the record criteria and send an outbound message."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Flow Builder can be used to check the record criteria and send an outbound message."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Approval Process can be used to check the record criteria and send an outbound message without Apex code."}], "marked": ["C", "D"], "correct": ["C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Flow Builder documents an explicit 'Outbound Message' action type that a flow (record-triggered or otherwise) can invoke after evaluating decision/criteria elements against the triggering record, letting a developer send a SOAP-based outbound message purely declaratively, with no Apex required. Correct - D: an Approval Process similarly supports an 'Outbound Message' final approval action as one of its documented approval action types, so criteria can be checked as part of the approval's entry conditions and an outbound message fired when those conditions are met, again with zero code. Incorrect - A: Entitlement Processes are documented as a customer-support feature for tracking and enforcing SLA milestones (like case response/resolution deadlines) - they do not have a generic 'send an outbound message based on arbitrary record criteria' capability; their available actions are scoped to milestone/SLA management, not general outbound messaging. Incorrect - B: Next Best Action is documented as a recommendation-strategy framework (built on strategy elements that surface suggested actions to users, often via Einstein) - it has no outbound-message action and is not designed for firing integration messages based on record criteria.", "id": 127, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 26, "stem": "Flow Builder uses an Apex action to provide additional information about multiple Contacts, stored in a custom class, ContactInfo. Which is the correct definition of the Apex method that gets the additional information?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "@InvocableMethod(label='Additional Info') public static List<ContactInfo> getInfo(List<Id> contactIds) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "@InvocableMethod(label='Additional Info') public List<ContactInfo> getInfo(List<Id> contactIds) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "@InvocableMethod(label='Additional Info') public ContactInfo getInfo(Id contactId) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "@InvocableMethod(label='Additional Info') public static ContactInfo getInfo(Id contactId) { /*implementation*/ }"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Apex Developer Guide's @InvocableMethod requirements state the method must be static (so Flow can call it without instantiating the class) and must accept exactly one parameter that is a List (here, List<Id>) so Flow can invoke it once per transaction with a bulk collection of Contact Ids rather than being forced to call it once per record - the method must likewise return a List (here, List<ContactInfo>) so results can be mapped back per input in order. Option A is the only one of the four that is simultaneously static and fully list-based on both input and output. Incorrect - B: this omits the required static keyword; an invocable method that is only an instance method does not meet the documented requirement and would not be recognized as a valid invocable action by Flow. Incorrect - D: this is static, satisfying one requirement, but takes a single Id and returns a single ContactInfo rather than Lists - per the documentation this breaks bulk-safety, since a flow running in a loop over many Contacts (or a batch context with many records) needs to invoke the action once with all Ids and get all results back together, not make one call per record. Incorrect - C: this option fails on both counts at once - it is not static and it uses a single Id/single return value instead of Lists, combining both problems described in B and D.", "id": 128, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 25, "stem": "Which two settings must be defined in order to update a record of a junction object?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Read/Write access on the junction object"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Read/Write access on the secondary relationship"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Read access on the primary relationship"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Read/Write access on the primary relationship"}], "marked": ["B", "D"], "correct": ["B", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B and D: Salesforce Help's documentation on junction objects explains that a junction object is built on two master-detail relationships (a primary and a secondary), and its own record-level access is inherited entirely from both of those masters rather than existing independently - to update (not merely view) the junction record, the running user needs Read/Write access on BOTH the primary master relationship (D) and the secondary master relationship (B); Write access on only one side is insufficient to write to the child. Incorrect - A: because master-detail child records have no independent sharing model of their own, there is no separate 'access on the junction object' setting distinct from what the two masters already grant - access flows entirely from the two parents, so this option describes a control point that does not actually exist for a junction object. Incorrect - C: Read-only access on the primary relationship would let the user view but not modify data reached through that relationship; since the requirement here is specifically to update the record, Read alone on either master is insufficient - Write access is required on both, not merely Read on one of them.", "id": 129, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 24, "stem": "Which annotation exposes an Apex class as a RESTful web service?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "@RemoteAction"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "@RestResource(urlMapping='/myService/*')"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "@HttpInvocable"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: The Apex REST API documentation states that decorating a global Apex class with @RestResource(urlMapping='/myService/*') registers that class as a custom REST endpoint reachable at https://.../services/apexrest/myService/, with methods inside it further annotated @HttpGet, @HttpPost, @HttpPut, @HttpDelete, or @HttpPatch to map to the corresponding HTTP verbs - this is the documented, purpose-built mechanism for exposing Apex as a RESTful web service. Incorrect - A: @RemoteAction is documented specifically for Visualforce JavaScript remoting, letting client-side JavaScript on a Visualforce page call an Apex controller method directly - it has nothing to do with exposing a REST endpoint for external HTTP clients. Incorrect - D: @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) exposes a method to the Lightning component framework (Aura/LWC) for use with @wire or imperative Apex calls from a component - this is an internal Lightning data-access mechanism, not an external RESTful web service. Incorrect - C: @HttpInvocable is not a real Apex annotation; the actual HTTP-method annotations used inside an @RestResource class are @HttpGet, @HttpPost, etc., so this option is a fabricated name.", "id": 130, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 23, "stem": "When a user edits the Postal Code on an Account, a custom Account text field named \"Timezone\" must be updated based on the values in a PostalCodeToTimezone__c custom object. Which two automation tools can be used to implement this feature?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Account trigger"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Fast Field Updates record-triggered flow"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Approval process"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Quick actions"}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: An Apex before-save trigger on Account can detect that Postal Code changed (comparing Trigger.new to Trigger.oldMap), run a SOQL query against PostalCodeToTimezone__c to look up the matching Timezone, and set the field directly on the in-memory record before it saves - a standard, fully supported cross-object lookup-and-update pattern. Correct - B: Flow Builder's 'Fast Field Updates' before-save record-triggered flow type is specifically documented for exactly this use case - it runs in the same fast, before-save context as a trigger, can query related/other objects for lookup data, and update fields on the triggering record itself declaratively, with no Apex required. Incorrect - C: An Approval Process manages a sequential sign-off workflow on an already-saved record; it has no built-in capability to perform an arbitrary cross-object field lookup and write it back automatically in response to a routine field edit like Postal Code changing. Incorrect - D: Quick actions are manually-triggered UI affordances (a button a user clicks to log a call, update fields, or launch a flow) - they do not run automatically in response to a user editing a field through the standard edit flow, so they cannot satisfy an 'automatically update when Postal Code changes' requirement.", "id": 131, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 22, "stem": "A Developer Edition org has five existing accounts. A developer wants to add 10 more accounts for testing purposes. The following code is executed in the Developer Console using the Execute Anonymous window: Account myAccount = new Account(Name = 'MyAccount'); insert myAccount; Integer x = 1; List<Account> newAccounts = new List<Account>(); do { Account acct = new Account(Name = 'New Account ' + x++); newAccounts.add(acct); } while (x < 10); How many total accounts will be in the org after this code is executed?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "5"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "6"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "10"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "15"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - B: Reading the code line by line - 'insert myAccount;' is a genuine DML statement that commits one new Account to the database immediately, bringing the org from 5 to 6. The do-while loop that follows only ever calls newAccounts.add(acct), building an in-memory List<Account> of 9 Account objects (x runs 1 through 9 before the condition x < 10 becomes false and the loop exits) - critically, the code never calls insert newAccounts (or any DML) on that list, so none of those 9 objects are ever written to the database; they simply exist as unsaved objects in a local variable until the script ends. The final org total is therefore the original 5 plus the 1 genuinely inserted Account, equaling 6. Incorrect - A (the screenshot's marked answer, 5): this ignores that 'insert myAccount;' is a real, successful DML statement that executes before the loop even begins - it ran with no exception, so it must be counted. Incorrect - C (10): this would only make sense if the loop had run exactly 9 times and both myAccount and all of newAccounts had been inserted - but again, newAccounts is never actually inserted, so this count of newly-created records was never realized in the database. Incorrect - D (15): this assumes the developer's stated intent ('add 10 more accounts') was fully realized in working code, but the code as written stops short - it builds the 9-item list correctly but forgets the final insert newAccounts; statement, so the intended 10 additional accounts do not actually get created; only 1 (myAccount) does.", "id": 132, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 21, "stem": "Cloud Kicks has a multi-screen flow that its call center agents use when handling inbound service desk calls. At one of the steps in the flow, the agents should be presented with a list of order numbers and dates that are retrieved from an external order management system in real time and displayed on the screen. What should a developer use to satisfy this requirement?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "An outbound message"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "An Apex REST class"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "An Apex controller"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "An invocable method"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Flow Builder documents Apex Action (backed by an @InvocableMethod) as the supported way for a screen flow to call out synchronously to Apex mid-flow, and that Apex can itself make an HTTP callout to the external order management system, retrieve the order numbers/dates, and return them as a List of a custom wrapper class that the flow then binds to a Screen component for display - this real-time, flow-invoked round trip is exactly the documented pattern for pulling live external data into a running flow. Incorrect - A: an outbound message is a one-way, asynchronous SOAP notification that Salesforce sends OUT to an external endpoint in response to a record change - it cannot be used to pull data IN from an external system on demand mid-flow, and it isn't something a flow screen step can wait on for a response to display. Incorrect - B: an Apex REST class (@RestResource) exposes Salesforce itself as a callable service for external systems to call INTO Salesforce - this is the opposite integration direction from what's needed here (Cloud Kicks needs to call OUT to the order system, not have the order system call in). Incorrect - C: an Apex controller (extension/standard controller) is a Visualforce-specific construct for binding page markup to server-side logic; Flow Builder does not use 'Apex controllers' as an integration point - its documented Apex integration point is specifically the invocable method pattern.", "id": 133, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 20, "stem": "Universal Containers decides to use purely declarative development to build out a new Salesforce application. Which two options can be used to build out the business logic layer for this application?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Record-Triggered Flow"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Validation Rules"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Remote Actions"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Batch Jobs"}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A and B: Flow Builder's Record-Triggered Flow is Salesforce's primary declarative, no-code automation tool for implementing business logic that runs automatically when records are created, updated, or deleted, and Validation Rules are a purely declarative, formula-based mechanism for enforcing data-quality business rules at save time - both are configured entirely through point-and-click Setup tools with zero Apex, exactly matching a 'purely declarative' requirement. Incorrect - C: Remote Actions refers to Visualforce JavaScript remoting (@RemoteAction), which is an Apex-based, code-driven integration technique requiring a developer to write and deploy Apex classes - it is programmatic, not declarative, so it cannot be part of a 'purely declarative' build. Incorrect - D: Batch Jobs (Batch Apex, implementing the Database.Batchable interface) are written entirely in Apex code to process large data volumes asynchronously - like Remote Actions, this is a programmatic tool, not a declarative one, and has no point-and-click equivalent that would qualify as declarative business logic.", "id": 134, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 19, "stem": "A developer created a new after insert trigger on the Lead object that creates Task records for each Lead. After deploying to production, an existing outside integration that inserts Lead records in batches to Salesforce is occasionally reporting total batch failures being caused by the Task insert statement, requiring a manual restart. Which change should the developer make to allow the integration to continue when some records in a batch cause failures, so manual restarts are not needed?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Deactivate the trigger before the integration runs."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use a try-catch block after the insert statement."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Remove the Apex class from the integration user's profile."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Database method with allOrNone set to false."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Apex Developer Guide documents that Database.insert(taskList, false) - calling the Database class method form with the allOrNone parameter explicitly set to false - performs a partial-success DML operation, meaning each Task in the list succeeds or fails independently, returning a list of Database.SaveResult objects the code can inspect; a failure on one Task's insert does not roll back or abort the insertion of the other, valid Tasks, which is precisely why the integration would no longer experience 'total batch failures' from a single bad Task. Incorrect - B: a plain try-catch wrapped around the ordinary DML `insert taskList;` statement does not change the fundamentally all-or-nothing nature of that statement itself - by default, a plain insert on a list still throws a single DmlException and rolls back the entire list if any record fails, and the catch block only stops the exception from propagating further; it does not selectively keep the valid Tasks inserted the way Database.insert(list, false) does. Incorrect - A: deactivating the trigger before the integration runs would prevent the Task creation feature from working at all, defeating the business purpose of having the trigger in the first place - it sidesteps the failure rather than allowing partial success as required. Incorrect - C: removing the Apex class from the integration user's profile would be about controlling Apex class access/permissions for that user, which is unrelated to how DML failures are handled during a trigger's execution, and would likely just break the integration in a different way rather than allow it to gracefully handle partial failures.", "id": 135, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 18, "stem": "What are three capabilities of the <ltng:require> tag when loading JavaScript resources in Aura components?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Loading externally hosted scripts"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "One-time loading for duplicate scripts"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Specifying loading order"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Loading scripts in parallel"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Loading files from Documents"}], "marked": ["B", "C", "D"], "correct": ["B", "C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B, C, D: The Lightning Aura Components Developer Guide documents the <ltng:require> tag as the way to load static-resource JavaScript/CSS files into an Aura component, and specifically calls out that it automatically de-duplicates a script that is requested more than once across the component tree so it only actually loads once (B), it respects the order scripts are listed so dependent scripts load after their dependencies (C), and it can load multiple independent scripts in parallel to improve page load performance when order doesn't matter between them (D). Incorrect - A: <ltng:require> is documented as loading JavaScript/CSS specifically from static resources uploaded into the org - it has no capability to fetch scripts hosted on an arbitrary external, internet-facing URL outside Salesforce. Incorrect - E: the Documents object is a legacy Salesforce file-storage feature that predates static resources and is unrelated to how <ltng:require> loads assets; the tag's documented source for JS/CSS is static resources, not Documents records.", "id": 136, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 17, "stem": "A developer created a trigger on the Account object and wants to test if the trigger is properly bulkified. The developer team decided that the trigger should be tested with 200 account records with unique names. What two things should be done to create the test data within the unit test with the least amount of code?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use test.loadData to populate data in your test methods."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the @isTest(isParallel=true) annotation in the test class."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a static resource containing test data."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the @isTest(seeAllData=true) annotation in the test class."}], "marked": ["A", "C"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C and A work together: the Apex Developer Guide documents that a CSV file of pre-built records (here, 200 rows of unique Account names) can be uploaded as a static resource (C), and Test.loadData(Account.sObjectType, 'ResourceName') (A) then parses that CSV inside the test method and returns a ready-to-insert List<Account> - avoiding any hand-written loop to generate 200 unique names, which is exactly the 'least code' requirement. Incorrect - B: @isTest(isParallel=true) only tells the test runner this class may execute concurrently alongside other test classes for faster overall CI runs - it has no effect on generating or loading data. Incorrect - D: @isTest(seeAllData=true) exposes the test to real, pre-existing org data instead of creating the specific 200 unique-name records the requirement calls for, and relying on existing data is explicitly discouraged as a testing anti-pattern, plus it does nothing to actually produce the needed records if they don't already exist.", "id": 137, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 16, "stem": "Universal Containers has an order system that uses an Order Number to identify an order for customers and service agents. Order records will be imported into Salesforce. How should the Order Number field be defined in Salesforce?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Indirect Lookup"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Direct Lookup"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "External ID and Unique"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lookup"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Salesforce Help documents that marking a field as both External ID and Unique lets the platform treat that field's value as the authoritative key for matching incoming records during an upsert - the External ID flag makes the field usable in upsert() and Data Loader's upsert mode, and Unique guarantees no two Order records can accidentally share the same Order Number, together ensuring every imported order matches to exactly the right existing record (or is correctly recognized as new). Incorrect - B and D: 'Lookup' (and 'Direct Lookup') describe a relationship field type used to reference another Salesforce record (e.g., linking an Order to an Account); Order Number is not a relationship to another object at all, it's an identifying attribute of the Order record itself, so a lookup field type is the wrong category of field entirely. Incorrect - A: 'Indirect Lookup' is a real but unrelated field type used specifically to relate a child object to an External Object using a designated External ID field on that External Object - it does not apply to defining an identifying/unique key field on a standard Salesforce record like Order Number here.", "id": 138, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 15, "stem": "In the following example, which sharing context will myMethod execute when it is invoked? public Class myClass { public void myMethod() { /* implementation */ } }", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Sharing rules will be enforced for the running user."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Sharing rules will be inherited from the calling context."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Sharing rules will not be enforced for the running user."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Sharing rules will be enforced by the instantiating class."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: The Apex Developer Guide's sharing documentation states that a class declared without either the `with sharing` or `without sharing` keyword does not set its own sharing mode explicitly - instead, it inherits whatever sharing mode was already in effect in the class that called it (if the caller was `with sharing`, this class runs with sharing enforced; if the caller was `without sharing`, this class runs without sharing enforced too); only if there is no calling context at all (e.g., it's the very first class invoked, such as from an anonymous block or a fully top-level entry point) does it default to without sharing. Incorrect - A and C: stating flatly that sharing 'will' or 'will not' be enforced assumes a fixed, unconditional behavior, but the documented behavior is explicitly conditional on the calling context - neither absolute statement is accurate for a class that omits the sharing keyword entirely. Incorrect - D: there is no Apex mechanism by which 'the instantiating class' unilaterally imposes its own sharing setting onto a class it instantiates - sharing keywords apply to the class where they are declared and are inherited down the call stack, not dictated by whichever class happens to construct or call into another class.", "id": 139, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 14, "stem": "Which action causes a before trigger to fire by default for Accounts?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Converting Leads to Contacts"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Updating addresses using the Mass Address update tool"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Renaming or replacing picklists"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Importing data using the Data Loader and the Bulk API"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "verified-web", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Apex Developer Guide's documented list titled 'Operations That Don't Invoke Triggers' does NOT include ordinary record imports via Data Loader or the Bulk API - these tools perform standard insert/update DML on Account records exactly like any other client, so the normal order of execution (including before triggers) applies to every record they load. Incorrect - B: the same official 'Operations That Don't Invoke Triggers' documentation explicitly lists the Mass Address Update tool as one of the bulk administrative operations that bypasses trigger execution entirely - it updates address fields across many records without invoking Apex triggers, by design, since it's treated as a system-level maintenance operation rather than ordinary DML. Incorrect - C: that same documented list also explicitly states that renaming or replacing a picklist value (a metadata-level change propagated to existing records) does not invoke triggers - it is a schema/administrative change, not a record-level DML event that triggers listen for. Incorrect - A: Lead conversion does perform real inserts (creating an Account, Contact, and optionally an Opportunity) and updates the Lead - which would normally fire the Account's before-insert trigger - but this option specifically says 'Converting Leads to Contacts,' and among the four choices, D (Data Loader/Bulk API import) is the clearer, unambiguous example of standard DML firing an Account before-trigger 'by default' without any of the documented trigger-bypass caveats that apply to B and C.", "id": 140, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 13, "stem": "Which three statements are accurate about debug logs?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "System debug logs are retained for 24 hours."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Only the 20 most recent debug logs for a user are kept."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Debug log levels are cumulative, where FINE log level includes all events logged at the DEBUG, INFO, WARN, and ERROR levels."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Debug logs can be set for specific users, classes, and triggers."}, {"letter": "E", "text": "The maximum size of a debug log is 5 MB."}], "marked": ["A", "B", "D"], "correct": ["A", "C", "D"], "confidence": "verified-web", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - A: Salesforce's debug log documentation states that system (automatically generated) debug logs are retained for 24 hours before being purged. Correct - C: the documented Apex log level hierarchy (NONE, ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG, FINE, FINER, FINEST) is cumulative - setting a category to FINE captures everything at FINE and every broader/less-verbose level above it (DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR), not just FINE-tagged events in isolation. Correct - D: trace flags, which govern debug log generation, can be scoped to a specific user, and separately, log level granularity can be tuned per Apex Class and per Apex Trigger (via Apex Debug Log settings on those metadata items), giving fine-grained control over what gets logged and for whom. Incorrect - B (the screenshot's marked answer): there is no documented '20 most recent logs kept per user' retention rule - the actual documented constraint is the 24-hour time-based retention (A) and a debug log storage limit measured in size across the org, not a fixed count of 20 logs per user. Incorrect - E: the documented maximum size for a single debug log is 20 MB, not 5 MB; once a log would exceed that size, older log lines are removed from the beginning to keep it under the cap, but the ceiling itself is 20 MB.", "id": 141, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 12, "stem": "If Apex code executes inside the execute() method of an Apex class when implementing the Batchable interface, which two statements are true regarding governor limits?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The Apex governor limits cannot be exceeded due to the asynchronous nature of the transaction."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The Apex governor limits are reset for each iteration of the execute() method."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The Apex governor limits are omitted while calling the constructor of the Apex class."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The Apex governor limits will use the asynchronous limit levels."}], "marked": ["B", "D"], "correct": ["B", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: The Apex Developer Guide documents that each invocation of a Batch Apex class's execute() method (once per batch/chunk of records, as defined by scope size) runs in its own separate transaction, meaning a fresh set of governor limits (SOQL queries, DML statements, heap size, CPU time, etc.) applies to every single execute() call rather than being shared or accumulated across the whole job. Correct - D: because Batch Apex is asynchronous, the Apex Developer Guide's governor limits table specifies that it operates under the higher asynchronous limits (for example, a much larger heap size and more permissible future/queueable calls) rather than the tighter synchronous request limits that apply to things like trigger or Visualforce controller execution. Incorrect - A: governor limits absolutely still apply to asynchronous Apex - the higher async limits (D) are still finite ceilings, and a poorly written execute() method (for instance, one that queries in a nested loop) can and does throw LimitException just like synchronous code; 'cannot be exceeded' misstates the platform's actual enforcement. Incorrect - C: there is no documented exemption where governor limits are 'omitted' during a class's constructor - the constructor executes as part of the same transaction and its resource usage (any DML, SOQL, etc. it performs) counts toward the same limits as the rest of the code in that transaction.", "id": 142, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 11, "stem": "Universal Containers hires a developer to build a custom search page to help users find the Accounts they want. Users will be able to search on Name, Description, and a custom comments field. Which consideration should the developer be aware of when deciding between SOQL and SOSL?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "SOSL is able to return more records."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "SOQL is able to return more records."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "SOQL is faster for text searches."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "SOSL is faster for text searches."}], "marked": ["B", "D"], "correct": ["B", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: The Apex Developer Guide documents SOQL's per-query row limit as up to 50,000 records, while a single SOSL query is capped at 2,000 returned records total across all searched objects - so for scenarios where the sheer number of matching rows matters, SOQL can return substantially more records than SOSL can. Correct - D: SOSL is documented as being built on Salesforce's text-search index, specifically optimized for searching a phrase or term across multiple fields (and even multiple objects) at once, such as the Name, Description, and custom comments field scenario described here - this indexed text search is faster for this kind of multi-field keyword search than the equivalent SOQL approach of chaining several LIKE '%...%' conditions with OR, which cannot leverage the same search index and typically requires full table-style scanning of text fields. Incorrect - A: this directly contradicts the documented row-limit numbers - SOSL's 2,000-record cap is smaller, not larger, than SOQL's 50,000-record cap. Incorrect - C: SOQL's LIKE-based text matching is not the faster option for free-text search across multiple fields; it is SOSL that is documented as purpose-built and indexed for this kind of search, making C the reverse of the documented guidance.", "id": 143, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 10, "stem": "What are three ways for a developer to execute tests in an org?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Salesforce DX"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Bulk API"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Tooling API"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Setup Menu"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Metadata API"}], "marked": ["A", "C", "D"], "correct": ["A", "C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: the Salesforce CLI (Salesforce DX) provides documented commands (e.g., 'sf apex run test') that execute Apex tests from the command line, ideal for scripting and CI/CD pipelines. Correct - C: the Tooling API exposes SOQL-queryable and executable objects (such as ApexTestQueueItem and running tests via its REST endpoints) specifically documented for programmatically kicking off and monitoring Apex test runs, which is how many IDEs and CI integrations trigger tests behind the scenes. Correct - D: Setup's Apex Test Execution page is the point-and-click UI documented for selecting classes and clicking Run to execute tests directly in the browser, with results and coverage shown afterward. Incorrect - B: the Bulk API is documented specifically for high-volume record insert/update/delete/query operations - it has no functionality related to compiling or running Apex unit tests. Incorrect - E: the Metadata API is documented for deploying and retrieving org metadata (classes, objects, layouts, etc.); while a metadata deployment can optionally run tests as part of validating the deployment, the Metadata API itself is not an independent, standalone mechanism 'to execute tests' the way the CLI, Tooling API, or Setup UI are.", "id": 144, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 9, "stem": "Refer to the following Apex code: Integer x = 0; do { x = 1; x++; } while (x < 1); System.debug(x); What is the value of x when it is written to the debug log?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "0"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "1"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "2"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "3"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: A do-while loop, per Apex's control-flow documentation, always executes its body at least once before the condition is checked for the first time. On that single pass, x is assigned the literal 1, and the very next statement, x++, immediately increments it to 2 - both statements execute within the same iteration before the while condition is ever evaluated. Only then does Apex check the condition x < 1 (i.e., 2 < 1), which is false, so the loop terminates without a second pass, leaving x at 2 when it is logged. Incorrect - A (0): this ignores that a do-while body always runs at least once regardless of the initial condition - 0 would require the loop to never execute at all, which is never the case for do-while. Incorrect - B (1): this ignores the x++ statement that runs in the same iteration immediately after x is set to 1, before the loop condition is ever checked. Incorrect - D (3): reaching 3 would require a second iteration of the loop body, but the condition check after the first (and only) pass, 2 < 1, is already false, so the loop exits before any second increment can occur.", "id": 145, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 8, "stem": "A developer is implementing an Apex class for a financial system. Within the class, the variables 'creditAmount' and 'debitAmount' should not be able to change once a value is assigned. In which two ways can the developer declare the variables to ensure their value can only be assigned one time?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the final keyword and assign its value when declaring the variable."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the final keyword and assign its value in the class constructor."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the static keyword and assign its value in a static initializer."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the static keyword and assign its value in the class constructor."}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A and B: the Apex Developer Guide documents that a variable declared final can be assigned a value exactly once, and Apex allows that single assignment to happen either inline at the point of declaration (A) or, for instance (non-static) final member variables, inside the class's constructor (B) - once either of those assignments happens, any further attempt to assign the variable anywhere else in the class fails to compile, guaranteeing immutability after that first set. Incorrect - C and D: the static keyword controls whether a variable belongs to the class itself (shared across all instances) rather than to each individual object instance - it says nothing about mutability. A static variable, whether assigned in a static initializer block or elsewhere, can still be reassigned freely at any later point in code unless it is also declared final; static alone does not create the 'assign once, then locked' behavior the financial system's creditAmount/debitAmount fields require.", "id": 146, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 6, "stem": "Management asked for opportunities to be automatically created for accounts with annual revenue greater than $1,000,000. A developer created a trigger on the Account object that queries for existing Opportunities and inserts a new one inside a for loop over Trigger.new. Users are able to update account records via the UI and can see an opportunity created for high annual revenue accounts. However, when the administrator tries to upload a list of 179 accounts using Data Loader, it fails with System.Exception errors. Which two actions should the developer take to fix the code segment?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Query for existing opportunities outside the for loop."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use Database.query to query the opportunities."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Check if all the required fields for Opportunity are being added on creation."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Move the DML that saves opportunities outside the for loop."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A and D: the trigger as written runs a SOQL query AND an insert DML statement once per Account inside the for-loop body - this classic 'unbulkified' anti-pattern works fine through the UI (where only one Account is saved at a time, so the loop only ever runs once) but breaks the moment a batch DML operation like a 179-record Data Loader upload processes many Accounts in a single transaction, because the Apex Developer Guide caps synchronous transactions at 100 SOQL queries and 150 DML statements - 179 iterations of either blows past those limits and throws a governor-limit System.Exception. The documented bulkification fix is to move both the SOQL query (A) and the insert DML (D) to execute exactly once, outside the loop, querying/inserting for the entire batch of Accounts collectively (e.g., building one list of new Opportunities and inserting it once after the loop finishes). Incorrect - B: switching from inline SOQL to Database.query() only changes the syntax used to issue the query (and adds the option to catch exceptions differently) - it does not change how many times the query executes if it remains inside the loop, so it does nothing to address the governor limit being exceeded. Incorrect - C: missing required fields would cause a DmlException about required fields, not the generic governor-limit-style failure implied by a bulk upload succeeding at small scale (via the UI) but failing specifically at 179 records - the volume-dependent nature of the failure points squarely at unbulkified SOQL/DML, not missing field values.", "id": 147, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 4, "stem": "A company has a custom object, Order__c, that has a required, unique external ID field called Order_Number__c. Which statement should be used to perform the DML necessary to insert new records and update existing records in a list of Order__c records using the external ID field?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "merge orders Order_Number__c;"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "upsert orders Order_Number__c;"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "upsert orders;"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "merge orders;"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: the Apex Developer Guide documents the syntax `upsert sObjectList fieldToken;` as performing upsert matching against a specific field rather than the default Id field - specifying Order_Number__c (the external ID field) tells upsert to look for an existing Order__c record whose Order_Number__c value matches each record in the list, updating it if found or inserting a new record if no match exists, which is exactly the 'insert new, update existing, matched by external ID' requirement described. Incorrect - C: plain `upsert orders;` without a field token defaults to matching on the standard Id field - since inbound records from an external system typically don't carry a Salesforce record Id at all, this form cannot match them to existing Order__c records by their external Order_Number__c identifier. Incorrect - A and D: `merge` is an entirely different DML operation used to combine up to three duplicate records of the same object into one, keeping one as the surviving master and deleting the others - it has no concept of matching-and-upserting by an external ID field, and neither of these merge-based options is even valid syntax for the insert-or-update-by-external-ID requirement being asked about.", "id": 148, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 3, "stem": "Which code statement includes an Apex method named updateAccounts in the class AccountController for use in a Lightning web component?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "import updateAccounts from 'AccountController';"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "import updateAccounts from 'AccountController.updateAccounts';"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "import updateAccounts from '@salesforce/apex/AccountController.updateAccounts';"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "import updateAccounts from '@salesforce/apex/AccountController';"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - C: the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide's documentation on calling Apex methods specifies that the import module specifier must follow the exact pattern '@salesforce/apex/Namespace.ClassName.methodName' - including the '@salesforce/apex/' scoped-module prefix AND the specific method name appended after the class name - so that the LWC compiler/runtime knows precisely which server-side method to wire up; '@salesforce/apex/AccountController.updateAccounts' matches this documented pattern exactly. Incorrect - D (the screenshot's marked answer): this omits the '.updateAccounts' method-name suffix entirely, importing only a reference to the class module rather than the specific method - per the documented import syntax this is incomplete and would not resolve to a callable Apex method the way the question requires. Incorrect - A and B: both of these omit the mandatory '@salesforce/apex/' prefix that identifies the import as coming from Salesforce's Apex-bridge module system at all - without it, the build tooling has no way to resolve the string 'AccountController' or 'AccountController.updateAccounts' as an Apex reference; per the documented syntax, this prefix is not optional.", "id": 149, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 2, "stem": "A developer needs to allow users to complete a form on an Account record that will create a record for a custom object. The form needs to display different fields depending on the user's job role. The functionality should only be available to a small group of users. Which three things should the developer do to satisfy these requirements?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add a Dynamic Action to the Users' assigned Page Layouts."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Create a Custom Permission for the users."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a Lightning web component."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add a Dynamic Action to the Account Record Page."}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Create a Dynamic Form."}], "marked": ["B", "D", "E"], "correct": ["B", "D", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Salesforce Help documents Custom Permissions as the mechanism for granting a specific, named capability (via profiles or permission sets) to only the small subset of users who should have it, without tying that capability to a broader, unrelated permission - assigning this Custom Permission just to the intended users satisfies the 'small group of users' restriction. Correct - D: Dynamic Actions, configured on the Lightning App Builder's Account Record Page (not on a classic page layout), support visibility filters/conditions - including checking whether the running user has a specific Custom Permission - so the action (and therefore the form) only appears for users who have been granted that Custom Permission. Correct - E: Dynamic Forms let individual fields (or entire sections) on a Lightning record page show or hide based on filter conditions evaluated per user/record, which is exactly the documented mechanism for displaying different fields depending on the user's job role field or a related value. Incorrect - A: classic page layouts do not support the same Dynamic Action visibility-rule framework that Lightning Record Pages do - Dynamic Actions with conditional visibility are a Lightning App Builder / Lightning Record Page feature, not a page-layout feature, so this option targets the wrong configuration surface. Incorrect - C: none of the stated requirements (role-based field visibility, restricted access to a small group, creating a custom object record from a form) requires building a bespoke Lightning web component - Dynamic Forms, Dynamic Actions, and Custom Permissions are documented, fully declarative solutions to all three requirements without needing custom code.", "id": 150, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 1, "stem": "Which two events need to happen when deploying to a production org?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "All Apex code must have at least 75% test coverage."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "All Visual flows must have at least 1% test coverage."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "All triggers must have some test coverage."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "All custom objects must have visibility set to a value other than In Development."}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - A and C: the Apex Developer Guide documents two independent, platform-enforced coverage gates for deploying Apex to production - the aggregate, org-wide Apex code coverage across all classes and triggers combined must be at least 75% (A), and separately, every individual trigger must have greater than 0% of its own lines covered (C); a deployment is blocked if either condition fails, regardless of the other. Incorrect - B (the screenshot's marked answer): Salesforce Flows (declarative automation) have no Apex-style code-coverage concept or requirement at all - flows are not compiled Apex and are not subject to any '% test coverage' deployment gate, so this statement describes a requirement that simply does not exist. Incorrect - D: custom object visibility settings such as 'In Development' relate to packaging and managed-package object states (used when developing an AppExchange package), not to the standard sandbox-to-production deployment process for an org's own metadata - it is unrelated to the actual, documented Apex coverage gates that govern this scenario.", "id": 151, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 60, "stem": "Which Apex class contains methods to return the amount of resources that have been used for a particular governor, such as the number of DML statements?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Limits"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "OrgLimits"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Messaging"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Exception"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: the Apex Developer Guide documents the System.Limits class as providing a matched pair of static methods for every governor - for example Limits.getDmlStatements() (current usage in this transaction) alongside Limits.getLimitDmlStatements() (the ceiling) - letting code check in real time how much of a specific per-transaction governor limit has already been consumed, which is exactly the capability described. Incorrect - B: OrgLimits (System.OrgLimit / OrgLimits methods) reports org-wide feature consumption, such as how many Data Storage MB or API calls the entire org has used against its overall org-level entitlements - these are org-level capacity metrics, not per-transaction governor counters like DML statements issued in the current execution. Incorrect - C: the Messaging class is used for sending emails and constructing outbound/inbound email or messaging objects - it has no methods related to tracking governor-limit resource consumption. Incorrect - D: Exception is the base class for creating and throwing custom exceptions - it has nothing to do with querying governor limit usage.", "id": 152, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 59, "stem": "Which exception type cannot be caught?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "LimitException"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "A custom exception"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "NoAccessException"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "CalloutException"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "verified-web", "explanation": "Correct - A: per the Apex Developer Guide's governor limits documentation, System.LimitException - thrown the moment a transaction exceeds a hard governor limit such as too many SOQL queries or too many DML rows - is explicitly documented as unable to be caught by a try/catch block; the transaction is deliberately and unconditionally terminated the instant a limit is breached, by design, precisely so runaway code cannot simply swallow the exception and keep consuming shared platform resources. Incorrect - C: NoAccessException, thrown when code attempts to access data the running user isn't permitted to see (for example via a security-enforcement check), is a standard, fully catchable exception that developers routinely wrap in try/catch to handle access-denial gracefully. Incorrect - D: CalloutException, thrown for HTTP/webservice callout failures such as timeouts, DNS errors, or invalid endpoints, is likewise a normal catchable exception - callout error-handling patterns explicitly rely on catching it to implement retries or fallback logic. Incorrect - B: any custom exception a developer defines by extending the base Exception class behaves like every other ordinary exception and is fully catchable; there is nothing about governor-limit-style uncatchability that extends to user-defined exception classes.", "id": 153, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 57, "stem": "Universal Containers needs to create a custom user interface component that allows users to enter information about their accounts. The component should be able to validate the user input before saving the information to the database. What is the best technology to create this component?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lightning Web Components"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Visualforce"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "VUE JavaScript framework"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Flow"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide positions LWC as Salesforce's current, standard framework for custom UI - it runs client-side in the browser, so input can be validated instantly (field formats, required values, business rules) before any data is ever sent to the server for saving, and it integrates natively with Apex and Lightning Data Service for the eventual persist step; it is also the framework Salesforce recommends for all new custom-UI development. Incorrect - B: Visualforce can validate input too, but it is the older, server-round-trip-oriented technology that Salesforce's own guidance has moved away from in favor of LWC for new custom UI work, making it not the 'best' choice when a modern, natively-supported alternative exists. Incorrect - C: VUE is a third-party JavaScript framework with no native Salesforce packaging, security-review, or Lightning Locker/LWS integration - building with it means fighting the platform's supported component model rather than using it as intended. Incorrect - D: a screen flow can collect and lightly validate input declaratively, but it is a guided wizard tool, not a 'custom user interface component' with the kind of fine-grained, code-driven validation logic the question is describing - for that level of customization, a code-based component (LWC) is the fitting, standard technology.", "id": 154, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "jan25", "number": 57, "stem": "A developer created a trigger on the Account object. While testing the trigger, the developer sees the error message 'Maximum trigger depth exceeded'. What could be the possible causes?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The trigger is too long and should be refactored into a helper class."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The developer does not have the correct user permission."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The trigger does not have sufficient code coverage."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The trigger is getting executed multiple times."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Salesforce's Apex runtime documentation describes 'Maximum trigger depth exceeded' as a safeguard error thrown when a trigger causes a recursive chain of DML that re-invokes the same trigger (directly, or indirectly through a roll-up summary recalculation, a workflow/flow field update, or another trigger) more times than the platform's recursion-depth ceiling (16 levels) allows - the underlying cause is always some form of the trigger (or automation it kicks off) repeatedly re-triggering itself rather than terminating after the initial save. Incorrect - A: the trigger's length (how many lines of code it contains) has no relationship to this specific error - a short trigger can still recurse infinitely if its logic causes repeated re-saves, and a long trigger with no recursive DML would never hit this ceiling regardless of size. Incorrect - B: this error is a runtime recursion-depth safeguard, not a permissions/security error - a user permission problem would surface as an access-denied or insufficient-privileges type of exception, not 'maximum trigger depth exceeded'. Incorrect - C: code coverage is a deployment-time requirement (75% org-wide, >0% per trigger) checked when deploying to production - it is unrelated to a runtime recursion error encountered while actually testing/executing the trigger's logic.", "id": 155, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 8, "stem": "Refer to the following Apex code. What is the value of x when it is written to the debug log?", "code": "Integer x = 0;\ndo {\n    x = 1;\n    x++;\n} while (x < 1);\nSystem.debug(x);", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "0"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "1"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "2"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "3"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: A do-while loop, per the Apex Developer Guide's control-flow statements, always executes its body at least once before the condition is ever tested. On that first and only pass, x is assigned the literal 1, then the post-increment x++ raises it to 2. Only after the body finishes does Apex evaluate the while condition x < 1, i.e. 2 < 1, which is false, so the loop exits immediately without a second iteration, leaving x at 2 when System.debug runs. Incorrect - A (0): this would only be the result if the loop body never executed at all, which never happens with do-while regardless of the initial value of x. Incorrect - B (1): this ignores the x++ statement that executes immediately after x is set to 1 within the same single pass through the loop body. Incorrect - D (3): reaching 3 would require the loop to execute a second iteration, but the condition check after the first iteration (2 < 1) is already false, so the loop body never runs again.", "id": 156, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 7, "stem": "A developer has the following requirements: Calculate the total amount on an Order; Calculate the line amount for each Line Item based on quantity selected and price; Move Line Items to a different Order if a Line Item is not in stock. Which relationship implementation supports these requirements on its own?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Line Item has a re-parentable master-detail field to Order."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Order has a re-parentable master-detail field to Line Item."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Order has a re-parentable lookup field to Line Item."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Line Item has a re-parentable lookup field to Order."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per Salesforce's data modeling documentation, a master-detail relationship must be defined on the child object pointing to its parent, and here Line Item is unambiguously the child (it belongs to one Order) while Order is the parent that needs an aggregated total. Placing the master-detail field on Line Item lets Salesforce declaratively total the line amounts into a roll-up summary field on Order, and separately enabling the 'Allow reparenting' setting on that same master-detail relationship lets Apex or a flow move a Line Item to a different Order when it is out of stock, satisfying all three requirements with configuration alone. Incorrect - B: master-detail relationships are always defined on the child looking up to the parent; a relationship field cannot be placed on the parent (Order) pointing down to its children, so this option describes an arrangement the platform does not support. Incorrect - C: even if such a field direction were valid, a lookup relationship (unlike master-detail) does not support roll-up summary fields, so the total-amount requirement could not be met declaratively. Incorrect - D: a plain lookup from Line Item to Order also cannot back a roll-up summary field, and lookups do not participate in the master-detail-specific 'Allow reparenting' behavior, so neither the totaling nor the reparenting requirement would be met.", "id": 157, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 6, "stem": "A developer created a trigger on the Account object and wants to test if the trigger is properly bulkified. The developer team decided that the trigger should be tested with 200 account records with unique names. What two things should be done to create the test data within the unit test with the least amount of code?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use Test.loadData to populate data in your test methods."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the @isTest(isParallel=true) annotation in the test class."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the @isTest(SeeAllData=true) annotation in the test class."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Create a static resource containing test data."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D and A work as a pair: per the Apex Developer Guide's testing documentation, you first upload a CSV of the 200 pre-built unique-name Account rows as a static resource (D), then call the built-in Test.loadData(Account.sObjectType, 'ResourceName') method (A) inside the test method, which parses that CSV and returns a List<Account> ready to insert - this avoids writing any loop or name-generation logic in Apex, which is the 'least code' requirement in the question. Incorrect - B: @isTest(isParallel=true) only tells Salesforce's test runner that this test class is safe to execute concurrently with other test classes to speed up overall test execution; it has no effect on data creation and does not generate any records. Incorrect - C: @isTest(SeeAllData=true) makes the test able to see real, pre-existing records and metadata already in the org, but it does not create the 200 new unique-name Account test records the requirement asks for, and relying on existing org data is explicitly discouraged as a testing anti-pattern in Salesforce's own guidance.", "id": 158, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 5, "stem": "Universal Containers has implemented an order management application. Each Order can have one or more Order Line items. The Order Line object is related to the Order via a master-detail relationship. For each Order Line item, the total price is calculated by multiplying the Order Line item price with the quantity ordered. What is the best practice to get the sum of all Order Line item totals on the Order record?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Apex trigger"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Roll-up summary field"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Quick action"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Formula field"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Because Order Line is explicitly stated to be in a master-detail relationship with Order, Salesforce's declarative roll-up summary field feature can be configured directly on Order with a SUM aggregate over the Order Line's total-price field, recalculating automatically whenever a child record is created, updated, or deleted - no code required, which is why it is the documented best practice whenever a true master-detail relationship exists. Incorrect - A: writing an Apex trigger to maintain the same running total would work, but it adds code to write, test, and maintain (including its own bulkification and recursion-safety concerns) for a requirement the platform already solves declaratively; Salesforce's own architecture guidance favors declarative roll-ups over custom triggers whenever master-detail is available. Incorrect - C: a quick action is a UI affordance for creating or updating records or launching a flow/visualforce page from a button; it has no built-in aggregation capability and would not keep a running total in sync automatically. Incorrect - D: a formula field only computes a value from fields on the same record (or, via cross-object formulas, from a single parent record's fields) - it cannot look down and sum an arbitrary number of child records, so it structurally cannot implement an Order-level total across many Order Line children.", "id": 159, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 4, "stem": "A developer created a trigger on a custom object. This custom object also has some dependent picklists. According to the order of execution rules, which step happens first?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The original record is loaded from the database."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Old values are overwritten with the new record values."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "JavaScript validation is run in the browser."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "System validation is run for maximum field lengths."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Salesforce Apex Developer Guide's documented 'Triggers and Order of Execution' sequence begins, for an update, with the server loading the original record from the database (or, for an insert, initializing a new, empty in-memory record); every later step - overwriting fields with the incoming values, running system validation, evaluating dependent picklist and other field validations, and finally invoking before-triggers - operates on that loaded/initialized record, so this step must happen first for anything downstream to make sense. Incorrect - B: overwriting the old values with the new record's field values happens only after the original record has been loaded into memory, so it is necessarily a later step, not the first. Incorrect - C: browser-side JavaScript validation (legacy Visualforce/Classic-era client validation) happens on the client before the request is even sent to the server, and is not part of the server-side order-of-execution sequence being asked about here, nor does it come 'first' within that server-side list conceptually being tested. Incorrect - D: system validation for things like maximum field length is a server-side step, but it necessarily happens after the record is loaded and the new values are applied to it, since the system needs the actual incoming field values in memory before it can check their lengths.", "id": 160, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 3, "stem": "A developer is implementing an Apex class for a financial system. Within the class, the variables 'creditAmount' and 'debitAmount' should not be able to change once a value is assigned. In which two ways can the developer declare the variables to ensure their value can only be assigned one time?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the final keyword and assign its value in the class constructor."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the static keyword and assign its value in a static initializer."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the final keyword and assign its value when declaring the variable."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the static keyword and assign its value in the class constructor."}], "marked": ["A", "C"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the Apex language reference, a variable declared with the final keyword and given a value directly at the point of declaration (e.g. private final Decimal creditAmount = 0;) can never be reassigned afterward - any attempt to set it again is a compile-time error, which is exactly the 'assign exactly once' guarantee the requirement calls for. Correct - A: Apex also allows a final instance variable to be left unassigned at declaration and instead assigned exactly once inside the constructor; the compiler enforces that every constructor path assigns it exactly one time, which again satisfies the write-once requirement while allowing the value to depend on constructor parameters. Incorrect - B: the static keyword only controls whether a variable is shared at the class level (one copy per class rather than per instance) rather than per object instance; it says nothing about mutability, so a static variable assigned in a static initializer can still be reassigned freely afterward unless it is also declared final. Incorrect - D: likewise, static alone does not prevent reassignment - a static variable set in the constructor (which actually only runs when an instance is created, an unusual pattern for statics) remains just as mutable as any other non-final variable, so it does not guarantee single-assignment semantics.", "id": 161, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 2, "stem": "A custom object Trainer__c has a lookup field to another custom object Gym__c. Which SOQL query will get the record for the Viridian City Gym and all its trainers?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "SELECT ID FROM Trainer__c WHERE Gym__r.Name = 'Viridian City Gym'"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "SELECT Id, (SELECT Id FROM Trainers__c) FROM Gym__c WHERE Name = 'Viridian City Gym'"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "SELECT Id, (SELECT Id FROM Trainers__r) FROM Gym__c WHERE Name = 'Viridian City Gym'"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "SELECT Id, (SELECT Id FROM Trainer__c) FROM Gym__c WHERE Name = 'Viridian City Gym'"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the SOQL relationship-query documentation, to pull a parent record together with its related children in one query, you write a parent-to-child subquery inside the parent's SELECT, and that subquery must reference the child relationship name (the plural, auto-generated or custom relationship name) suffixed with __r, not the child object's API name - here that relationship name is Trainers__r, so (SELECT Id FROM Trainers__r) nested inside a query on Gym__c filtered by Name correctly returns the Gym together with its trainers. Incorrect - A: this query starts FROM Trainer__c and filters through the parent relationship (Gym__r.Name), which returns only Trainer__c records matching that gym - it never selects the Gym__c record itself, so it fails the 'get the record for the Gym' half of the requirement. Incorrect - B: this subquery uses Trainers__c instead of Trainers__r; SOQL child relationship subqueries must use the relationship name with the __r suffix, and Trainers__c is not a valid relationship name, so this query would fail to compile/run. Incorrect - D: this subquery uses Trainer__c (singular, the object's own API name) instead of the plural relationship name Trainers__r, which is also invalid syntax for a child relationship subquery.", "id": 162, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 1, "stem": "Universal Containers decided to transition from Classic to Lightning Experience. They asked a developer to replace a JavaScript button that was being used to create records with prepopulated values. What can the developer use to accomplish this?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Quick Actions"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Validation rules"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Record triggered flows"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Apex triggers"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Salesforce's own Lightning Experience migration guidance calls out that legacy JavaScript buttons are not supported in Lightning Experience and must be replaced with supported alternatives; a Create Record Quick Action lets an admin/developer define default field values (including formulas) that pre-populate the new record's fields when the user clicks the action, which is a direct, declarative, Lightning-Experience-native replacement for the old 'create with prepopulated values' JavaScript button pattern. Incorrect - B: a validation rule only runs at save time to block or allow a record based on a formula; it has no button/UI presence and cannot itself create a new record or prepopulate field values for a user to fill in, so it does not replace a create-record button. Incorrect - C: a record-triggered flow reacts automatically to a DML event (a record being created/updated/deleted) that has already happened - it is not something a user clicks to initiate creating a new record with prefilled values, so it solves a different problem than an on-demand creation button. Incorrect - D: an Apex trigger, like a record-triggered flow, only executes in response to DML that has already occurred on the server; it is not a user-facing UI element and cannot serve as the button replacement the requirement describes.", "id": 163, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 65, "stem": "Refer to the Visualforce page snippet: The Account Type is {!actType}. The value of the account type field is not being displayed correctly on the page. Assuming the custom controller is properly referenced on the Visualforce page, what should the developer do to correct the problem?", "code": "The Account Type is {!actType}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Change theAccount attribute to public."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add a getter method for the actType attribute."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add with sharing to the custom controller."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Convert theAccount.Type to a String."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per the Visualforce Developer Guide, a merge-field expression like {!actType} on the page resolves by calling a public getter method on the controller named getActType() (Salesforce's naming convention strips 'get' and lower-cases the first letter to derive the merge field name) - if no such getter exists on the custom controller, the expression simply renders nothing, which matches the symptom described, so adding the getter method is the fix. Incorrect - A: making an internal variable like theAccount public changes its visibility as an Apex member but does not, by itself, create the getter Visualforce needs to resolve {!actType}; visibility and the getter/property mechanism Visualforce relies on are different concerns. Incorrect - C: the with sharing keyword controls whether the class enforces the running user's record-level sharing rules; it has no effect on whether merge fields render or resolve values, so it would not fix a display problem. Incorrect - D: converting a field's underlying type to String might matter for how a value is formatted, but it does not address the root cause here - if actType has no getter at all, the page cannot display it regardless of what data type the underlying field is.", "id": 164, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 64, "stem": "Universal Containers has a Visualforce page that displays a table of every Container__c being rented by a given Account. Recently this page is failing with a view state limit because some of the customers rent over 10,000 containers. What should a developer change about the Visualforce page to help with the page load errors?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Implement pagination with a StandardSetController."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use lazy loading and a transient List variable."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use JavaScript remoting with SOQL Offset."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Implement pagination with an OffsetController."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Visualforce's built-in StandardSetController is documented to provide native pagination support (setPageSize, next(), previous(), etc.) so that only one page's worth of records - not the entire 10,000+ row result set - is held in the view state at any time; because Visualforce's view state limit (around 135KB, serialized) is exactly what's being exceeded here, reducing the number of records actually held in view state via built-in pagination directly targets the reported error. Incorrect - B: marking a variable transient excludes it from the view state, but that also means the data must be re-fetched on every postback since it isn't preserved between requests; 'lazy loading' is not a defined Visualforce mechanism on its own and this option does not actually solve how to display 10,000+ rows without still loading and rendering all of them at once. Incorrect - C: JavaScript remoting can fetch data asynchronously outside the normal view state life cycle, and SOQL OFFSET could theoretically page through results, but the SOQL OFFSET clause is documented to be capped at 2,000 rows - beyond that limit queries using OFFSET throw an error, so this approach does not reliably scale to 10,000+ containers. Incorrect - D: there is no 'OffsetController' class in the Visualforce or Apex standard library; this option is a fabricated name that does not correspond to any real Salesforce API.", "id": 165, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 63, "stem": "Which two actions may cause triggers to fire?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Renaming or replacing a picklist entry"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Updates to FeedItem"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Cascading delete operations"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Changing a user's default division when the transfer division option is checked"}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["B", "C"], "confidence": "verified-web", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - B: FeedItem (Chatter feed items) is a standard sObject that supports Apex triggers like most other objects, so any insert, update, or delete performed against FeedItem records through normal DML invokes FeedItem's triggers exactly as it would for any other object - there is nothing special exempting it. Correct - C: per Salesforce's documented trigger execution order for master-detail relationships, when a parent record in a master-detail relationship is deleted, the platform cascades the delete to its detail (child) records, and this cascading delete explicitly does invoke the delete triggers on those child records immediately after the parent's own delete triggers run - it is treated as a real DML delete on each child, not a silent bypass. Incorrect - A (the screenshot's marked answer): the official Apex Developer Guide page 'Operations That Don't Invoke Triggers' explicitly lists renaming or replacing a picklist value as one of the bulk administrative/metadata operations that does NOT invoke Apex triggers on the affected records, because it is treated as a schema-level change applied directly at the database layer rather than as record-level DML. Incorrect - D: that same official 'Operations That Don't Invoke Triggers' list also names mass user division transfers (changing a user's default division with the transfer option checked) as an operation that bypasses trigger invocation, since it is likewise a bulk administrative operation handled outside the normal DML/trigger pipeline.", "id": 166, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 51, "stem": "How should a developer write unit tests for a private method in an Apex class?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add a test method in the Apex class."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the SeeAllData annotation."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the @TestVisible annotation."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Mark the Apex class as global."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the Apex Developer Guide, annotating a private method or member variable with @TestVisible exposes it specifically to test context (any code running inside an @isTest class or method) while leaving it fully private to all other, non-test callers - this is the documented, purpose-built mechanism for unit-testing internal implementation details without weakening the class's real access control. Incorrect - A: simply adding a test method to the same Apex class does nothing on its own to grant that method access to a private member of a different class, and even within the same class a test method still cannot call a truly private method from outside the object's own instance context in the way the question implies without @TestVisible if the method is being tested indirectly; the annotation is what actually changes visibility. Incorrect - B: @isTest(SeeAllData=true) only controls whether a test can see pre-existing org data; it has no effect on Apex member access modifiers and does nothing to expose a private method to tests. Incorrect - D: marking the class global changes its accessibility to other namespaces/packages (relevant for managed packages), not the accessibility of a private method's members to test code - a global class can still have fully private methods that remain invisible to tests without @TestVisible.", "id": 167, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 50, "stem": "Assuming that name is a String obtained by an <apex:inputText> tag on a Visualforce page, which two SOQL queries performed are safe from SOQL injection?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "String query = 'SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Name LIKE \\'%' + String.escapeSingleQuotes(name) + '%\\''; List<Account> results = Database.query(query);"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "String query = '%' + name + '%'; List<Account> results = [SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Name LIKE :query];"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "String query = 'SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Name LIKE \\'%' + name.noQuotes() + '%\\''; List<Account> results = Database.query(query);"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "String query = 'SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Name LIKE \\'%' + name + '%\\''; List<Account> results = Database.query(query);"}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Apex Developer Guide's secure coding documentation specifically recommends String.escapeSingleQuotes() as a sanitizer for any user-supplied string that must be concatenated into a dynamic SOQL string, because it escapes single-quote characters (the character an attacker would use to break out of the LIKE literal) before the string is ever built, closing off the injection vector. Correct - B: this option avoids building dynamic SOQL text altogether - it uses inline (static) SOQL with a bind variable (:query); per Salesforce's secure coding guide, bind variables are inherently safe because the query engine treats the bound value strictly as a literal data value, never as part of the query's structure, so no user input can alter the query's logic no matter what characters it contains. Incorrect - C: name.noQuotes() is not a real Apex String method - there is no built-in sanitizer with that name - so this code would not even compile, and even conceptually it does nothing to prevent injection since the raw name value is still concatenated straight into the dynamic query text. Incorrect - D: this concatenates the raw, unsanitized name value directly into a dynamic SOQL string with no escaping and no bind variable, which is the textbook SOQL injection vulnerability the Apex secure coding guidelines warn against - a value like a single quote followed by additional SOQL clauses could alter the query's meaning.", "id": 168, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 49, "stem": "Developers at Universal Containers (UC) use version control to share their code changes, but they notice that when they deploy their code to different environments they often have failures. They decide to set up Continuous Integration (CI). What should the UC development team use to automatically run tests as part of their CI process?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Salesforce CLI"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Developer Console"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Visual Studio Code"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Force.com Toolkit"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Salesforce CLI (sf/sfdx) is explicitly documented as the command-line tool designed to be scripted into automated pipelines - commands like deploying source and running Apex tests (sf apex run test / force:apex:test:run) can be invoked non-interactively from any CI system (Jenkins, GitHub Actions, CircleCI, etc.), which is exactly what a CI pipeline needs: a scriptable, headless way to deploy and test on every change. Incorrect - B: the Developer Console is a browser-based, point-and-click interactive tool meant for a human developer to manually run tests, inspect logs, and write anonymous Apex; it has no command-line or API surface that a CI server could invoke automatically. Incorrect - C: VS Code with the Salesforce Extension Pack is also an interactive desktop IDE for a human developer; while it uses the Salesforce CLI under the hood for many of its commands, VS Code itself is not what a CI server runs - the CI server would call the underlying CLI directly, not launch a code editor. Incorrect - D: the Force.com Toolkit refers to older client libraries/SDKs for building integrations in specific languages (e.g., a PHP or Node toolkit for calling Salesforce APIs); it is not a deployment or test-automation tool and has no built-in CI scripting role.", "id": 169, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 48, "stem": "A business has a proprietary Order Management System (OMS) that creates orders from its website and fulfills the orders. When the order is created in the OMS, an integration also creates an order record in Salesforce and relates it to the contact as identified by the email on the order. As the order goes through different stages in the OMS, the integration also updates it in Salesforce. The business notices that each update from the OMS creates a new order record in Salesforce. Which two actions should prevent the duplicate order records from being created in Salesforce?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ensure that the order number in the OMS is unique."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the email on the contact record as an external ID."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Write a trigger on the Order object to delete the duplicates."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the order number from the OMS as an external ID."}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per Salesforce Help on External ID fields, marking the OMS order number field as an External ID lets the integration call upsert (rather than plain insert) keyed on that field - Salesforce will look for an existing Order record whose External ID matches the incoming order number and update it in place instead of creating a new record every time the OMS sends a status update, which directly targets the root cause of the duplicates. Correct - A: an upsert's matching logic depends entirely on the External ID value being unique per record; if the OMS could ever emit the same order number for two different orders, the upsert could either throw a 'duplicate external id' error or match the wrong record, so guaranteeing uniqueness in the source system is a necessary precondition for D to work reliably. Incorrect - B: the contact's email identifies which person the order belongs to, not which specific order a given update refers to - a single contact can have many orders, so matching only on contact email would incorrectly collapse or misroute distinct orders rather than correctly updating the one specific order the OMS is referring to. Incorrect - C: a trigger that deletes duplicates after they've already been created is purely reactive clean-up; it does nothing to stop the integration from creating a new record on every update in the first place, and could easily delete legitimate data if not scoped with extreme care, whereas fixing the upsert key stops duplicates from occurring at all.", "id": 170, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 47, "stem": "Universal Containers (UC) processes orders in Salesforce in a custom object, Order__c. They also allow sales reps to upload CSV files with thousands of orders at a time. A developer is tasked with integrating orders placed in Salesforce with UC's enterprise resource planning (ERP) system. After the status for an Order__c is first set to 'Placed', the order information must be sent to a REST endpoint in the ERP system that can process one order at a time. What should the developer implement to accomplish this?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Flow with a callout from an invocable method"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Callout from a Queueable class called from a trigger"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Callout from an @future method called from a trigger"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Callout from a Batchable class called from a scheduled job"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "verified-web", "explanation": "Correct - C: The trigger event here is a field change (status first becomes 'Placed'), which by definition must be detected from a trigger; per the Apex Developer Guide, Apex triggers cannot make synchronous HTTP callouts at all, so any callout initiated from a trigger must be deferred to an asynchronous context, and @future(callout=true) is the simplest, most standard documented pattern for firing a single asynchronous callout per invocation directly from trigger context - which matches the ERP endpoint's 'process one order at a time' constraint well, since each future call handles one order. Incorrect - D: a scheduled Batchable job runs on a time-based schedule (e.g., nightly) rather than being triggered by the record's status actually changing to 'Placed,' so it is not event-driven and would introduce unwanted delay or process orders that haven't just changed status, failing the 'after the status is first set to Placed' requirement. Incorrect - B: a Queueable class could technically perform the callout, but @future is the simpler, more conventional choice specifically for a one-off async callout invoked directly from a trigger, and Queueable's chaining/state-carrying capabilities aren't needed for this single, isolated per-record callout; while not technically wrong, it isn't the standard answer for this exact well-known trigger-plus-single-record-callout pattern that Salesforce exam content associates with @future. Incorrect - A: an invocable method callout from a Flow works for user- or process-initiated automation, but this business requirement is phrased as a direct field-change trigger scenario, which the exam consistently associates with the trigger + @future(callout=true) pattern rather than a Flow-based approach.", "id": 171, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 46, "stem": "Which three statements are accurate about debug logs?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The maximum size of a debug log is 5 MB."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Debug log levels are cumulative, where FINE log level includes all events logged at the DEBUG, INFO, WARN, and ERROR levels."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Only the 20 most recent debug logs for a user are kept."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "System debug logs are retained for 24 hours."}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Debug logs can be set for specific users, classes, and triggers."}], "marked": ["C", "D", "E"], "correct": ["B", "D", "E"], "confidence": "verified-web", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - B: Salesforce's documented Apex log levels form a cumulative hierarchy (NONE < ERROR < WARN < INFO < DEBUG < FINE < FINER < FINEST), meaning setting a category to FINE captures everything at ERROR, WARN, INFO, and DEBUG as well as FINE-level detail - the levels are not independent buckets but nested thresholds. Correct - D: Salesforce Help states that system (automatically generated) debug logs are retained for 24 hours before being purged, distinct from logs a user manually captures via a trace flag which are retained longer (typically 7 days or until the size/count limit forces rotation). Correct - E: Trace flags, which govern debug log generation, can be scoped to a specific user and, for the Developer Console/log inspection tooling, log levels can be configured per Apex class and per Apex trigger, letting a developer capture fine-grained detail only for the code paths they care about. Incorrect - A (the plausible-looking wrong option): the documented maximum size for a single debug log is 20 MB, not 5 MB; once a log would exceed that size, older log lines are removed from the beginning to make room for new ones, but the ceiling itself is 20 MB. Incorrect - C (the screenshot's marked answer): there is no rule limiting a user to their 20 most recent debug logs - retention is governed by the 24-hour/size-based rules described above, not a fixed count of log entries, so this statement does not reflect actual, documented Salesforce log-retention behavior.", "id": 172, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 45, "stem": "A developer created a new after insert trigger on the Lead object that creates Task records for each Lead. After deploying to production, an existing outside integration that inserts Lead records in batches to Salesforce is occasionally reporting total batch failures being caused by the Task insert statement. This causes the integration process in the outside system to stop, requiring a manual restart. Which change should the developer make to allow the integration to continue when some records in a batch cause failures due to the Task insert statement, so that manual restarts are not needed?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Deactivate the trigger before the integration runs."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use a try-catch block after the insert statement."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Remove the Apex class from the integration user's profile."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Database method with allOrNone set to false."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Apex Developer Guide's Database class documentation, calling Database.insert(taskList, false) with allOrNone explicitly set to false switches the DML operation into partial-success mode: each record in the list succeeds or fails independently, a Database.SaveResult array is returned describing which rows failed and why, and - critically - the transaction is not automatically rolled back just because some Task records failed, so the successful Tasks are committed and the trigger (and therefore the integration's batch) does not abort entirely on one bad row. Incorrect - B: wrapping a plain, all-or-nothing insert statement in a try-catch only lets the trigger catch and log the resulting DmlException after the fact - by the time the exception is thrown, the entire DML statement (and everything else in that trigger invocation) has already been rolled back, so the successful Task inserts are lost too and the underlying batch-partial-failure problem is not solved. Incorrect - A: deactivating the trigger removes the very functionality (creating Task records for each Lead) that the business needs; it 'fixes' the failure by eliminating the feature rather than making it resilient to partial failures, which does not meet the actual requirement. Incorrect - C: removing the Apex class from the integration user's profile would only prevent that user from being able to invoke Apex functionality they have explicit permission dependencies on (relevant to classes with 'API Enabled'/class-level security some contexts require); it has no bearing on how the trigger's own internal DML behaves on partial failures and does not address the reported problem at all.", "id": 173, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 44, "stem": "A Next Best Action strategy uses an Enhance element that invokes an Apex method to determine a discount level for a Contact, based on a number of factors. What is the correct definition of the Apex method?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "@InvocableMethod global List<List<Recommendation>> getLevel(List<ContactWrapper> input) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "@InvocableMethod global Recommendation getLevel(ContactWrapper input) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "@InvocableMethod global static ListRecommendation getLevel(List<ContactWrapper> input) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "@InvocableMethod global static List<List<Recommendation>> getLevel(List<ContactWrapper> input) { /*implementation*/ }"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Apex Developer Guide's @InvocableMethod documentation and the Next Best Action (Strategy Builder) requirements specifically, a method used by an Enhance element must be declared static (invocable methods are always static, called without an instance), must accept exactly one parameter that is a List (here List<ContactWrapper>) so the platform can bulkify calls across multiple records in one invocation, and for a Next Best Action strategy specifically must return List<List<Recommendation>> - an outer list matching the input list's size/order and an inner list of Recommendation objects generated for each corresponding input item. Only option D satisfies all three requirements simultaneously (static, correct List input, correct List<List<Recommendation>> output). Incorrect - A: this omits the static keyword; per Apex's invocable-method rules an instance (non-static) method cannot be registered as an @InvocableMethod target at all, so this signature is invalid regardless of its otherwise-correct return type. Incorrect - B: this both omits static and uses a single ContactWrapper parameter plus a single Recommendation return instead of Lists, breaking bulkification entirely - invocable methods must always operate on collections, never a single input/output pair, per the documented bulk-safety requirement. Incorrect - C: 'ListRecommendation' is not valid Apex syntax at all (it is missing the generic angle-bracket type parameter, e.g. List<Recommendation>), so this method signature would fail to compile.", "id": 174, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 43, "stem": "Which two characteristics are true for Lightning Web Component custom events?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Data may be passed in the payload of a custom event using a property called detail."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "By default a custom event only propagates to it's immediate container."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Data may be passed in the payload of a custom event using @wire decorated properties."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "By default a custom event only propagates to its immediate container and to its immediate child component."}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Lightning Web Components Developer Guide documents that when you construct a custom event with new CustomEvent('eventname', { detail: {...} }), any data you want to pass along with the event is placed in the detail property of the event-init options object; listeners receive that payload via event.detail. Correct - B: by default a CustomEvent has bubbles: false and composed: false, meaning per the standard DOM event model that LWC events follow, the event dispatches and is only visible to listeners registered directly on the component that fired it (or, more precisely, its own host element in the parent's template) - it does not automatically bubble up through the DOM tree or cross shadow boundaries unless explicitly configured with bubbles: true / composed: true. Incorrect - C: @wire is an entirely separate mechanism for declaratively provisioning data (from Apex methods or Lightning Data Service) into a component's properties/functions; it has nothing to do with the event system or an event's payload, so it cannot be used to pass custom event data. Incorrect - D: this misstates the default propagation - a default (non-bubbling) custom event is scoped to its own container/parent listener registration point, not automatically forwarded to 'immediate child components' as well; events flow up the containment hierarchy toward ancestors when bubbling is enabled, never automatically down to children, so describing default behavior as reaching child components is incorrect.", "id": 175, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 42, "stem": "A developer created this Apex trigger that calls MyClass.myStaticMethod. The developer creates a test class with a test method that calls MyClass.myStaticMethod directly, resulting in 81% overall code coverage. What happens when the developer tries to deploy the trigger and two classes to production, assuming no other code exists?", "code": "trigger myTrigger on Contact (before insert) {\n    MyClass.myStaticMethod(trigger.new);\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The deployment fails because no assertions were made in the test method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The deployment passes because the Apex code has the required >75% code coverage."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The deployment fails because the Apex trigger has no code coverage."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The deployment passes because both classes and the trigger were included in the deployment."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Salesforce's deployment requirements, documented in the Apex Developer Guide, impose two separate coverage rules simultaneously: at least 75% coverage across all Apex code org-wide, AND every individual trigger must have at least 1% (i.e., some) coverage of its own. Because the test method calls MyClass.myStaticMethod(trigger.new) directly rather than performing an actual insert DML statement on Contact, the trigger body itself is never executed by any test, giving the trigger exactly 0% coverage - this independently fails the per-trigger requirement even though the 75% org-wide number (81%) is satisfied. Incorrect - B: this option only checks the 75% aggregate number and ignores the separate, mandatory 0%-trigger-coverage rule; both conditions must hold for a deployment with Apex changes to succeed, so meeting only one of them is not sufficient. Incorrect - A: Salesforce's deployment validation does not require test methods to contain System.assert statements to pass - a test with no assertions can still 'pass' and contribute to code coverage percentages (though it is extremely poor practice to write one), so lack of assertions is not what blocks this specific deployment. Incorrect - D: simply including the trigger and both classes in the deployment package does not by itself satisfy coverage requirements; the validation step still separately checks that the trigger achieved some execution coverage, which it did not here.", "id": 176, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 41, "stem": "When a user edits the Postal Code on an Account, a custom Account text field named \"Timezone\" must be updated based on the values in a PostalCodeToTimezone__c custom object. Which two automation tools can be used to implement this feature?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Quick actions"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Fast Field Updates record-triggered flow"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Approval process"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Account trigger"}], "marked": ["B", "D"], "correct": ["B", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: An Apex trigger on Account (before update, checking if Postal Code changed) can perform a SOQL query against PostalCodeToTimezone__c to look up the matching timezone and set the field directly on the record before it saves - this is a standard cross-object lookup-and-set pattern well within Apex's capabilities. Correct - B: a record-triggered flow configured to run before-save with 'Fast Field Updates' can, within the same transaction and without an extra DML/save round-trip, run a Get Records element against PostalCodeToTimezone__c filtered by the new Postal Code and then set the Timezone field on the triggering Account record directly - this declarative pattern is specifically documented by Salesforce as the recommended fast, same-record field-update approach for exactly this kind of scenario. Incorrect - A: a quick action is a UI element a user manually clicks to create/update/log a record or launch a flow; it is not something that runs automatically whenever the Postal Code field is edited, so it cannot implement an automatic, trigger-like update. Incorrect - C: an approval process is designed to route a record through a sequence of approval steps and can perform limited field updates as part of that approval workflow, but it is not an appropriate or typical mechanism for a simple 'field changes, look up and set another field' automation - it requires submitting the record for approval, which is a mismatched and heavyweight solution to this requirement.", "id": 177, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 40, "stem": "As part of new feature development, a developer is asked to build a responsive application capable of responding to touch events, that will be executed on stateful clients. Which two technologies are built on a framework that fully supports the business requirement?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Aura Components"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Visualforce Pages"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Visualforce Components"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lightning Web Components"}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A and D: both Aura Components and Lightning Web Components are built on the Lightning Component framework, which the Lightning Components Developer Guide describes as a modern, client-side, stateful UI framework - components maintain their own state in the browser, re-render efficiently on data changes, and are responsive/touch-friendly by design because they render as standard HTML/CSS/JS that responds to native browser touch and pointer events, unlike older server round-trip-based technologies. Incorrect - B and C: Visualforce Pages and Visualforce Components are both built on a server-centric, stateless request/response model - each interaction typically triggers a full or partial page postback to the server, which is fundamentally not a 'stateful client' architecture, and while Visualforce pages can be made mobile-responsive with extra CSS/JS work, the underlying framework itself was not designed around touch-first, client-held state the way the Lightning Component framework was, so it does not 'fully support' this requirement the way Aura/LWC do.", "id": 178, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 62, "stem": "A developer is creating an app that contains multiple Lightning web components. One of the child components is used for navigation purposes. When a user clicks a button called Next in the child component, the parent component must be alerted so it can navigate to the next page. How should this be accomplished?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Update a property on the parent."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Call a method in the Apex controller."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Fire a notification."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Create a custom event."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Lightning Web Components Developer Guide documents that child-to-parent communication must go through the standard DOM event model - the child constructs a CustomEvent (optionally carrying data in its detail property) and calls this.dispatchEvent(event), while the parent registers a listener on the child's tag in its template (e.g. onnext={handleNext}) to react when that event fires; this is the only supported, documented direction-safe way for a child to notify an ancestor of something that happened inside it. Incorrect - A: a child component has no direct reference to its parent's internal properties and cannot reach up and mutate them - LWC's one-way data-down, events-up data flow model deliberately disallows a child writing to parent state directly, precisely because it would break encapsulation and make components untestable in isolation. Incorrect - B: calling an Apex controller method would reach the Salesforce server, not the parent component in the browser's DOM; it has no mechanism to notify a sibling in-memory JavaScript object like the parent component about a client-side button click. Incorrect - C: 'fire a notification' is not a real, named LWC API - there is no generic Notification object in the component communication model; the actual mechanism, again, is a CustomEvent.", "id": 179, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 61, "stem": "A developer needs to implement a custom SOAP Web Service that is used by an external Web Application. The developer chooses to include helper methods that are not used by the Web Application in the implementation of the Web Service Class. Which code segment shows the correct declaration of the class and methods?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "global class WebServiceClass { private static Boolean helperMethod() {...} webservice static String updateRecords() {...} }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "global class WebServiceClass { private static Boolean helperMethod() {...} global String updateRecords() {...} }"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "webservice class WebServiceClass { private static Boolean helperMethod() {...} global static String updateRecords() {...} }"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "webservice class WebServiceClass { private static Boolean helperMethod() {...} webservice static String updateRecords() {...} }"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Apex Developer Guide's documentation on custom SOAP web services, the enclosing class must be declared global (so the platform can generate a WSDL that exposes it externally), and any method that should actually appear as a SOAP operation in that WSDL must use the dedicated webservice keyword (webservice methods are implicitly static in the sense that they generate a static-style external operation, and Salesforce's own examples declare them 'webservice static'); methods that should remain internal helpers - not part of the exposed SOAP contract - can simply stay private, exactly as helperMethod() does here. Incorrect - C and D: 'webservice class' is not valid Apex syntax at all - webservice is a method-level keyword, never a class-level declaration keyword; a class is declared global/public/private, never 'webservice', so both options fail to compile. Incorrect - B: declaring updateRecords as global (rather than using the webservice keyword) makes it callable from other Apex in the same namespace/managed package context, but it does NOT expose the method as a SOAP web service operation in the generated WSDL - global and webservice serve different exposure purposes, and only webservice methods appear in the WSDL that an external SOAP client would consume.", "id": 180, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 60, "stem": "A developer wants to improve runtime performance of Apex calls by caching results on the client. What is the most efficient way to implement this and follow best practices?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Call the setStorable() method on the action in the JavaScript client-side code."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Decorate the server-side method with @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Set a cookie in the browser for use upon return to the page."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Decorate the server-side method with @AuraEnabled(storable=true)."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "verified-web", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - B: The current Apex Developer Guide documents @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) as the modern, declarative way to let Lightning Data Service's client-side storage cache an Apex method's results, avoiding unnecessary server round-trips on subsequent calls; it is framework-managed (works uniformly in both Aura and LWC) and is a hard requirement for using the method with the @wire decorator, which Salesforce's own guidance treats as best practice. Incorrect - A (the screenshot's marked answer): action.setStorable() is the older, Aura-specific, imperative API for marking a server action as cacheable; it still functions but is documented as the legacy technique superseded by the declarative cacheable=true annotation, which is why it is not considered the 'most efficient... best practices' choice the question is asking for. Incorrect - D: storable is not a valid parameter name recognized by the @AuraEnabled annotation - the only supported boolean flag for this purpose is cacheable, so this option is invalid syntax masquerading as a plausible variant. Incorrect - C: manually storing data in a browser cookie sidesteps the platform's built-in caching layer entirely, requiring the developer to hand-roll serialization, size limits, expiry, and invalidation - none of which is efficient or considered a Salesforce best practice compared to the framework-provided caching mechanism.", "id": 181, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 59, "stem": "What does the Lightning Component framework provide to developers?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Extended governor limits for applications"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Support for Classic and Lightning UIs"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Templates to create custom components"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Prebuilt components that can be reused"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Lightning Components Developer Guide documents a library of base Lightning components (lightning-button, lightning-input, lightning-card, and dozens more, plus their Aura equivalents like lightning:button) that developers can drop directly into their own components rather than building common UI patterns from scratch, and the framework's own component-bundle architecture is built around composing and reusing components across an app - this reuse is a headline benefit called out in Salesforce's documentation. Incorrect - A: the Lightning Component framework has no special relationship to Apex governor limits; server-side Apex methods called from a Lightning component are still bound by the exact same limits (SOQL rows, DML statements, CPU time, etc.) as any other Apex invocation - the framework does not extend or relax these. Incorrect - B: the Lightning Component framework is specifically the modern UI framework for Lightning Experience; it does not provide any special support for rendering inside Salesforce Classic, and in fact Aura/LWC components generally cannot be placed directly onto Classic page layouts without extra bridging work (e.g., Lightning Out). Incorrect - C: while component bundles have a defined file structure, the framework does not ship 'templates' as a distinct developer-facing product feature in the way base/reusable components are - the emphasis in Salesforce's own materials is on the prebuilt component library, not scaffolding templates.", "id": 182, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 58, "stem": "A developer must troubleshoot to pinpoint the causes of performance issues when a custom page loads in their org. Which tool should the developer use to troubleshoot query performance?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Developer Console"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Setup Menu"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "AppExchange"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Visual Studio Code IDE"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: the Developer Console is documented to include a Query Plan tool that shows the estimated cost, cardinality, and index usage of a SOQL query before it runs, plus an execution log viewer/Analysis tab that breaks down exactly how much time each SOQL query, DML statement, and method call consumed during a real page load - this combination is Salesforce's purpose-built diagnostic toolset for query and page-load performance work. Incorrect - D: VS Code with the Salesforce Extension Pack is the recommended tool for writing and deploying Apex/LWC code and can retrieve and view debug logs, but it does not include an equivalent built-in Query Plan analyzer, so it is not the primary documented tool for this specific diagnostic task. Incorrect - B: the Setup Menu is where an admin configures org features, permissions, and metadata; it contains no query-execution analysis capability. Incorrect - C: AppExchange is Salesforce's marketplace for browsing and installing third-party or Salesforce-built applications and packages; it has no diagnostic or performance-analysis functionality at all.", "id": 183, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 57, "stem": "Which scenario is valid for execution by unit tests?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Generate a Visualforce PDF with getContentAsPDF()."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Execute anonymous Apex as a different user."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Set the created date of a record using a system method."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Load data from a remote site with a callout."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: the Apex Developer Guide documents Test.setCreatedDate(recordId, dateTime) as a test-only system method specifically provided so unit tests can simulate an older CreatedDate on a record - useful for testing date-based or aging logic - without needing real time to elapse; this is an explicitly supported testing scenario. Incorrect - A: rendering a Visualforce page as a PDF via getContentAsPDF() is explicitly documented as unsupported in a test context - calling it inside an @isTest method throws a runtime exception, because the underlying PDF rendering service is unavailable during test execution. Incorrect - D: Apex tests cannot make live HTTP callouts at all; attempting one during test execution throws System.CalloutException - the documented, supported pattern for testing callout code is to implement HttpCalloutMock and register it with Test.setMock() before invoking the code under test, not to actually 'load data from a remote site.' Incorrect - B: 'execute anonymous Apex as a different user' conflates two different tools - Execute Anonymous is a Developer Console feature for running one-off scripts outside of a test class, not a unit-testing construct; the correct, documented way to run test logic as a different user inside a test method is System.runAs(user) { ... }, which is a different mechanism than what this option describes.", "id": 184, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 56, "stem": "A developer wants to import 500 Opportunity records into a sandbox. Why should the developer choose to use Data Loader instead of Data Import Wizard?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Data Import Wizard can not import all 500 records."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Data Loader runs from the developer's browser."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Data Import Wizard does not support Opportunities."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Data Loader automatically relates Opportunities to Accounts."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Salesforce Help's documentation of the Data Import Wizard explicitly limits its supported objects to Accounts, Contacts, Leads, Solutions, Campaign Members, and custom objects - Opportunity is not among them, so there is simply no UI path in the Wizard to import Opportunity records at all, regardless of volume, forcing the use of Data Loader (which supports any standard or custom object via the Bulk or SOAP API). Incorrect - A: the Wizard's actual documented ceiling is 50,000 records per import job; 500 records is trivially within that limit, so record count is not the blocking factor here - object support is. Incorrect - B: this reverses the tools - Data Loader is the installed desktop client application (Windows/Mac), while the Data Import Wizard is the one that runs entirely in the browser from Setup; describing Data Loader as browser-based is factually backwards. Incorrect - D: neither tool automatically infers or creates relationships without explicit field mapping - both require the developer to map a lookup field (e.g., matching Account Name or an External ID) during setup; there is no special 'auto-relate' capability unique to Data Loader.", "id": 185, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 55, "stem": "What can be used to override the Account's standard Edit button for Lightning Experience?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lightning component"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Lightning flow"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lightning page"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lightning action"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Salesforce Help's documentation on button overrides states that in Lightning Experience, a standard button such as Edit can be overridden with either a Visualforce page or a Lightning (Aura) component - the override is configured in Setup under the object's Buttons, Links, and Actions section, and choosing a Lightning component as the override target is one of the two documented supported options. Incorrect - B: a 'Lightning flow' (a Flow) is not one of the two documented override types for a standard button; flows are launched from actions, quick actions, or automation - they are not directly assignable as a standard Edit button override target. Incorrect - C: a Lightning (record) page is a layout/container that can be assigned as an app, org, or record-type-specific page, but it is not one of the recognized override targets for the standard Edit button in Setup. Incorrect - D: 'Lightning action' is not itself a button-override type in Setup's Buttons/Links/Actions configuration; actions are things you add to page layouts, not something you designate as an override for a standard button.", "id": 186, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 54, "stem": "Universal Containers has an order system that uses an Order Number to identify an order for customers and service agents. Order records will be imported into Salesforce. How should the Order Number field be defined in Salesforce?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "External ID and Unique"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Direct Lookup"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lookup"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Indirect Lookup"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: per Salesforce Help's field-definition documentation, marking a custom field as both External ID and Unique lets integration and import tools use it as the match key for an upsert operation - incoming records are matched against existing Salesforce records by that field's value, so re-importing or updating orders by their source-system Order Number correctly finds and updates the right record rather than creating duplicates; 'Unique' additionally prevents two different Order records from accidentally sharing the same Order Number. Incorrect - B and C: 'Lookup' (and 'Direct Lookup') field types are used to create relationships between two objects (e.g., an Order looking up to an Account); Order Number is simply an identifying value on the Order record itself, not a reference to another object, so a relationship field type is conceptually the wrong tool. Incorrect - D: an 'Indirect Lookup' field type is specifically used to relate a child object to a Salesforce-to-Salesforce or external-object parent by matching on an External ID field of that parent object - it is not a general-purpose way to store or match an identifying number on the Order object itself.", "id": 187, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 53, "stem": "In the following example, which sharing context will myMethod execute when it is invoked?", "code": "public class myClass {\n    public void myMethod() { /* implementation */ }\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Sharing rules will be enforced for the running user."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Sharing rules will be inherited from the calling context."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Sharing rules will be enforced by the instantiating class."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Sharing rules will not be enforced for the running user."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: the Apex Developer Guide's security documentation states that a class declared without either the with sharing or without sharing keyword does not define its own sharing behavior - instead, it inherits the sharing mode of whatever class called it (if the calling class runs with sharing, this class enforces sharing too; if the caller runs without sharing, so does this class). If it turns out to be the very first class invoked in the call stack (nothing called it), the default in that specific case is without sharing, but the general rule being tested here - and the one that correctly describes the class's behavior as a callable unit - is that it inherits from its caller. Incorrect - A: sharing enforcement is not automatic for an unspecified class; only a class explicitly marked with sharing enforces the running user's record-level sharing rules unconditionally - this class makes no such declaration. Incorrect - D: it is not correct to say sharing is unconditionally NOT enforced either - if this class happens to be called by a with sharing class, it will inherit and enforce sharing, so a blanket 'not enforced' statement is also wrong in the general case. Incorrect - C: sharing behavior is a property of the executing class's own declaration (or lack thereof) and the call chain, not something 'enforced by the instantiating class' as a separate concept - there is no such mechanism in Apex's sharing model.", "id": 188, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 52, "stem": "Universal Containers recently transitioned from Classic to Lightning Experience. One of its business processes requires certain values from the Opportunity object (Name, Amount, Account) to be sent via an HTTP REST callout to its external order management system when the user presses a custom button on the Opportunity detail page. Which two methods should the developer implement to fulfill the business requirement?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Create a custom Visualforce quick action that performs the HTTP REST callout, and use a Visualforce quick action to expose the component on the Opportunity detail page."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Create an after update trigger on the Opportunity object that calls a helper method using @future(callout=true) to perform the HTTP REST callout."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a Lightning component quick action that performs the HTTP REST callout, and use a Lightning Action to expose the component on the Opportunity detail page."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Create a Remote Action on the Opportunity object that executes an Apex immediate action to perform the HTTP REST callout whenever the Opportunity is updated."}], "marked": ["A", "C"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A and C: the requirement is explicitly button-press-driven ('when the user presses a custom button'), and Salesforce's documented mechanism for a button that runs custom logic on a record detail page is a quick action - either a Visualforce-based quick action (A) whose controller performs the callout, or a Lightning component-based quick action exposed via a Lightning Action (C) whose JS/Apex controller performs the callout; both are legitimate, supported ways to wire a detail-page button to custom callout logic in Lightning Experience, which is why the question asks for 'two methods' rather than one. Incorrect - B: an after update trigger with @future(callout=true) fires automatically whenever the Opportunity is updated for any reason - it is not tied to the user pressing a specific button, so it does not satisfy the 'when the user presses a custom button' trigger condition; it would fire on any update, button-press or otherwise. Incorrect - D: 'Remote Action' is not a real, current Salesforce automation or callout mechanism (this is a fabricated/outdated-sounding term, distinct from real features like JavaScript remoting or invocable actions), and like option B it is also framed as reacting to 'whenever the Opportunity is updated' rather than to a button press specifically, so it fails the requirement on both counts.", "id": 189, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 39, "stem": "Universal Containers needs to create a custom user interface component that allows users to enter information about their accounts. The component should be able to validate the user input before saving the information to the database. What is the best technology to create this component?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lightning Web Components"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Flow"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "VUE JavaScript framework"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Visualforce"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Lightning Web Components Developer Guide positions LWC as Salesforce's current standard framework for building custom, client-side UI - it runs in the browser, can perform immediate field-level or form-level validation before any server call is made, and integrates natively with Lightning Data Service and Apex for the eventual save. It is also the technology Salesforce recommends for all new custom UI development going forward. Incorrect - D: Visualforce is the older, server-round-trip-based UI technology; while it can validate input, Salesforce's own guidance has shifted new development toward LWC, making Visualforce not the 'best' choice when a modern alternative exists. Incorrect - C: VUE is a third-party JavaScript framework with no native Salesforce support, packaging, or security-review integration - using it means fighting the platform's supported tooling (Lightning Locker/LWS, static resource packaging, deployment) rather than working with it, so it is not the recommended approach for building a supported custom component. Incorrect - B: A Flow (specifically a screen flow) can collect input and includes some built-in validation on screen components, but it is a declarative, wizard-style tool rather than a 'custom user interface component' in the sense the question is asking about - for genuinely custom UI with fine-grained programmatic validation logic, a code-based component (LWC) is the more fitting and standard technology.", "id": 190, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 38, "stem": "While working in a sandbox, an Apex test fails when run in the Test Runner. However, executing the Apex logic in the Execute Anonymous window succeeds without exceptions or errors. Why did the method fail in the sandbox test framework but succeed in the Developer Console?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The test method is calling an @future method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The test method does not use System.runAs to execute as a specific user."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The test method relies on existing data in the sandbox."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The test method has a syntax error in the code."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the Apex Developer Guide's testing documentation, test methods run in an isolated test context and by default cannot see any data that already exists in the org (unless the class is annotated @isTest(SeeAllData=true), which is discouraged) - only records the test itself explicitly inserts are visible. Execute Anonymous, by contrast, runs directly against the org's real, live data. If the logic being tested queries for or depends on records that happen to already exist in the sandbox, it will find that data (and succeed) in Execute Anonymous, but find nothing (and fail, e.g. with a null reference or an empty query result) inside the isolated test context - exactly matching the symptom described. Incorrect - A: whether the code calls an @future method affects how/when that particular piece of logic executes (asynchronously, after Test.stopTest() flushes the queue) but does not by itself explain a pass/fail discrepancy between Execute Anonymous and the Test Runner - both contexts can invoke future methods (Execute Anonymous runs them asynchronously too), so this isn't the differentiating factor described. Incorrect - B: System.runAs is used inside tests specifically to test behavior under a different user's permissions/sharing context; its absence would not cause a test to fail outright unless the code explicitly required a different running user for correctness, which isn't indicated here, and Execute Anonymous already runs as whichever user is logged into the Developer Console, so this doesn't explain the discrepancy either. Incorrect - D: a genuine syntax error would fail to compile in both Execute Anonymous and the test framework identically (Apex compiles once regardless of where the code runs), so a syntax error cannot explain why one context succeeds while the other fails.", "id": 191, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 37, "stem": "What can be easily developed using the Lightning Component framework?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Customized JavaScript buttons"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Salesforce integrations"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lightning Pages"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Salesforce Classic user interface pages"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "verified-web", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - C: The Lightning Components Developer Guide describes the Lightning Component framework as the UI framework used to build the components that are then assembled, via Lightning App Builder, into Lightning Pages (record pages, app pages, home pages) and Lightning apps - this composition of components into pages is the framework's core, documented purpose and the thing it makes easy to build. Incorrect - B (the screenshot's marked answer): 'Salesforce integrations' (connecting to and exchanging data with external systems) is the domain of Apex HTTP callouts, REST/SOAP APIs, Connected Apps, and platform events - the Lightning Component framework is a client-side UI rendering framework and has no special built-in capability for building integrations; a component might call Apex that happens to perform an integration, but that is not what the framework itself 'provides' or makes easy. Incorrect - A: customized JavaScript buttons are an older Salesforce Classic feature (now deprecated/unsupported in Lightning Experience) built with raw JavaScript attached to a button, entirely unrelated to and not built with the Lightning Component framework. Incorrect - D: the Lightning Component framework is specifically for Lightning Experience; Aura/LWC components are not natively placed onto Salesforce Classic page layouts (doing so requires the separate Lightning Out bridging technique), so building Classic UI pages is not something this framework 'easily' does.", "id": 192, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 36, "stem": "A developer needs to make a custom Lightning Web Component available in the Salesforce Classic user interface. Which approach can be used to accomplish this?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the Lightning Out JavaScript library to embed the Lightning Web Component in a Visualforce page and add to the page layout."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Wrap the Lightning Web Component in an Aura Component and surface the Aura Component as a Visualforce tab."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Embed the Lightning Web Component in a Visualforce Component and add directly to the page layout."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use a Visualforce page with a custom controller to invoke the Lightning Web Component using a call to an Apex method."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Lightning Components Developer Guide documents Lightning Out as the officially supported bridging technology for exactly this scenario - it lets a Visualforce page load the Lightning Out JavaScript library, bootstrap a Lightning dependency app, and instantiate a Lightning web component (or Aura component) inside that Visualforce page; because Visualforce pages themselves can be added to Classic page layouts, this chain (LWC inside Lightning Out inside Visualforce inside a Classic layout) is the documented supported path to surface an LWC in Classic. Incorrect - B: wrapping an LWC in an Aura component does not change the fundamental limitation that neither Aura nor LWC components can be dropped directly onto a Visualforce tab or a Classic page layout without going through Lightning Out - simply wrapping the component doesn't solve the Classic-compatibility gap; Lightning Out (or an equivalent bridge) is still required underneath. Incorrect - C: there is no native mechanism to 'embed' an LWC inside a Visualforce component and place it directly on a page layout - Visualforce components and Lightning web components are built on fundamentally different rendering technologies and are not directly nestable that way. Incorrect - D: invoking an LWC 'using a call to an Apex method' does not make sense as a mechanism for surfacing a client-side UI component - an Apex method call from a Visualforce controller executes server-side logic and returns data; it does not instantiate or render a Lightning web component in the browser, which is a client-side operation Lightning Out is specifically built to perform instead.", "id": 193, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 35, "stem": "An Opportunity needs to have an amount rolled up from a custom object that is not in a master-detail relationship. How can this be achieved?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Write a trigger on the Opportunity object and use tree sorting to sum the amount for all related child objects under the Opportunity."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the Streaming API to create real-time roll-up summaries."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Write a trigger on the child object and use an aggregate function to sum the amount for all related child objects under the Opportunity."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Metadata API to create real-time roll-up summaries."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: because roll-up summary fields (Salesforce's declarative aggregation feature) require a master-detail relationship, and the relationship here is a lookup instead, the Apex Developer Guide's documented workaround is to place a trigger on the child object - the object whose records, when inserted/updated/deleted, should cause the Opportunity's total to change - and inside that trigger run a SOQL aggregate query (SUM() grouped by the parent Opportunity Id) to compute the new total and write it back to the Opportunity. Incorrect - A: a trigger placed on the Opportunity object itself only executes when an Opportunity record is saved; it is never invoked just because a related child custom-object record changed, so it structurally cannot detect the very events that should update the rollup, and 'tree sorting' is not a real Apex/SOQL aggregation concept in any case. Incorrect - B: the Streaming API only delivers change notifications to external subscribers - it has no computation or write-back capability and cannot itself create or maintain a summed value on a record. Incorrect - D: the Metadata API is a deployment/configuration API for managing fields, objects, layouts, and other metadata - it has no runtime data-processing role and cannot calculate or update a live aggregate value in response to data changes.", "id": 194, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 34, "stem": "Universal Containers decides to use purely declarative development to build out a new Salesforce application. Which two options can be used to build out the business logic layer for this application?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Remote Actions"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Record-Triggered Flow"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Batch Jobs"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Validation Rules"}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["B", "D"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - B: A record-triggered flow is built entirely through Flow Builder's point-and-click interface - no Apex is written - and can implement business logic such as field updates, related-record creation, and conditional branching whenever a record is created, updated, or deleted, making it a genuinely declarative way to implement business logic. Correct - D: a validation rule is configured purely through a point-and-click formula builder in Setup with zero code, and it directly implements business-rule enforcement (blocking saves that violate a condition), which is a core part of an application's business logic layer, built entirely declaratively. Incorrect - A (the screenshot's marked answer): Remote Actions (JavaScript remoting, using the @RemoteAction annotation) are, by definition, Apex methods invoked from client-side JavaScript - writing and maintaining a @RemoteAction method is programmatic development, not declarative, so it does not belong in a 'purely declarative' toolkit. Incorrect - C: Batch Apex (a class implementing the Database.Batchable interface) is, like Remote Actions, Apex code that a developer writes and deploys - it is one of the most code-centric automation mechanisms on the platform and is the opposite of a declarative tool.", "id": 195, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 33, "stem": "Cloud Kicks has a multi-screen flow that its call center agents use when handling inbound service desk calls. At one of the steps in the flow, the agents should be presented with a list of order numbers and dates that are retrieved from an external order management system in real time and displayed on the screen. What should a developer use to satisfy this requirement?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "An Apex REST class"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "An invocable method"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "An Apex controller"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "An outbound message"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Flow Builder's documented extensibility mechanism for calling custom Apex from within a flow (including screen flows) is the @InvocableMethod annotation; an invocable Apex method can perform a synchronous HTTP callout to the external order management system, retrieve the order data in real time, and return it as a collection the flow can bind to a screen component for display to the agent mid-call - this is exactly the documented pattern for 'flow needs live external data' scenarios. Incorrect - A: an Apex REST class (annotated @RestResource) exposes Salesforce data TO external callers over REST - it is the inbound-facing side of an integration, the opposite direction of what's needed here, where Salesforce (the flow) needs to call OUT to the external OMS. Incorrect - C: an 'Apex controller' is the term for the backing class of a Visualforce page (or, loosely, an Aura component); a Flow does not use an 'Apex controller' as its extensibility point - it uses invocable methods, so this option misapplies terminology from a different technology. Incorrect - D: an outbound message is a one-way, asynchronous SOAP notification triggered by workflow rules when a record changes - it pushes data out to an external endpoint but has no mechanism to synchronously request and receive real-time data back for immediate display on a screen, so it cannot satisfy this requirement.", "id": 196, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 32, "stem": "Which two statements are true about using the @testSetup annotation in an Apex test class?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "A method defined with the @testSetup annotation executes once for each test method in the test class and counts towards system limits."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The @testSetup annotation is not supported when the @isTest(SeeAllData=True) annotation is used."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Records created in the test setup method cannot be updated in individual test methods."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "In a test setup method, test data is inserted once and made available for all test methods in the test class."}], "marked": ["B", "D"], "correct": ["B", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: per the Apex Developer Guide, a @testSetup method runs exactly one time for the whole test class, and Salesforce captures a database snapshot immediately after it completes; every subsequent @isTest method then starts from that same snapshot in its own rolled-back transaction, effectively making the baseline data 'available' to each test method without the setup logic re-running or re-consuming limits per test. Correct - B: the documentation explicitly states @testSetup is incompatible with @isTest(SeeAllData=true) at the class level, because SeeAllData causes the test to operate against real org data rather than the isolated, snapshotted test database that @testSetup's mechanism depends on. Incorrect - A: this describes the opposite of the real behavior - @testSetup methods run once per class, not once per test method, which is the entire performance/limits benefit of using them instead of a helper method called individually inside every test. Incorrect - C: records created in @testSetup are ordinary rows within each test method's own transaction when it starts, and can be freely inserted, updated, or deleted by that test method - there is no special immutability imposed on them; each test's changes are simply rolled back afterward without affecting the other test methods.", "id": 197, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 31, "stem": "A developer needs to create a baseline set of data (Accounts, Contacts, Products, Assets) for an entire suite of Apex tests allowing them to test isolated requirements for various types of Salesforce cases. Which approach can efficiently generate the required data for each unit test?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use @testSetup with a void method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add @isTest(seeAllData=true) at the start of the unit test class."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a mock using the HttpCalloutMock interface."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Create test data before Test.startTest() in the unit test."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: a void method annotated @testSetup creates the baseline Accounts, Contacts, Products, and Assets exactly once, and per the Apex Developer Guide's documented behavior, every test method in the class then starts from a database snapshot containing that data - this is the most efficient (least duplicated code, least DML) way to give an entire suite of tests a shared baseline. Incorrect - B: seeAllData=true relies on whatever data already happens to exist in the org rather than creating the specific, controlled baseline records described, and Salesforce's own testing best practices explicitly discourage it because it makes tests fragile and dependent on org state that can change. Incorrect - C: HttpCalloutMock is unrelated to creating sObject baseline data - it is specifically for mocking the HTTP response of an outbound callout during a test, addressing a completely different testing concern. Incorrect - D: creating the same baseline records freshly inside every single test method (even if done before Test.startTest()) duplicates the setup code and DML across every method rather than sharing one snapshot, making it markedly less efficient than the one-time @testSetup approach.", "id": 198, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 30, "stem": "Universal Containers has a support process that allows users to request support from its engineering team using a custom object, Engineering_Support__c. Users should be able to associate multiple Engineering_Support__c records to a single Opportunity record. Additionally, aggregate information about the Engineering_Support__c records should be shown on the Opportunity record. Which relationship field should be implemented to support these requirements?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lookup field from Engineering_Support__c to Opportunity"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Master-detail field from Engineering_Support__c to Opportunity"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Master-detail field from Opportunity to Engineering_Support__c"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lookup field from Opportunity to Engineering_Support__c"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: 'aggregate information... shown on the Opportunity record' is a direct requirement for a roll-up summary field, which per Salesforce Help can only be defined on the master side of a true master-detail relationship, and that relationship must be declared on the child object (Engineering_Support__c) pointing up to its parent (Opportunity) - this is the only configuration among the four options that simultaneously supports many Engineering_Support__c records per Opportunity and enables a roll-up summary on the Opportunity. Incorrect - A: a lookup relationship (in either direction) does not support roll-up summary fields at all, so while it could technically let multiple Engineering_Support__c records reference one Opportunity, it fails the 'aggregate information should be shown' half of the requirement entirely. Incorrect - C: a master-detail field must be defined on the child object referencing its parent - Salesforce does not allow a master-detail field to be placed on Opportunity (the parent) referencing Engineering_Support__c (the child); relationship direction on master-detail fields is fixed by which object is the detail record. Incorrect - D: like A, a lookup field cannot back a roll-up summary field, and placing it on Opportunity pointing to Engineering_Support__c also does not match the 'one Opportunity, many Engineering_Support__c records' cardinality the requirement describes as naturally as a child-to-parent relationship does.", "id": 199, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 29, "stem": "How many Accounts will be inserted by the following block of code?", "code": "for (Integer i = 0; i < 500; i++) {\n    Account a = new Account(Name='New Account ' + i);\n    insert a;\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "150"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "100"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "500"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "0"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: because the `insert a;` statement sits inside the for-loop body, it executes as a brand-new, separate DML statement on every single iteration rather than as one bulk statement for all 500 Accounts - per the Apex Developer Guide's governor limits, a single transaction is capped at 150 total DML statements, so on the 151st iteration the platform throws an unhandled System.LimitException ('Too many DML statements'). Because that exception is never caught, the entire transaction is rolled back - Apex transactions are all-or-nothing when an unhandled exception propagates out - meaning even the 150 Accounts that were successfully inserted before the failure are undone, leaving 0 Accounts actually committed to the database. Incorrect - A (150): this is the number of DML statements that succeed before the limit is hit, but it is not the final persisted state, because the unhandled exception rolls back the whole transaction including those first 150 successful inserts. Incorrect - B (100): 100 is not a documented governor-limit threshold relevant to this scenario at all - the real ceiling being tested here is 150 DML statements per transaction, not 100. Incorrect - C (500): reaching 500 would require all inserts to succeed, but the loop never gets that far - it fails and rolls back once it exceeds 150 DML statements, well before iteration 500.", "id": 200, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 28, "stem": "A developer needs to create a baseline set of data (Accounts, Contacts, Products, Assets) for an entire suite of tests allowing them to test independent requirements for various types of Salesforce Cases. Which approach can efficiently generate the required data for each unit test?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use @testSetup with a void method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add @isTest(seeAllData=true) at the start of the unit test class."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create test data before Test.startTest() in the unit test."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Create a mock using the Stub API."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: per the Apex Developer Guide, a void method annotated @testSetup runs exactly once for the entire test class, and Salesforce snapshots the database state right after it finishes; every subsequent @isTest method then begins from that same shared snapshot in its own rolled-back transaction, so the baseline Accounts, Contacts, Products, and Assets are created a single time yet remain available to every test - the most efficient documented pattern for shared baseline data. Incorrect - B: seeAllData=true makes the test rely on whatever real data already happens to exist in the org rather than a controlled, purpose-built baseline, and Salesforce's own testing guidance discourages it because it produces fragile tests whose results depend on org state that can change. Incorrect - C: creating the same baseline records at the top of every individual test method (even before Test.startTest()) means the setup DML and object-creation code is duplicated and re-executed once per test method, which is markedly less efficient than the shared one-time @testSetup snapshot. Incorrect - D: the Stub API (System.StubProvider) is used to create mock implementations of interfaces for isolating dependencies in unit tests - it does not create or persist sObject records at all, so it cannot generate the baseline Account/Contact/Product/Asset data described.", "id": 201, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 27, "stem": "What is the value of the Trigger.old context variable in a before insert trigger?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "An empty list of sObjects"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "null"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "A list of newly created sObjects without IDs"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Undefined"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: per the Apex Developer Guide's trigger context variable documentation, Trigger.old (and Trigger.oldMap) only contain meaningful data in update and delete trigger contexts, where there is a genuine 'before' version of the record to compare against; in any insert context (before or after insert), there is no prior version of the record at all, so Trigger.old is simply null rather than any kind of list. Incorrect - A: an empty list is a distinct value from null in Apex (you can call .size() on an empty list without error, but calling it on null throws a NullPointerException) - Salesforce's documented behavior is specifically that Trigger.old is null in insert triggers, not an empty, iterable list. Incorrect - C: this describes something closer to what Trigger.new would hold in an insert context (new records not yet assigned Ids in a before insert trigger) - it does not describe Trigger.old, which is unrelated to the new records being inserted. Incorrect - D: 'Undefined' is not an Apex concept at all (unlike JavaScript, Apex variables are either assigned a value, a default, or null) - describing the value as 'undefined' misapplies terminology from a different language.", "id": 202, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 26, "stem": "Universal Containers implemented a private sharing model for the Account object. A custom Account search tool was developed with Apex to help sales representatives find accounts that match multiple criteria they specify. Since its release, users of the tool report they can see Accounts they do not own. What should the developer use to enforce sharing permissions for the currently logged in user while using the custom search tool?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the without sharing keyword on the class declaration."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the schema describe calls to determine if the logged-in user has access to the Account object."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the with sharing keyword on the class declaration."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the UserInfo Apex class to filter all SOQL queries to returned records owned by the logged-in user."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: per the Apex Developer Guide's security documentation, declaring a class with sharing causes every SOQL query and DML operation executed by that class to respect the running user's record-level sharing rules (role hierarchy, sharing rules, manual shares, teams, etc.) - given the org already has a private OWD on Account, simply adding with sharing to the search class's declaration is enough to make its queries automatically filter out Accounts the user isn't allowed to see, which directly fixes the reported symptom. Incorrect - A: without sharing does the opposite of what's needed - it explicitly runs the class's DML/queries in system context, ignoring sharing rules entirely, which is consistent with (and likely the actual cause of) users being able to see Accounts they don't own; adding it would make the problem worse, not better. Incorrect - B: schema describe calls (like isAccessible()) check object- and field-level security (CRUD/FLS - whether the user's profile/permission set grants access to the Account object or its fields at all) - they say nothing about record-level sharing (which specific Account records this particular user can see), so they don't address the reported issue of seeing accounts they don't own. Incorrect - D: manually filtering every query to 'records owned by the logged-in user' via UserInfo.getUserId() only handles direct ownership - it would incorrectly hide Accounts the user should see through role hierarchy, sharing rules, or team membership even though they don't personally own them, and it requires hand-maintaining this filter in every query rather than using the platform's built-in, automatically-correct with sharing enforcement.", "id": 203, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 25, "stem": "Which annotation exposes an Apex class as a RESTful web service?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "@RestResource(urlMapping='/myService/*')"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "@RemoteAction"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "@HttpInvocable"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: the Apex REST API documentation states that annotating a global Apex class with @RestResource(urlMapping='...') registers it as a custom REST endpoint under /services/apexrest/, with individual methods further marked @HttpGet/@HttpPost/@HttpPut/@HttpDelete/@HttpPatch to handle the corresponding HTTP verbs - this is the documented, purpose-built way to expose Apex as a RESTful web service consumable by any external HTTP client. Incorrect - B: @RemoteAction is documented specifically for Visualforce JavaScript remoting - letting client-side JS on a Visualforce page call a controller method directly - it does not create an externally addressable REST endpoint. Incorrect - D: @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) exposes a method to the Lightning Component framework (Aura/LWC) for use with @wire or imperative Apex calls from a component running inside Salesforce - it is an internal UI-to-Apex data channel, not an external RESTful service. Incorrect - C: @HttpInvocable is not a real Apex annotation; there is no such decorator in the Apex language - the actual annotations for handling HTTP verbs inside a @RestResource class are @HttpGet, @HttpPost, and so on.", "id": 204, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 24, "stem": "A developer considers the following snippet of code. Based on this code, what is the value of x?", "code": "Boolean isOK;\nInteger x;\nString theString = 'Hello';\n\nif (isOK == false && theString == 'Hello') {\n    x = 1;\n} else if (isOK == true && theString == 'Hello') {\n    x = 2;\n} else if (isOK != null && theString == 'Hello') {\n    x = 3;\n} else {\n    x = 4;\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "4"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "2"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "1"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "3"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A (x = 4): isOK is declared as a Boolean but never assigned a literal value, so per Apex's default-value rules it holds null rather than false. Each condition is evaluated explicitly against that null: isOK == false is false (null does not equal the primitive false), isOK == true is likewise false, and isOK != null is false because isOK genuinely is null - since theString == 'Hello' is true throughout, the truth of every branch hinges entirely on the isOK comparison, and all three fail, so control reaches the final else and x is set to 4. Incorrect - B (x = 2): would require isOK == true, which cannot be true while isOK is null. Incorrect - C (x = 1): would require isOK == false, but Apex treats null as distinct from the literal false for a Boolean, so this comparison also evaluates to false. Incorrect - D (x = 3): would require isOK != null to be true, which is false precisely because isOK is null - this branch is the most common trap in this question because it looks like it should catch 'any non-null value,' but null itself never satisfies != null.", "id": 205, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 23, "stem": "A developer created a custom order management app that uses an Apex class. The order is represented by an Order object and an OrderItem object that has a master-detail relationship to Order. During order processing, an order may be split into multiple orders. What should a developer do to allow their code to move some existing OrderItem records to a new Order record?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add without sharing to the Apex class declaration."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Change the master-detail relationship to an external lookup relationship."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a junction object between OrderItem and Order."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Select the Allow reparenting option on the master-detail relationship."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: per Salesforce Help on master-detail relationships, a detail record's parent reference is locked by default and cannot be changed by any means, including Apex, in order to protect roll-up summary integrity; explicitly checking the 'Allow reparenting' setting on the master-detail relationship definition lifts that restriction, permitting code to update an OrderItem's master-detail field to point at a different Order - exactly the capability needed to split an order. Incorrect - A: without sharing only changes whether the running class enforces the current user's record-level sharing rules; it has no bearing on whether a master-detail child's locked parent reference can be reassigned, which is governed purely by the reparenting setting. Incorrect - B: converting to an 'external lookup' relationship is a Salesforce Connect feature used for relating to external objects, not a way to relax reparenting restrictions on a normal internal master-detail relationship, and doing so would also remove the ability to use roll-up summary fields the app likely depends on. Incorrect - C: OrderItem-to-Order is a straightforward one-to-many relationship, not a many-to-many one, so introducing a junction object (the pattern for many-to-many relationships) is a data-model mismatch and does nothing to address the reparenting restriction.", "id": 206, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 22, "stem": "What are two considerations for deploying from a sandbox to production?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Unit tests must have calls to the System.assert method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Should deploy during business hours to ensure feedback can be quickly addressed."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "At least 75% of Apex code must be covered by unit tests."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "All triggers must have at least one line of test coverage."}], "marked": ["C", "D"], "correct": ["C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: per Salesforce's documented deployment requirements, deploying Apex changes to production requires at least 75% aggregate code coverage across all Apex classes and triggers org-wide - this is a hard gate enforced automatically by the deployment/validation pipeline. Correct - D: separately from the 75% aggregate figure, every individual trigger must independently have greater than 0% of its own lines covered by tests; a trigger with zero coverage blocks deployment even when overall org coverage comfortably exceeds 75%, because both conditions are checked. Incorrect - A: Salesforce's deployment validation does not require test methods to contain System.assert calls to count toward coverage or to pass - a test with no assertions still executes lines of code and contributes to coverage percentages (though writing assertion-free tests is poor practice, it is not what blocks a deployment). Incorrect - B: there is no platform requirement or Salesforce-documented best practice mandating deployments happen during business hours; in fact many teams intentionally deploy during low-traffic windows to minimize user impact, making this the opposite of standard guidance.", "id": 207, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 21, "stem": "A development team wants to use a deployment script to automatically deploy to a sandbox during their development cycles. Which two tools can they use to run a script that deploys to a sandbox?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "VSCode"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "SFDX CLI"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Change Sets"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Developer Console"}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: the Salesforce CLI (sfdx/sf) is explicitly designed to be invoked from scripts and CI pipelines - commands to deploy source to an org can run entirely non-interactively, which is exactly what an automated deployment script needs. Correct - A: VS Code with the Salesforce Extension Pack provides an integrated terminal and task-runner interface that can execute those same CLI deployment commands (or wrapper shell/npm scripts calling the CLI) as part of a developer's local or semi-automated workflow, making it a practical tool for running deployment scripts during development. Incorrect - C: Change Sets are a point-and-click, UI-driven mechanism for selecting and promoting metadata between two connected orgs - they have no scripting or command-line interface at all, so they cannot be 'run' by an automated deployment script. Incorrect - D: the Developer Console is an interactive, browser-based tool for writing/running Apex, viewing logs, and using the Query Editor - it has no deployment or scripting capability and cannot be invoked from an external automation script.", "id": 208, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 20, "stem": "Where are two locations a developer can look to find information about the status of batch or future methods?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Apex Flex Queue"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Developer Console"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Paused Flow Interviews component"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Apex Jobs"}], "marked": ["A", "D"], "correct": ["A", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: the Apex Jobs page in Setup is documented as the central place to view the status (queued, processing, completed, failed), batch counts, and error details for asynchronous Apex including Batch Apex, future methods, Queueable jobs, and Scheduled Apex. Correct - A: the Apex Flex Queue, also in Setup, specifically shows Batch Apex jobs that are holding/queued (waiting for an available processing slot) before they begin executing, which is documented as a related but distinct monitoring view from the main Apex Jobs page. Incorrect - B: the Developer Console is used to view debug logs and write/execute Apex interactively; it does not have a dedicated job-status monitoring view equivalent to Apex Jobs or the Flex Queue. Incorrect - C: 'Paused Flow Interviews' is a Setup page specifically for monitoring paused Flow (not Apex) interviews - it is unrelated to batch Apex or future method status, since those are entirely different automation technologies with separate monitoring surfaces.", "id": 209, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 19, "stem": "Considering the following code snippet, when the code executes a DML exception is thrown. How should a developer modify the code to ensure exceptions are handled gracefully?", "code": "public static void insertAccounts(List<Account> theseAccounts) {\n    for (Account thisAccount : theseAccounts) {\n        if (thisAccount.website == null) {\n            thisAccount.website = 'https://www.demo.com';\n        }\n    }\n    update theseAccounts;\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Implement Change Data Capture."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Implement the upsert DML statement."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Remove null items from the list of Accounts."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Implement a try/catch block for the DML."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: wrapping the `update theseAccounts;` statement in a try/catch block lets the code catch the resulting DmlException (or its subtype) at the point it is thrown, log or otherwise respond to it, and continue execution afterward rather than letting the exception propagate unhandled and abort the whole calling transaction - this is the standard Apex pattern for graceful exception handling. Incorrect - A: Change Data Capture is a platform event mechanism for publishing notifications when records change, used for external integration/subscription scenarios - it has no relationship to catching or gracefully handling an exception thrown by a DML statement in this method. Incorrect - B: swapping update for upsert changes whether records are matched/inserted-or-updated by Id or an external ID, but it does not, by itself, add any exception handling - if the same underlying validation or field-level problem causes a failure, upsert can throw an exception just as readily as update, so this doesn't address the 'handle gracefully' requirement. Incorrect - C: removing null items from the list assumes the exception is caused by null elements in the collection, which isn't stated or implied by the snippet (the loop already null-checks the website field, not the Account elements themselves) - this doesn't provide graceful handling of whatever DML exception is actually being thrown (e.g., a validation rule failure).", "id": 210, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 18, "stem": "Which three resources in an Aura component can contain JavaScript functions?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Style"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Design"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Controller"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Renderer"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Helper"}], "marked": ["C", "D", "E"], "correct": ["C", "D", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C, D, E: per the Lightning Aura Components Developer Guide, an Aura component bundle includes a Controller resource (client-side action handlers wired to markup events), a Helper resource (shared reusable JS functions callable from both Controller and Renderer), and a Renderer resource (JS overrides of the component's rendering lifecycle methods) - all three are JavaScript files by design and can contain arbitrary functions. Incorrect - A: the Style resource is a .css file scoped automatically to the component; it holds only CSS rules and cannot contain JavaScript. Incorrect - B: the Design resource is a .design XML file used solely to expose which component attributes are configurable in the Lightning App Builder / Community Builder - it is declarative markup/metadata, not a script file, and contains no JavaScript functions.", "id": 211, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 17, "stem": "While developing an Apex class with custom search functionality that will be launched from a Lightning Web Component, how can the developer ensure only records accessible to the currently logged in user are displayed?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED clause within the SOQL."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the inherited sharing keyword."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the without sharing keyword."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the with sharing keyword."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: per the Apex Developer Guide's security documentation, declaring the class with sharing causes every SOQL query and DML statement it performs to respect the running user's record-level sharing rules (OWD, role hierarchy, sharing rules, manual shares) - so the custom search only ever returns records that user is actually permitted to see, which is exactly the requirement described. Incorrect - A: WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED enforces object- and field-level security (CRUD/FLS - whether the user's profile/permission set can access the object or specific fields at all), not record-level sharing (which individual records they can see); it would not by itself filter out records the user lacks sharing access to. Incorrect - C: without sharing explicitly runs the class in system context, ignoring the user's sharing rules entirely - the opposite of what's needed, and would let the search return records the user should not be able to see. Incorrect - B: inherited sharing means the class takes on whatever sharing mode its caller already has - if the caller happens to run without sharing, this class would too, providing no guarantee that sharing is enforced; the explicit, deterministic choice here is with sharing, not inherited sharing, when the requirement is to always enforce the running user's access regardless of caller.", "id": 212, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 16, "stem": "A lead developer creates a virtual class called \"OrderRequest\". How can a developer use the OrderRequest class within the CustomerOrder class?", "code": "public class CustomerOrder {\n    //code implementation\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "@Extends(class=\"OrderRequest\") public class CustomerOrder"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "@Implements(class=\"OrderRequest\") public class CustomerOrder"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "public class CustomerOrder extends OrderRequest"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "public class CustomerOrder implements Order"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - C: per the Apex Developer Guide, a virtual (or abstract) class is inherited by another class using the extends keyword directly in the class declaration - public class CustomerOrder extends OrderRequest - which lets CustomerOrder inherit OrderRequest's non-private members and optionally override any of its virtual methods. Incorrect - A and B (the screenshot's marked answer, A): @Extends and @Implements are not valid Apex annotations at all - Apex has no annotation-based mechanism for declaring inheritance or interface adoption; both class inheritance (extends) and interface adoption (implements) are expressed directly as keywords in the class declaration line itself, not via an annotation above it, so code written this way would fail to compile. Incorrect - D: implements is the correct keyword only for adopting an interface (a contract of method signatures with no implementation); OrderRequest is stated to be a virtual class, not an interface, so implements is the wrong relationship keyword here - and 'Order' is also a different, undefined type name in this snippet, not OrderRequest at all.", "id": 213, "bullets": []}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 15, "stem": "Universal Containers is building a recruiting app with an Applicant object that stores information about an individual person and a Job object that represents a job. Each applicant may apply for more than one job. What should a developer implement to represent that an applicant has applied for a job?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Master-detail field from Applicant to Job"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Formula field on Applicant that references Job"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Junction object between Applicant and Job"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lookup field from Applicant to Job"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: per Salesforce Help's data modeling documentation, a many-to-many relationship - here, one applicant can apply to many jobs, and one job can receive applications from many applicants - is modeled with a junction object that holds two separate master-detail relationships, one to each of the two related objects; this junction object (e.g., 'Application__c') represents each individual applicant-to-job application as its own record, which can also carry extra fields specific to that application (like application date or status). Incorrect - A and D: a direct master-detail or lookup field placed on Applicant pointing to Job can only ever reference a single Job per Applicant record (a one-to-many relationship, one Applicant to many Jobs is not even what's described - it would actually be the reverse, many Applicants pointing at one Job) - neither field type can represent one applicant relating to multiple jobs simultaneously without duplicating the Applicant record itself. Incorrect - B: a formula field can only read and display existing field values (including, via a relationship, fields from a single directly-related parent record) - it has no ability to create or represent a many-to-many association between two objects, and cannot store or reference multiple Job records for one Applicant.", "id": 214, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 14, "stem": "Which Lightning Web Component custom event property settings enable the event to bubble up the containment hierarchy and cross the Shadow DOM boundary?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "bubbles: true, composed: true"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "bubbles: false, composed: false"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "bubbles: true, composed: false"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "bubbles: false, composed: true"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide documents that a CustomEvent's bubbles property controls whether the event propagates upward through the DOM containment hierarchy toward ancestor elements, while the separate composed property controls whether that propagation is also allowed to cross shadow DOM boundaries (out of the component's own shadow tree into an ancestor's light DOM and beyond); to achieve both bubbling up the hierarchy AND crossing shadow boundaries, an event must be constructed with both bubbles: true and composed: true set simultaneously - either one alone is insufficient for the full behavior described. Incorrect - B: with both flags false (the LWC default), the event does not bubble at all and does not cross any shadow boundary - it is only visible to a listener registered directly on the dispatching element itself. Incorrect - C: bubbles: true alone lets the event travel up through elements within the same shadow tree, but composed: false stops it from crossing out of that shadow boundary into an ancestor component's DOM, so it would not reach listeners outside the immediate shadow tree. Incorrect - D: composed: true alone without bubbles: true means the event does not propagate through the DOM hierarchy at all (it only reaches a direct listener on the dispatching element) - composed only matters for events that are already bubbling, so setting only this flag has no meaningful propagation effect.", "id": 215, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 13, "stem": "Which three data types can a SOQL query return?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "sObject"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Integer"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "List"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Long"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Double"}], "marked": ["A", "B", "C"], "correct": ["A", "B", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A, B, C: per the Apex Developer Guide's SOQL documentation, an inline SOQL query can be assigned to a List<sObject> when it returns any number of rows (C), to a single sObject variable when it is guaranteed to return zero or one row (A), or to an Integer specifically when the query is a COUNT() aggregate query, since COUNT() always yields a single whole-number result (B). Incorrect - D and E: Long and Double are not documented SOQL query return types - while numeric aggregate functions like SUM() or AVG() can return a Decimal (or be wrapped in AggregateResult), Apex's SOQL result-binding rules for inline queries specifically recognize sObject, List<sObject>, and Integer-for-COUNT() as the standard assignment targets, not Long or Double directly from a query result.", "id": 216, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 12, "stem": "A developer is asked to prevent anyone other than a user with the Sales Manager profile from changing the Opportunity Status to Closed Lost if the loss reason is blank. Which automation allows the developer to satisfy this requirement in the most efficient manner?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "A record trigger flow on the Opportunity object"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "An approval process on the Opportunity object"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "An Apex trigger on the Opportunity object"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "An error condition formula on a validation rule on Opportunity"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: a validation rule's error-condition formula can, in a single declarative expression, combine $Profile.Name (or a profile-check function) to identify non-Sales-Manager users, ISPICKVAL(StageName, 'Closed Lost') to detect the specific status change, and ISBLANK(Loss_Reason__c) to check for a blank reason - ANDing these together and returning an error blocks the save with zero code, which is the most efficient (fastest to build, native, no maintenance overhead) way to satisfy a pure save-time conditional-block requirement like this. Incorrect - A and C: a record-triggered flow or an Apex trigger could technically implement the same conditional check and block the save (via a fault/error path or addError), but both require more setup - additional elements/logic in a flow, or writing and testing actual code in a trigger - than a single validation rule formula, making them less efficient for a requirement that is purely 'block this save under these conditions,' which is precisely what validation rules exist for. Incorrect - B: an approval process routes an already-permitted save through a sequence of approval steps after the fact; it does not block the save outright at the point of editing the way a validation rule does, and is a heavier-weight mechanism poorly suited to a simple conditional field-level restriction like this.", "id": 217, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 11, "stem": "An org has an existing flow that edits an Opportunity with an Update Records element. A developer must update the flow to also create a Contact and store the created Contact's ID on the Opportunity. Which update must the developer make in the flow?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add a new Update Records element."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add a new Create Records element."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add a new Get Records element."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add a new Roll Back Records element."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: per the Flow Builder documentation, a Create Records element inserts a brand-new record (here, the Contact) and automatically stores that record's newly assigned Id in a flow variable the moment it runs, which a subsequent Update Records element on the Opportunity can then reference to write that Contact Id onto the Opportunity - this is the standard, documented 'create then capture the new Id' pattern in Flow. Incorrect - A: an additional Update Records element only modifies fields on records that already exist and already have an Id; it has no capability to insert a brand-new Contact record in the first place, so it cannot on its own satisfy the 'create a Contact' half of the requirement. Incorrect - C: Get Records only retrieves data about existing records matching filter criteria - it cannot create a new Contact, so it produces no new Id to store. Incorrect - D: there is no 'Roll Back Records' element in Flow Builder's palette at all; flow transaction handling is managed automatically by the platform, not through a manually-added rollback element.", "id": 218, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 10, "stem": "A company has a custom object, Order__c, that has a required, unique external ID field called Order_Number__c. Which statement should be used to perform the DML necessary to insert new records and update existing records in a list of Order__c records using the external ID field?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "merge orders Order_Number__c;"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "upsert orders;"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "merge orders;"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "upsert orders Order_Number__c;"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: per the Apex Developer Guide's DML documentation, the upsert statement can optionally take a second argument naming the field to match on - upsert orders Order_Number__c; tells Salesforce to look up each record in the list by its Order_Number__c value: if a matching existing record is found it is updated, and if none is found a new record is inserted - exactly the 'insert new, update existing, matched by external ID' behavior the requirement calls for. Incorrect - B: plain upsert orders; (with no field specified) matches records by their Salesforce record Id instead, which is not useful here since the incoming list is being matched by the business's own Order_Number__c identifier, not by internal Salesforce Ids the caller may not even have. Incorrect - A and C: merge is an entirely different DML operation used to combine up to three duplicate records of the same object into one, retaining a single surviving record and deleting the others - it does not insert new records or update existing ones based on a matching key the way upsert does, so it cannot fulfill this requirement at all (and 'merge orders Order_Number__c;' with a field argument isn't even valid merge syntax, which uses a master record and a list of duplicates, not a field name).", "id": 219, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "oct24", "number": 9, "stem": "Universal Containers has developed custom Apex code and Lightning Components in a Sandbox environment. They need to deploy the code and associated configurations to the Production environment. What is the recommended process for deploying the code and configurations to Production?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the Force.com IDE to deploy the Apex code and Lightning Components."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use a change set to deploy the Apex code and Lightning Components."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use Salesforce CLI to deploy the Apex code and Lightning Components."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Ant Migration Tool to deploy the Apex code and Lightning Components."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: for a straightforward, one-off promotion of metadata from a sandbox that is connected to production via a deployment connection, Salesforce Help documents Change Sets as the built-in, point-and-click mechanism specifically designed for this exact sandbox-to-production migration path - no external tooling, CLI setup, or source-control pipeline is required, making it the recommended default for this scenario. Incorrect - A: the Force.com IDE (the old Eclipse-based plugin) has been officially retired/deprecated by Salesforce in favor of VS Code and the Salesforce CLI - it is no longer the recommended tool for any deployment workflow. Incorrect - C and D: the Salesforce CLI and the Ant Migration Tool are both real, supported deployment mechanisms, but they are aimed at source-driven, scripted, or CI/CD-pipeline-based release processes where deployments are automated and repeatable across many environments - for the simple, described scenario of promoting sandbox changes directly to production without mention of a broader pipeline, Change Sets remain the documented, simpler recommended approach.", "id": 220, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 5, "stem": "The Account object in an organization has a master-detail relationship to a child object called Branch. The following automations exist: Roll-up summary fields; Custom validation rules; Duplicate rules. A developer created a trigger on the Account object. Which two things should the developer consider while testing the trigger code?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Rollup summary fields can cause the parent record to go through Save."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Duplicate rules are executed once all DML operations commit to the database."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The trigger may fire multiple times during a transaction."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The validation rules will cause the trigger to fire again."}], "marked": ["A", "C"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Whenever a Branch (child) record is inserted, updated, or deleted, Salesforce recalculates any roll-up summary field on the parent Account and writes the new value back to it. That write is itself a save of the Account record, which means the Account's own before/after triggers run again as part of the same transaction. A developer testing the Account trigger must account for this indirect, roll-up-driven save path, not just direct DML on Account. Correct - C: Because of chains like the one in A, plus workflow field updates, process builder/flow updates, and recursive DML inside the trigger itself, a single logical transaction can invoke the same trigger multiple times. Well-written triggers must be idempotent and bulk-safe across repeated invocations, and tests should simulate this by causing multiple qualifying updates in one transaction. Incorrect - B: Duplicate rules run synchronously as part of the save pipeline before the record is committed (during the 'Duplicate Rules' step of the order of execution), not after commit; they can block or warn on a save in progress, so this statement misdescribes the timing. Incorrect - D: Validation rules only evaluate field values and either allow the save to proceed or throw an error that stops it (via addError-equivalent behavior); they have no mechanism to invoke a trigger again - only an actual DML operation (an insert/update/delete/undelete) fires a trigger, and a validation failure prevents DML from completing at all.", "id": 221, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 4, "stem": "When importing and exporting data into Salesforce, which two statements are true?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Data Import Wizard is an application that is installed on your computer."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Developer and Developer Pro sandboxes have different storage limits."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Bulk API can be used to import large data volumes in development environments without bypassing the storage limits."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Bulk API can be used to bypass the storage limits when importing large data volumes in development environments."}], "marked": ["B", "C"], "correct": ["B", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per Salesforce's sandbox documentation, a Developer sandbox has a much smaller data and file storage allocation (around 200MB) than a Developer Pro sandbox (around 1GB), even though both copy only metadata (no production data) on refresh. Anyone loading large volumes of test data into a Developer sandbox will hit that ceiling far sooner than in Developer Pro. Correct - C: The Bulk API is simply a faster, asynchronous, chunked transport for getting large numbers of records into or out of Salesforce - it still creates real records subject to every governor and storage limit that applies to any other insert, so importing through it in a sandbox still counts against that org's storage allocation. Incorrect - A: The Data Import Wizard is a point-and-click, browser-based tool reached from Setup; nothing is installed locally. The desktop application that is installed on a machine is Data Loader (a separate tool), so this statement confuses the two. Incorrect - D: No Salesforce API - Bulk, REST, SOAP, or otherwise - bypasses storage limits; all of them insert records into the same underlying database subject to the same per-org storage ceiling, so the premise of 'bypassing' storage limits is false regardless of API choice.", "id": 222, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 3, "stem": "A developer considers the following snippet of code. Based on this code, what is the value of x?", "code": "Boolean isOK;\nInteger x;\nString theString = 'Hello';\n\nif (isOK == false && theString == 'Hello') {\n    x = 1;\n} else if (isOK == true && theString == 'Hello') {\n    x = 2;\n} else if (isOK != null && theString == 'Hello') {\n    x = 3;\n} else {\n    x = 4;\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "3"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "4"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "2"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "1"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B (x = 4): isOK is declared as a Boolean but never assigned a literal, so like every un-initialized Apex primitive wrapper type it defaults to null rather than false. Apex evaluates each boolean comparison against null explicitly: null == false evaluates to false (null is not equal to the primitive false), null == true also evaluates to false, and isOK != null evaluates to false because isOK genuinely is null. Since theString == 'Hello' is true in every branch, the truth of each branch depends entirely on the isOK comparison, and all three fail, so control falls through to the final else and x is set to 4. Incorrect - A (x = 3) would require isOK != null to be true, which only happens if isOK holds an actual true or false value, not null. Incorrect - C (x = 2) would require isOK == true, which is false because isOK is null, not true. Incorrect - D (x = 1) would require isOK == false, which is also false in Apex when isOK is null - null is a distinct third state from true/false for Boolean, a common trap in Apex null-handling questions.", "id": 223, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 2, "stem": "A developer wants to improve runtime performance of Apex calls by caching results on the client. What is the most efficient way to implement this and follow best practices?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Decorate the server-side method with @AuraEnabled(storable=true)."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Set a cookie in the browser for use upon return to the page."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Decorate the server-side method with @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Call the setStorable() method on the action in the JavaScript client-side code."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "verified-web", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the current Apex Developer Guide, annotating an Apex method with @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) tells the Lightning Data Service / UI API client-side storage layer that the method's result can be cached and reused without a server round trip, and this is now the documented, declarative, framework-level way to cache Apex results - it is also mandatory for any method used with the modern @wire decorator in LWC. Incorrect - D (the screenshot's marked answer): setStorable() is the older imperative Aura-only API for marking an $A.enqueueAction call as storable; it still works in legacy Aura components but Salesforce's own guidance has moved developers toward the declarative cacheable=true annotation because it works uniformly across Aura and LWC, is easier to reason about, and integrates with Lightning Data Service's cache invalidation. Because the question specifically asks for the 'most efficient' approach that 'follows best practices,' the modern declarative annotation, not the legacy imperative call, is the intended answer. Incorrect - A: storable is not a recognized parameter name for @AuraEnabled; the only boolean cache-control parameter the annotation accepts is cacheable, so this option is simply invalid syntax dressed up to look plausible. Incorrect - B: manually stashing data in a browser cookie bypasses the platform's built-in Lightning Data Service caching entirely, forces the developer to hand-roll serialization, expiry, and invalidation logic, and does nothing to reduce the number of Apex invocations the framework itself considers cacheable - it is neither efficient nor a recognized best practice.", "id": 224, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 1, "stem": "Universal Containers wants to automatically assign new cases to the appropriate support representative based on the case origin. They have created a custom field on the Case for the representative name. What is the best solution to assign the case to the appropriate support representative?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use an Assignment Flow element."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use a trigger on the Case object."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use a validation rule on the Case object."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use a formula field on the Case object."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: An Apex trigger on Case (before insert) can read the Origin field on the incoming record, run whatever conditional/lookup logic is needed (including querying a mapping of origins to representatives), and directly set the custom representative-name field before the record is committed - this is a normal, supported programmatic assignment pattern for a field-based routing requirement. Incorrect - A: 'Assignment Flow element' is not an actual Salesforce automation tool or Flow Builder element; Case assignment rules exist as a separate, standard Salesforce feature (Setup > Case Assignment Rules) but they are not called an 'Assignment Flow element,' making this option a distractor built from mismatched terminology rather than a real mechanism. Incorrect - C: A validation rule can only block or allow a save by evaluating a formula against the record's field values at save time and throwing an error - it has no ability to write or assign a value to a field, so it cannot populate the representative field. Incorrect - D: A formula field is always read-only and recalculated live from other field values every time it is displayed or queried; it cannot be assigned a value by automation, and it also cannot itself invoke a lookup against a separate object to determine the correct representative, so it cannot satisfy an 'assign' requirement.", "id": 225, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 60, "stem": "Which two statements are true about using the @testSetup annotation in an Apex test class?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "In a test setup method, test data is inserted once and made available for all test methods in the test class."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "A method defined with the @testSetup annotation executes once for each test method in the test class and counts towards system limits."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The @testSetup annotation is not supported when the @isTest(SeeAllData=True) annotation is used."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Records created in the test setup method cannot be updated in individual test methods."}], "marked": ["A", "C"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: A method annotated @testSetup runs exactly one time per test class execution, before any test method runs. Salesforce takes a database snapshot after that method completes, and each subsequent @isTest method starts from that same snapshot (its own rolled-back transaction), so every test method effectively 'sees' the same baseline data without the setup method re-running or re-consuming DML/query limits for each test. Correct - C: The Apex documentation explicitly states that @testSetup is not supported in combination with @isTest(SeeAllData=true) at the class level; when SeeAllData is enabled the test operates against real org data instead of the isolated, rolled-back test database, which conflicts with the snapshot-and-reset mechanism @testSetup relies on, so Salesforce disallows the combination. Incorrect - B: This precisely inverts the real behavior - the whole benefit of @testSetup is that it runs once for the class, not once per test method; running once per method (and therefore counting toward limits) is actually a fair description of what would happen without @testSetup, i.e. if you called your own data-creation helper method individually inside each test method. Incorrect - D: Test methods are perfectly free to further insert, update, or delete the baseline records that @testSetup created, because those records simply exist as normal rows in the test's isolated transaction when the method starts - there is no special immutability applied to them; each test method's changes are then rolled back after that test finishes, without affecting other test methods.", "id": 226, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 59, "stem": "Which three code lines are required to create a Lightning component on a Visualforce page?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "$Lightning.use"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "$Lightning.createComponent"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "<apex:slds/>"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "<apex:includeLightning/>"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "$Lightning.useComponent"}], "marked": ["A", "B", "D"], "correct": ["A", "B", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: The Lightning Out feature that lets a Lightning component run inside a Visualforce page requires the page to first load the Lightning Out JavaScript library, which is done by adding the <apex:includeLightning/> component to the Visualforce markup - without this nothing else in the pattern works. Correct - A: Once the library is loaded, $Lightning.use('namespace:appName', callback) is called to bootstrap and reference the specific Lightning Dependency App (an .app resource with implements='ltng:outApp') that exposes the components you want to use; this establishes the connection between the Visualforce page and the Lightning runtime. Correct - B: Inside the callback passed to $Lightning.use, the developer calls $Lightning.createComponent('namespace:componentName', attributes, 'domId', callback) to actually instantiate the desired Aura component and attach it to a DOM element on the Visualforce page - this is the line that creates the visible component. Incorrect - C: <apex:slds/> only pulls in the Salesforce Lightning Design System CSS/styling resources for a Visualforce page; it has nothing to do with loading or instantiating actual Lightning components, so while it might make the page look consistent with Lightning it is unrelated to the Lightning Out mechanism being tested here. Incorrect - E: $Lightning.useComponent is not a real method in the Lightning Out JavaScript API - the actual API surface is $Lightning.use and $Lightning.createComponent, so this option is a plausible-sounding but fabricated API name.", "id": 227, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 58, "stem": "Which three resources in an Aura component can contain JavaScript functions?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Helper"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Controller"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Design"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Style"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Renderer"}], "marked": ["A", "B", "E"], "correct": ["A", "B", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: The Controller resource (Component.js in the bundle) holds client-side controller functions that are directly wired to component markup event handlers such as onclick. Correct - A: The Helper resource (ComponentHelper.js) holds reusable JavaScript functions that the Controller and Renderer can both call, per Salesforce's documented pattern of keeping shared logic out of the controller so it can be reused and unit tested independently. Correct - E: The Renderer resource (ComponentRenderer.js) lets a developer override the framework's default rendering lifecycle methods (render, rerender, afterRender, unrender) and, being JavaScript, can contain arbitrary functions. Incorrect - D: The Style resource is a CSS file (ComponentName.css) scoped automatically to the component; CSS cannot contain JavaScript functions. Incorrect - C: The Design resource is a Design.xml file that only declares which attributes are exposed to declarative tools like Lightning App Builder and the Community Builder; it is pure XML metadata with no scripting capability.", "id": 228, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 57, "stem": "A developer wants to import 500 Opportunity records into a sandbox. Why should the developer choose to use Data Loader instead of Data Import Wizard?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Data Import Wizard can not import all 500 records."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Data Loader automatically relates Opportunities to Accounts."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Data Loader runs from the developer's browser."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Data Import Wizard does not support Opportunities."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Salesforce Help explicitly lists the objects supported by the Data Import Wizard as Accounts, Contacts, Leads, Solutions, Campaign Members, and custom objects; Opportunity is not on that list, so the Wizard simply has no UI path to import Opportunity records, forcing the developer to Data Loader instead. Incorrect - A: The Data Import Wizard's actual ceiling is 50,000 records per import, so a mere 500 records would be well within its capacity if the object were supported at all - the blocker here is object support, not volume. Incorrect - C: This gets the tools backwards - Data Loader is the installed desktop client (available for Windows/Mac) that connects via the Bulk or SOAP API, while the Data Import Wizard is the one that runs entirely inside the browser from Setup; describing Data Loader as browser-based misidentifies which tool is which. Incorrect - B: Neither tool 'automatically' relates records without configuration - both require the developer to map a lookup/relationship field (for example matching on Account Name or an External ID) during the import wizard steps; Data Loader does not have some unique auto-relate capability that the Wizard lacks.", "id": 229, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 56, "stem": "Universal Containers (UC) uses out-of-the-box order management, that has a Master-Detail relationship between Order and Order Line Item. UC stores the availability date on each Order Line Item and Orders are only shipped when all of the Order Line Items are available. Which method should be used to calculate the estimated ship date for an Order?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use a LATEST formula on each of the latest availability date fields."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use a MAX Roll-Up Summary field on the latest availability date fields."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use a CEILING formula on each of the latest availability date fields."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use a DAYS formula on each of the availability date fields and a COUNT Roll-Up Summary field on the Order."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Because the order can only ship once every line item is available, the ship date is driven by whichever line item becomes available last - the maximum of all the child availability dates. Since Order Line Item has a true master-detail relationship to Order, Salesforce's declarative roll-up summary field type MAX can be placed on the Order to automatically track the largest (latest) availability date among its children, recalculating live as line items change - no code required. Incorrect - A: There is no LATEST() function in the Salesforce formula language; formulas also cannot aggregate values across multiple child records at all, since a formula field only ever evaluates fields on the record it lives on (or its direct parent via a relationship, not its children). Incorrect - C: CEILING() is a numeric rounding function (rounds a number up to the nearest integer); it has no relevance to dates and, like any formula function, still cannot reach across child records to aggregate their values. Incorrect - D: DAYS() and COUNT() solve a different problem - DAYS() calculates elapsed time between two dates and COUNT() only tallies how many child records exist, neither of which produces the actual latest availability date value needed here.", "id": 230, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 55, "stem": "A developer creates a batch Apex job to update a large number of records, and receives reports of the job timing out and not completing. What is the first step towards troubleshooting the issue?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Decrease the batch size to reduce the load on the system."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Check the debug logs for the batch job."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Check the asynchronous job monitoring page to view the job status and logs."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Disable the batch job and recreate it with a smaller number of records."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per Salesforce's guidance on monitoring Apex jobs, the Apex Jobs page under Setup (the asynchronous job monitoring UI) is the first place to look because it shows the job's actual status, how many of the total batches have completed versus failed, the per-batch record counts, and any error messages recorded against failed batches - this diagnostic data tells the developer exactly what happened before any code or configuration change is made. Incorrect - A and D: Both jump straight to a fix (shrinking the batch size or the record set) without first confirming that batch size is even the cause of the timeout; changing configuration blind, before consulting the job monitoring data, risks solving the wrong problem or masking a logic bug such as a query-in-loop inside execute(). Incorrect - B: Debug logs are a legitimate deeper diagnostic tool, but they require a trace flag to have been active on the job's running user beforehand and only capture what happens inside a single execution; the job monitoring page is the correct first stop because it requires no advance setup and gives the overall picture (how many batches ran, how many failed) before drilling into any one execution's logs.", "id": 231, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 54, "stem": "When using Salesforce DX, what does a developer need to enable to create and manage scratch orgs?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Dev Hub"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Production"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Sandbox"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Environment Hub"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Salesforce DX Developer Guide, scratch orgs are short-lived, source-driven orgs that can only be created and managed once Dev Hub has been enabled in a production org, a Developer Edition org, or a qualifying trial org; the Salesforce CLI authenticates against that Dev Hub org to spin up and delete scratch orgs. Incorrect - B: Production is too broad an answer - a production org only supports scratch-org creation once the specific Dev Hub feature has been turned on inside it (Setup > Dev Hub); simply having a production org is not sufficient by itself. Incorrect - C: A sandbox is a copy of production used for testing configuration/code changes, but sandboxes cannot create or host scratch orgs - the two are unrelated environment types in the Salesforce DX model. Incorrect - D: The Environment Hub is a separate feature for centrally managing and linking multiple existing Salesforce orgs (production, sandboxes, Developer Edition orgs) from one place; it does not provision or manage scratch orgs, which is exclusively Dev Hub's role.", "id": 232, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 53, "stem": "A developer writes a trigger on the Account object on the before update event that increments a count field. A record triggered flow also increments the count field every time a record is created or updated. What is the value of the count field if an Account is inserted with an initial value of zero, assuming no other automation logic is implemented on the Account?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "2"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "3"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "1"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "4"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "verified-web", "explanation": "Correct - A (2): The trigger is scoped only to before update, so on the initial insert the before-update trigger does not execute at all - only the record-triggered flow, which is configured to run on both create and update, fires during the insert and increments the count from 0 to 1. Per Salesforce's documented order of execution, a record-triggered flow that performs an update as part of its own save re-enters the save pipeline for that record, which means the before-update trigger now runs (because this second pass is technically an update to the already-inserted record) and increments the count again from 1 to 2, after which the process settles. Incorrect - C (1): This would be the result only if the flow's update did not cause the before-update trigger to fire again, but Salesforce's order-of-execution documentation confirms that any update - even one issued from within the same overall transaction by automation - re-triggers before/after update logic on that object. Incorrect - B (3) and D (4): These would require the trigger or flow to fire an extra, third time, which does not happen here since there is only one flow-driven update following the initial insert and no other automation exists on the object per the question's stated assumption, so the chain stops at two increments.", "id": 233, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 52, "stem": "A developer must provide custom user interfaces when users edit a Contact in either Salesforce Classic or Lightning Experience. What should the developer use to override the Contact's Edit button and provide this functionality?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "A Visualforce page in Salesforce Classic and a Lightning page in Lightning Experience"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "A Visualforce page in Salesforce Classic and a Lightning component in Lightning Experience"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "A Lightning component in Salesforce Classic and a Lightning component in Lightning Experience"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "A Lightning page in Salesforce Classic and a Visualforce page in Lightning Experience"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Salesforce's standard button/link override feature (Setup > Object > Buttons, Links, and Actions > Edit) supports two override content types depending on the interface: a Visualforce page for Salesforce Classic, and a Lightning component (an Aura component implementing the lightning:actionOverride interface) for Lightning Experience - configuring both together is the documented way to give a fully custom Edit experience across both UIs. Incorrect - A and D: Lightning pages (App Builder record/app/home pages) are not a supported override type for standard buttons at all - overrides target either a Visualforce page or a Lightning component, never a Lightning page, so both options that mention a Lightning page as the override mechanism are structurally wrong. Incorrect - C: Lightning components cannot override standard buttons in Salesforce Classic - Classic's override mechanism only understands Visualforce pages (or a URL/standard Salesforce Classic option), so pairing a Lightning component with Classic is not something the platform supports.", "id": 234, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 51, "stem": "For which three items can a trace flag be configured?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Flow"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "User"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Apex Class"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Apex Trigger"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Visualforce"}], "marked": ["B", "C", "D"], "correct": ["B", "C", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B, C, D: Per Salesforce's Debug Log documentation, a trace flag can target a specific User (capturing everything that user's transactions execute, including the special Automated Process and Guest User entities), a specific Apex Class, or a specific Apex Trigger - these are exactly the three entity types the Trace Flags setup page (or the TraceFlag/TracedEntityType Tooling API objects) allows you to associate a debug-level configuration with. Incorrect - A: Flow execution is captured inside whatever debug log is generated for the transaction that invoked it (for example the user's or the trigger's log) rather than being a directly traceable entity of its own with a dedicated trace flag target. Incorrect - E: Visualforce page requests are likewise captured within the debug log of the triggering user/transaction; Visualforce pages are not one of the entity types you attach a TraceFlag to - only User, Apex Class, and Apex Trigger are.", "id": 235, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 50, "stem": "While writing an Apex class, a developer wants to make sure that all functionality being developed is handled as specified by the requirements. Which approach should the developer use to be sure that the Apex class is working according to specifications?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Create a test class to execute the business logic and run the test in the Developer Console."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Include a savepoint and Database.rollback()."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Run the code in an Execute Anonymous block in the Developer Console."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Include a try/catch block to the Apex class."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - A: The only way to formally verify that code meets stated requirements is to write an @isTest class with System.assertEquals/assert-style assertions that exercise every requirement scenario (including edge cases and bulk data) and then run it - a passing suite of targeted assertions is direct evidence the logic does what the requirements say, and this is also the mechanism Salesforce requires (75% coverage) before any class can be deployed to production. Incorrect - D (the screenshot's marked answer): a try/catch block only changes what happens when an exception is thrown during execution - it has nothing to do with confirming that the business outcome (the actual field values, records created, etc.) matches what the requirements specify; code with a try/catch can still silently produce the wrong result and pass without error. Incorrect - B: Savepoint and Database.rollback() are transaction-control tools used to undo partial DML within a transaction (often inside error handling) - they do not verify correctness of logic, they only manage what data persists if something goes wrong. Incorrect - C: Running code in Execute Anonymous lets a developer manually observe debug output for one ad hoc invocation, but it is not repeatable, is not part of the deployed test suite, provides no formal pass/fail assertions, and does not contribute to code coverage - it is a debugging convenience, not a verification method.", "id": 236, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 49, "stem": "What can be easily developed using the Lightning Component framework?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Salesforce integrations"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Customized JavaScript buttons"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Salesforce Classic user interface pages"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lightning Pages"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "verified-web", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - D: The Lightning Component framework (Aura and LWC) is Salesforce's UI-building framework whose components are the building blocks assembled, via Lightning App Builder, into Lightning Pages (record pages, app pages, home pages) and Lightning apps - this is precisely the intended, documented use case the framework is designed and marketed for. Incorrect - A (the screenshot's marked answer): integrations with external systems are handled through Apex (HTTP callouts, REST/SOAP web services), Named Credentials, and platform events - none of which is what the Lightning Component framework itself is for; the framework is a client-side UI layer, not an integration layer. Incorrect - B: customized JavaScript buttons are a legacy Salesforce Classic feature (onClick JavaScript configured directly on a button) that Salesforce has deprecated and explicitly recommends replacing with Lightning components/Quick Actions - they are not something the Lightning Component framework produces. Incorrect - C: Lightning components are designed to run inside Lightning Experience (and, via Lightning Out, can be embedded in a Visualforce page); they are not used to build native Salesforce Classic UI pages, which remain the domain of Visualforce.", "id": 237, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 48, "stem": "A Next Best Action strategy uses an Enhance element that invokes an Apex method to determine a discount level for a Contact, based on a number of factors. What is the correct definition of the Apex method?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "@InvocableMethod global static List<List<Recommendation>> getLevel(List<ContactWrapper> input) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "@InvocableMethod global List<List<Recommendation>> getLevel(List<ContactWrapper> input) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "@InvocableMethod global Recommendation getLevel(ContactWrapper input) { /*implementation*/ }"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "@InvocableMethod global static ListRecommendation getLevel(List<ContactWrapper> input) { /*implementation*/ }"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Apex Developer Guide's rules for @InvocableMethod, the method must be declared static, must accept a single List parameter (here List<ContactWrapper>, one wrapper per Contact being evaluated so the platform can invoke it in bulk), and for a Next Best Action Enhance element specifically must return List<List<Recommendation>> - an outer list matching the input list position-for-position, with an inner list of Recommendation objects generated for each input item; option A is the only definition satisfying all three requirements simultaneously. Incorrect - B: omits the static keyword, which @InvocableMethod requires - an instance method cannot be registered as an invocable action, so this would fail to compile/deploy as a valid invocable method. Incorrect - C: uses a single ContactWrapper input instead of a List, breaking the bulk-safe contract invocable methods must follow (the platform always calls invocable methods with a list, even for one record), and also returns a bare Recommendation instead of the nested List<List<Recommendation>> structure Next Best Action expects. Incorrect - D: declares the return type as 'ListRecommendation', which is not valid Apex syntax at all (it is neither List<Recommendation> nor List<List<Recommendation>>), so this option is a syntactically invalid distractor.", "id": 238, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 47, "stem": "An Opportunity needs to have an amount rolled up from a custom object that is not in a master-detail relationship. How can this be achieved?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use the Metadata API to create real-time roll-up summaries."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Write a trigger on the child object and use an aggregate function to sum the amount for all related child objects under the Opportunity."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the Streaming API to create real-time roll-up summaries."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Write a trigger on the Opportunity object and use an aggregate function to sum the amount for all related child objects under the Opportunity."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per Salesforce Help, declarative roll-up summary fields are only available on the master side of a master-detail relationship; when the relationship is a lookup instead, the documented workaround is to put the aggregation logic in Apex on the child object's trigger, because that is the object whose records actually change and must recompute the parent total whenever they are inserted, updated, deleted, or undeleted. Inside that trigger, a SOQL aggregate query (SUM() grouped by the parent Opportunity Id) calculates the new total, which the trigger then writes back to the Opportunity. Incorrect - D: a trigger on the Opportunity object only executes when an Opportunity record itself is saved, so it would never notice when a child custom-object record changes - the very event that should recalculate the sum - making it structurally unable to keep the rollup current. Incorrect - A: the Metadata API is for deploying configuration such as fields, objects, and layouts; it has no runtime data-processing capability and cannot calculate or refresh a summed value in response to data changes. Incorrect - C: the Streaming API only pushes notifications about data changes to subscribers - it does not perform calculations or write data back to records, so on its own it cannot implement a roll-up.", "id": 239, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 46, "stem": "How does the Lightning Component framework help developers implement solutions faster?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "By providing change history and version control"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "By providing device-awareness for mobile and desktops"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "By providing an Agile process with default steps"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "By providing code review standards and processes"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: The Lightning Component framework is documented as being built on a responsive, device-aware foundation (based on modern web components/Aura), meaning a single component automatically adapts its rendering and behavior across desktop, tablet, and phone form factors without the developer writing separate mobile and desktop versions - this single-build-many-devices capability is exactly what lets teams ship faster. Incorrect - A: version history and version control are provided by external tooling (Git, Salesforce DX source tracking, sandboxes/scratch orgs) rather than being a built-in feature of the Lightning Component framework itself, which is a UI/rendering framework, not a source-control system. Incorrect - C: the framework does not impose or supply an Agile methodology with defined sprints/ceremonies - project management process is a team/organizational choice entirely separate from which UI framework is used to build components. Incorrect - D: code review standards and processes are a team practice (often enforced via tools like static analysis, PMD, or peer review in a pull request workflow); the Lightning Component framework provides no built-in code-review mechanism.", "id": 240, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 45, "stem": "What are three considerations when using the @InvocableMethod annotation in Apex?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "A method using the @InvocableMethod annotation can have multiple input parameters."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "A method using the @InvocableMethod annotation can be declared as Public or Global."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Only one method using the @InvocableMethod annotation can be defined per Apex class."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "A method using the @InvocableMethod annotation must define a return value."}, {"letter": "E", "text": "A method using the @InvocableMethod annotation must be declared as static."}], "marked": ["B", "C", "E"], "correct": ["B", "C", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - E: The Apex Developer Guide states an invocable method must be declared static, because Flow and Process Builder invoke it directly on the class rather than instantiating an object first. Correct - C: the documentation also limits a class to exactly one @InvocableMethod - if you need multiple invocable actions, they must live in separate classes. Correct - B: the method's access modifier can be either public or global (global specifically being required if the method needs to be callable from a managed package's subscriber org), giving some flexibility depending on packaging needs. Incorrect - A: an invocable method can only accept a single parameter, and that parameter must be a List (of primitives, sObjects, or a custom Apex class) so Flow can call it in bulk with many records at once - multiple separate parameters are not supported. Incorrect - D: a return value is optional; many invocable methods are declared void because they only need to perform an action (like a DML update) and do not need to hand data back to the calling Flow.", "id": 241, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 43, "stem": "Which two are best practices when it comes to Aura component and application event handling?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use component events to communicate actions that should be handled at the application level."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Handle low-level events in the event handler and re-fire them as higher-level events."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Try to use application events as opposed to component events."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Reuse the event logic in a component bundle, by putting the logic in the helper."}], "marked": ["B", "D"], "correct": ["B", "D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: The Lightning Aura Components Developer Guide's event-handling best practices recommend that a component catch low-level, generic events (such as a raw click or a change event) in its handler and translate/re-fire them as a higher-level, semantically meaningful custom event (like 'itemSelected') so that consumers of the component only ever have to reason about business-meaningful events, not implementation details. Correct - D: the same guidance recommends putting reusable event-handling logic in the component's Helper resource rather than duplicating it inline in multiple Controller handler functions, since Helper functions can be called from any handler in the bundle, improving maintainability and testability. Incorrect - C: this inverts Salesforce's actual guidance - application events are documented as broadcast-style and should be used sparingly because every component listening for that event type receives it regardless of relevance, which is harder to reason about and less performant at scale; the recommended default is component events, which travel a defined bubble path from child to parent. Incorrect - A: component events are meant for parent-child communication within a containment hierarchy, not for broadcasting to unrelated parts of the application; 'communicate actions that should be handled at the application level' describes the actual use case for an application event, not a component event, so this option mismatches event type to use case.", "id": 242, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 41, "stem": "A PrimaryId__c custom field exists on the Candidate__c custom object. The field is used to store each candidate's id number and is marked as Unique in the schema definition. As part of a data enrichment process, Universal Containers has a CSV file that contains updated data for all candidates in the system. The file contains each Candidate's primary id. Universal Containers wants to upload this information into Salesforce, while ensuring all data rows are correctly mapped to a candidate in the system. Which technique should the developer implement to streamline the data upload?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Update the PrimaryId__c field definition to mark it as an External Id."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Create a before save flow to correctly map the records."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a before insert trigger to correctly map the records."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Upload the CSV into a custom object related to Candidate__c."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per Salesforce Help on External ID fields, marking PrimaryId__c as an External ID (it is already Unique, and External ID fields are automatically indexed) enables data-loading tools like Data Loader to perform an upsert operation keyed on that field - every incoming CSV row is matched to the existing Candidate__c record whose PrimaryId__c matches, with zero custom code, which is exactly the 'streamline' outcome the question asks for. Incorrect - B and C: both a before-save flow and a before-insert trigger would require the developer to write custom matching/mapping logic by hand, essentially re-implementing what upsert-on-External-ID already gives you for free declaratively; this is unnecessary engineering effort for a problem the platform solves natively, and a before insert trigger in particular would not even help update existing records, only new inserts. Incorrect - D: loading the CSV into a separate related custom object does not update the Candidate__c records' data at all - it just creates a parallel data set that would still need to be reconciled back to Candidate__c through additional logic, failing the 'correctly mapped to a candidate' requirement directly.", "id": 243, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 40, "stem": "An org has an existing flow that edits an Opportunity with an Update Records element. A developer must update the flow to also create a Contact and store the created Contact's ID. Which update must the developer make in the flow?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add a new Update Records element."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add a new Create Records element."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add a new Get Records element."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add a new Roll Back Records element."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per Flow Builder documentation, a Create Records element performs a DML insert of a new record (here, the Contact) and automatically populates a flow variable with that new record's Id once the insert succeeds, which is exactly the 'store the created Contact's ID' requirement - no separate Get Records lookup is needed since Create Records already returns the Id. Incorrect - A: Update Records only modifies fields on an existing record that the flow already has a reference to; it has no capability to insert a brand-new Contact record, so it cannot satisfy the 'create a Contact' half of the requirement. Incorrect - C: Get Records queries for existing records matching filter criteria and returns them into flow variables - it does not create anything, so it cannot produce a new Contact. Incorrect - D: 'Roll Back Records' is not an actual Flow Builder element; Flow does not expose a manual transaction-rollback element the way Apex's Database.rollback() savepoint mechanism does, making this option fabricated.", "id": 244, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 39, "stem": "A developer needs to create a baseline set of data (Accounts, Contacts, Products, Assets) for an entire suite of tests allowing them to test independent requirements. Which approach can efficiently generate the required data for each unit test?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Create test data before Test.startTest() in the unit test."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add @isTest(seeAllData=true) at the start of the unit test class."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Create a mock using the Stub API."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use @testSetup with a void method."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Apex Developer Guide, a method annotated @testSetup (which must be void) runs once before any test method in the class executes; Salesforce takes a database snapshot of the data it inserts, and every subsequent @isTest method automatically starts from that same snapshot without the setup code re-running, which is the most efficient way to give an entire suite of tests a shared baseline of Accounts, Contacts, Products, and Assets. Incorrect - A: creating the data 'before Test.startTest()' inside each individual test method means writing and re-executing that setup logic separately in every single test method, which is both more code and more redundant DML/query usage than a single shared @testSetup method - it works, but it is not the efficient, DRY approach the question is asking for. Incorrect - B: @isTest(seeAllData=true) makes real org data visible to the test instead of creating new baseline records at all; besides being explicitly discouraged as a testing anti-pattern because it makes tests fragile and dependent on org data state, it does not generate the specific Accounts/Contacts/Products/Assets the requirement calls for. Incorrect - C: the Stub API (System.StubProvider) is used to create mock implementations of a class or interface's methods for isolating a unit of code from its dependencies - it does not create actual sObject test records like Accounts or Contacts.", "id": 245, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 38, "stem": "A developer must create a Lightning component that allows users to input Contact record information to create a Contact record, including a Salary__c custom field. What should the developer use, along with a lightning-record-edit-form, so that the Salary__c field functions as a currency input and is only viewable and editable by users that have the correct field-level security on Salary__c?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "<lightning-input-currency value=\"Salary__c\"></lightning-input-currency>"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "<lightning-formatted-number value=\"Salary__c\" format-style=\"currency\"></lightning-formatted-number>"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "<lightning-input type=\"number\" value=\"Salary__c\" formatter=\"currency\"></lightning-input>"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "<lightning-input-field field-name=\"Salary__c\"></lightning-input-field>"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide, lightning-input-field is specifically designed to be used inside a lightning-record-edit-form; it reads the target field's metadata (data type, label, currency/number formatting, picklist values) directly from the schema and automatically renders the correct input control, while also automatically respecting the running user's field-level security - hiding the field entirely if the user lacks read access, and rendering it read-only if the user lacks edit access, with zero extra code from the developer. Incorrect - A and C: lightning-input-currency and a generic lightning-input with type='number' are standalone, generic input components not bound to a specific sObject field's metadata inside a record-edit-form context; they do not automatically apply field-level security checks and require the developer to manually wire up data binding and validation, failing the FLS requirement. Incorrect - B: lightning-formatted-number (with format-style='currency') is a read-only display component for showing a formatted number, not an input control at all - it cannot be used to let a user type in a new Salary value when creating a Contact.", "id": 246, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 37, "stem": "How many Accounts will be inserted by the following block of code?", "code": "for (Integer i = 0; i < 500; i++) {\n    Account a = new Account(Name='New Account ' + i);\n    insert a;\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "0"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "500"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "100"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "150"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A (0): The insert statement sits inside the for-loop body, so it runs once per iteration rather than once for the whole batch of 500 Accounts - this is the classic 'DML inside a loop' anti-pattern the Apex Developer Guide warns against. Per the documented governor limits, a synchronous Apex transaction may issue at most 150 DML statements; on the 151st loop iteration the 151st insert call throws an uncaught System.LimitException ('Too many DML statements: 151'). Because that exception is never caught, the entire transaction is rolled back atomically - Apex transactions are all-or-nothing when an unhandled exception propagates out - so none of the 150 Accounts that were successfully inserted before the failure remain committed; the final state is 0 Accounts in the database. Incorrect - B (500): would only be true if all inserts succeeded, but the 150-DML-statement limit is hit long before reaching 500 iterations. Incorrect - C (100) and D (150): both misunderstand that even the 150 Accounts inserted before the exception get rolled back once the unhandled exception aborts the transaction - the limit being hit at 150 does not mean 150 records persist; it means the whole operation fails and nothing persists.", "id": 247, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 36, "stem": "What should be used to create scratch orgs?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Developer Console"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Salesforce CLI"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Sandbox refresh"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Workbench"}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per the Salesforce DX Developer Guide, scratch orgs are created exclusively through the Salesforce CLI (commands such as sf org create scratch, formerly sfdx force:org:create) authenticated against an enabled Dev Hub; the CLI is the only supported client for this operation, and it also handles pushing source, assigning permission sets, and importing sample data into the newly created scratch org. Incorrect - A: the Developer Console is an in-browser Apex/debugging tool for writing and running code and viewing logs inside an existing org; it has no scratch-org provisioning capability. Incorrect - C: refreshing a sandbox copies metadata (and optionally data) from production into an existing sandbox - sandboxes and scratch orgs are entirely separate environment types with different provisioning mechanisms, and a sandbox refresh cannot create a scratch org. Incorrect - D: Workbench is a browser-based tool for ad hoc API calls, data queries, and metadata inspection against an existing org; it does not provision new orgs of any kind, scratch or otherwise.", "id": 248, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 35, "stem": "A developer created a custom order management app that uses an Apex class. The order is represented by an Order object and an OrderItem object that has a master-detail relationship to Order. During order processing, an order may be split into multiple orders. What should a developer do to allow their code to move some existing OrderItem records to a new Order record?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Change the master-detail relationship to an external lookup relationship."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Create a junction object between OrderItem and Order."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Select the Allow reparenting option on the master-detail relationship."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add without sharing to the Apex class declaration."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per Salesforce Help on master-detail relationships, by default a detail record's master (parent) reference is locked once set and cannot be changed by any means, including Apex - this protects roll-up summary integrity. Checking 'Allow reparenting' on the master-detail relationship definition explicitly lifts that restriction, letting code (or a user, if given access) update an OrderItem's master-detail lookup field to point at a different Order record, which is exactly the capability needed to split an order. Incorrect - A: converting to an 'external lookup' relationship is a feature specific to connecting to external objects (via Salesforce Connect), not a way to relax reparenting rules on a standard master-detail relationship between two internal custom objects; it also would remove the ability to use roll-up summary fields, which this order-total scenario likely relies on. Incorrect - B: a junction object is the pattern used to model a many-to-many relationship between two objects (via two master-detail relationships); OrderItem-to-Order is a straightforward one-to-many relationship, so introducing a junction object neither matches the data model nor solves the reparenting restriction. Incorrect - D: without sharing only changes whether a class enforces the running user's record-level sharing rules during its execution; it has no effect on whether a master-detail child record's parent reference can be changed - that is governed purely by the reparenting setting, not by sharing context.", "id": 249, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 34, "stem": "A developer completed modifications to a customized feature that is comprised of two elements: an Apex trigger and a Trigger handler Apex class. What are two factors that the developer must take into account to properly deploy the modification to the production environment?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Apex classes must have at least 75% code coverage org-wide."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "All methods in the test classes must use @isTest."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "At least one line of code must be executed for the Apex trigger."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Test methods must be declared with the testMethod keyword."}], "marked": ["A", "C"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per Salesforce's deployment requirements documented in the Apex Developer Guide, deploying Apex to a production org requires that the org-wide aggregate code coverage across all Apex classes and triggers be at least 75% - this is a hard platform gate enforced by the deployment/validation pipeline (change sets, Metadata API deployments, and CLI deployments alike). Correct - C: separately from the 75% aggregate figure, every individual Apex trigger must have greater than 0% of its own lines covered by tests - a trigger with zero coverage blocks deployment even if overall org coverage is well above 75%, because Salesforce checks both the aggregate number and each trigger's own minimal coverage. Incorrect - B: while using the @isTest annotation on test methods is standard modern practice, it is not itself a hard deployment gate the platform enforces as a blanket rule across all methods - some legacy patterns exist, and the deploy-blocking requirements Salesforce actually documents are about coverage percentages, not annotation style on every single method. Incorrect - D: the testMethod keyword is the older, now-discouraged way of marking a test method (Salesforce recommends @isTest instead), but it is not something Salesforce requires 'must' be used - in fact using @isTest, not testMethod, is the currently recommended pattern, making this statement backwards as a deployment requirement.", "id": 250, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 33, "stem": "What can be used to override the Account's standard Edit button for Lightning Experience?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lightning flow"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Lightning action"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lightning page"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lightning component"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per Salesforce's documented button/link override capabilities, a standard button such as Edit can be overridden in Lightning Experience specifically with a Lightning (Aura) component that implements the lightning:actionOverride interface - this is one of only two supported override content types (the other being a Visualforce page, which is the Classic-compatible option), and it is the one that works in Lightning Experience. Incorrect - A: a Flow can be surfaced via a Lightning page or invoked from a quick action, but Flow itself is not a supported override type for a standard Edit button - the override configuration screen does not offer 'Flow' as a content type. Incorrect - B: a Lightning action (a Lightning component packaged as a quick action) is a separate feature used to add custom buttons/actions to page layouts and the Global Actions menu; it is not the mechanism for replacing the platform's built-in standard Edit button itself. Incorrect - C: a Lightning page (built with App Builder) is a container/layout for record, app, or home pages; it is not an override target for a specific standard button, since standard button overrides target either a Visualforce page or a Lightning component, not a page container.", "id": 251, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 32, "stem": "A developer creates a Lightning web component that imports a method within an Apex class. When a Validate button is pressed, the method runs to execute complex validations. In this implementation scenario, which two options are part of the Controller according to the MVC architecture?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Apex class"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "XML file"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "JavaScript file"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "HTML file"}], "marked": ["A", "C"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: In an LWC, the component's JavaScript file (myComponent.js) is the client-side controller - it handles the button's click event, orchestrates calling the imported Apex method, and updates reactive properties that drive the view; this is the classic 'controller mediates between view and model/business-logic' role. Correct - A: the imported Apex class functions as the server-side controller in this MVC mapping - it contains the actual complex validation business logic that executes on the server when invoked, making the decision/processing that the client-side JS then reacts to. Incorrect - D: the HTML template file is the View in MVC terms - it declaratively defines what is rendered (the button, layout, data bindings) but contains no logic of its own; templates only reference properties and handlers that live in the JS controller. Incorrect - B: the component's XML configuration file (the *.js-meta.xml) is metadata describing where and how the component can be exposed (targets, API version, exposure flags) - it is configuration, not application logic, and is not part of the Controller layer in the MVC sense being tested here.", "id": 252, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 31, "stem": "A developer must write an Apex method that will be called from a Lightning component. The method may delete an Account stored in the accountRec variable. Which method should a developer use to ensure only users that should be able to delete Accounts can successfully perform deletions?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "accountRec.sObjectType.isDeletable()"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Account.isDeletable()"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Schema.sObjectType.Account.isDeletable()"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "accountRec.isDeletable()"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Per the Apex Developer Guide's documented CRUD-checking pattern, object-level delete permission is checked through the schema describe API: Schema.sObjectType.Account (a describe token for the Account object type) exposes a getDescribe() result whose isDeletable() method returns whether the running user's profile/permission sets grant delete access on that object - Schema.sObjectType.Account.isDeletable() is the exact documented syntax for this check. Incorrect - A: sObjectType is not a member you can call directly off an instance variable like accountRec in this form - the describe access pattern goes through the Schema namespace's sObjectType map (Schema.sObjectType.Account) or through accountRec.getSObjectType() as a method call, not a bare '.sObjectType' property access, so this is invalid syntax. Incorrect - B: 'Account.isDeletable()' calls isDeletable() directly on the SObject Type token without going through a describe result - the SObjectType token itself does not directly expose isDeletable(); you must first call getDescribe() (or use the Schema.sObjectType.Account shorthand which effectively does this) to get an object whose methods include isDeletable(). Incorrect - D: isDeletable() is a describe-level (object metadata) method, not an instance-level method on a specific record variable - calling it directly on accountRec (an actual Account record instance) is not valid, since a record instance does not expose permission-check methods; those live on the object's schema describe result.", "id": 253, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 30, "stem": "What is the result of the following code snippet?", "code": "public void doWork(Account acct) {\n    for (Integer i = 0; i <= 200; i++) {\n        insert acct;\n    }\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "0 Accounts are inserted."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "1 Account is inserted."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "200 Accounts are inserted."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "201 Accounts are inserted."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A (0): the same acct variable/reference is passed to insert on every loop iteration rather than a new Account being constructed each time. Per Apex DML semantics, the first `insert acct;` call succeeds and, as a side effect, populates acct.Id with the newly assigned record Id. On the second iteration, Apex attempts to insert that same sObject again - but because it already has a non-null Id, the platform recognizes this as an attempt to insert an existing record and throws a DmlException ('Cannot specify Id in an insert call' style error). Since this exception is never caught in a try/catch, it propagates up unhandled and rolls back the entire transaction, including the one Account that was successfully inserted on the first pass - Apex transactions are atomic when an unhandled exception aborts them. Incorrect - B (1): would be the result only if the loop somehow stopped safely after the first successful insert without the second insert call ever throwing, but the loop condition (i <= 200) guarantees a second iteration runs and triggers the failing re-insert. Incorrect - C (200) and D (201): both assume every single iteration's insert call succeeds, which is impossible here since every iteration after the first is re-inserting an sObject that already carries an assigned Id.", "id": 254, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 29, "stem": "A developer wants to mark each Account in a List<Account> as either Active or Inactive, based on the value in the LastModifiedDate field of each Account being greater than a given value. Which Apex technique should the developer use?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "A for loop, with a switch statement inside"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "An if-else statement, with a for loop inside"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "A for loop, with an if or if/else statement inside"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "A switch statement, with a for loop inside"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: The task requires visiting every Account in the list (an iteration, done with a for loop) and, for each one, evaluating a boolean range comparison against LastModifiedDate (a decision best expressed with if/else, since 'greater than a value' is a continuous comparison, not a match against a fixed set of discrete values) - nesting the if/else inside the for loop correctly applies the per-record decision to every record in a single pass. Incorrect - A and D: Apex's switch statement (introduced for Apex in the Winter '17 release per the Apex Developer Guide) is designed to match a single expression against a set of discrete literal values or enum constants (like matching a Status__c picklist value) - it has no native support for open-ended relational comparisons such as 'is this DateTime greater than X', so it cannot express the LastModifiedDate condition at all, regardless of loop nesting order. Incorrect - B: putting the for loop inside a single if-else (rather than the if-else inside the for loop) would mean the same one true/false decision is made once for the whole list rather than evaluated individually per Account, which does not match the requirement to mark each Account independently based on its own LastModifiedDate value.", "id": 255, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 28, "stem": "What is the result of the following code?", "code": "Account a = new Account();\nDatabase.insert(a, false);", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The record will be created and no error will be reported."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The record will not be created and no error will be reported."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The record will not be created and an exception will be thrown."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The record will be created and a message will be in the debug log."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: An Account with no Name set violates the required-field constraint (Name is a required standard field on Account), so the insert fails at the database level. Critically, Database.insert(records, false) is called with the allOrNone parameter explicitly set to false, which per the Apex Developer Guide means the method does partial-success processing and returns a Database.SaveResult (or list of them) describing per-record success/failure instead of throwing a DmlException - since the code here does not inspect that returned SaveResult or call anything like isSuccess()/getErrors(), the failure is captured in the returned result object but nothing in this snippet surfaces or throws it, so from the caller's perspective no exception propagates and no error is 'reported' anywhere visible. Incorrect - A and D: both assume the insert succeeds, but a missing required Name field always fails the save regardless of the allOrNone setting - allOrNone only changes how failures are communicated (exception vs. result object), not whether the validation failure happens. Incorrect - C: this describes the behavior of a plain `insert a;` DML statement (or Database.insert(a, true)), which does throw a DmlException on failure - but because allOrNone is explicitly false here, Database.insert suppresses the exception and instead returns a SaveResult, so no exception is thrown in this specific code.", "id": 256, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 27, "stem": "What are two benefits of using External IDs?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "An External ID field can be used to reference an ID from another external system."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "An External ID is indexed and can improve the performance of SOQL queries."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "An External ID can be a formula field to help create a unique key from two fields in Salesforce."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "An External ID can be used with Salesforce Mobile to make external data visible."}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per Salesforce Help, an External ID field is specifically designed to store a unique record identifier that comes from a system outside Salesforce, which lets integration and data-loading tools use upsert operations to match incoming records from that external system to the correct existing Salesforce record. Correct - B: Salesforce automatically indexes fields marked as External ID (alongside standard indexed fields like Name and Id), which per Salesforce's query-optimization documentation improves the performance of SOQL queries and report filters that search on that field, compared to an unindexed custom field. Incorrect - C: a formula field is, by definition, a calculated, derived, read-only value recomputed on the fly - it cannot simultaneously be marked as an External ID, because External ID fields must hold an actual, settable, unique stored value used for matching during data operations; the two field behaviors (formula vs. External ID) are mutually exclusive in Salesforce's field configuration options. Incorrect - D: External ID has no special relationship to the Salesforce Mobile App's ability to display or surface data - field visibility in the mobile app is governed by page layouts, mobile-specific layouts, and field-level security, none of which depend on whether a field happens to be flagged as an External ID.", "id": 257, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 26, "stem": "When a user edits the Postal Code on an Account, a custom Account text field named \"Timezone\" must be updated based on the values in another custom object. What is the optimal way to implement this feature?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Create an account approval process."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Create a formula field."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Build a flow with Flow Builder."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Build an account assignment rule."}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: A record-triggered flow on Account, configured to run when Postal Code changes, can use a Get Records element to look up the matching record in the other custom object (for example a Postal-Code-to-Timezone mapping object) and then an Update Records element to set the Timezone field - this cross-object lookup-then-update pattern is exactly what Flow Builder is designed for declaratively, and Salesforce's automation guidance favors Flow over Apex whenever the logic fits Flow's capabilities. Incorrect - B: a formula field can reference fields on the record itself or, via dot-notation, fields on its direct parent through a lookup/master-detail relationship - but it cannot perform an arbitrary lookup/search into an unrelated custom object to find a matching mapping row (that is a query operation, which formulas cannot perform), so it structurally cannot implement this cross-object mapping requirement. Incorrect - A: an approval process routes a record through a sequence of approval steps and can update fields as part of that approval routing, but it is not designed to run automatically and invisibly on every field edit - it is meant for a distinct, often human-involved workflow, making it a poor fit and unnecessary complexity for a simple automatic field update. Incorrect - D: an account assignment rule is not a real Salesforce feature (assignment rules exist for Leads and Cases, to route ownership to a queue or user based on criteria) - there is no 'account assignment rule' construct, and even lead/case assignment rules only reassign ownership, not arbitrary custom field values, so this option is both fabricated and mismatched to the requirement.", "id": 258, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 24, "stem": "A developer is migrating a Visualforce page into a Lightning web component. The Visualforce page shows information about a single record. The developer decides to use Lightning Data Service to access record data. Which security consideration should the developer be aware of?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lightning Data Service handles sharing rules and field-level security."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The with sharing keyword must be used to enforce sharing rules."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The isAccessible() method must be used for field-level access checks."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lightning Data Service ignores field-level security."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide, Lightning Data Service (accessed through wire adapters like getRecord, or through lightning-record-*-form components) automatically enforces the running user's object and field-level security and sharing rules on every record it reads or writes - since LDS calls run through the UI API under the hood, there is no way to bypass a user's actual permissions when going through it, and the developer does not need to write any additional enforcement code. Incorrect - B: with sharing is an Apex class-level keyword that only matters when a developer writes their own Apex controller/method that performs SOQL - it is irrelevant to Lightning Data Service, which is a client-side/UI-API mechanism that does not involve custom Apex at all for basic record access, so there is no Apex class here to even apply the keyword to. Incorrect - C: isAccessible() is an Apex describe-result method used to manually check field-level read access inside custom Apex code; because LDS already enforces FLS automatically under the hood, a developer using LDS specifically does not need to call isAccessible() themselves. Incorrect - D: this is the opposite of Lightning Data Service's actual, documented behavior - LDS explicitly does enforce field-level security rather than ignoring it, which is precisely why it is considered the secure, low-effort way to access record data from Lightning components.", "id": 259, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 25, "stem": "A developer has an Apex trigger that, in a loop over the Accounts being processed, calls the method getAssetsToUpdate (shown below) which contains a SOQL query. The code fails at run time. What should the developer change about the code to address the failure while still having the code update all of the Assets correctly?", "code": "12: public static List<Asset> getAssetsToUpdate(Account acct) {\n13:     List<Asset> assetsToUpdate = new List<Asset>();\n14:\n15:     for (Asset asst : [\n16:         SELECT Id, Status__c\n17:         FROM Asset\n18:         WHERE AccountId = :acct.Id]) {\n19:         if (asst.Status__c != acct.Status__c) {\n20:             asst.Status__c = acct.Status__c;\n21:             assetsToUpdate.add(asst);\n22:         }\n23:     }\n24:     return assetsToUpdate;\n25: }", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add a LIMIT clause to the SOQL query on line 16 to limit the number of Assets queried for an Account."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Change the getAssetsToUpdate method to process all Accounts in one call and call it outside of the for loop that starts on line 03."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add List<Asset> assets = [SELECT Id, Status__c FROM Asset WHERE AccountId = :acctId] to line 14 and iterate over the assets list in the for loop on line 15."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Move all of the logic to a Queueable class that queries for and updates the Assets and call it from the trigger."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: getAssetsToUpdate is called once per Account inside the trigger's for-loop, and each call runs its own SOQL query on line 16 - this is the classic 'SOQL inside a loop' anti-pattern the Apex Developer Guide explicitly warns against, and with enough Accounts in a single transaction it exceeds the 100-SOQL-queries-per-transaction governor limit, which is the runtime failure described. The documented fix (bulkification) is to refactor the method to accept the entire collection of Accounts (or their Ids) at once, run a single SOQL query filtered with an IN clause across all of them (grouping results by AccountId, e.g., with a Map<Id, List<Asset>>), and call this refactored method exactly once, outside the per-Account loop - this preserves correctness (every Account's Assets still get evaluated and updated) while collapsing many queries into one. Incorrect - A: adding a LIMIT clause would silently drop some Assets from being retrieved and therefore from being updated whenever an Account has more Assets than the limit allows - it 'fixes' the governor limit at the cost of correctness, which directly violates the requirement to still update all Assets correctly. Incorrect - C: this still runs the SOQL query inside the same per-Account loop context (just restructured cosmetically) - the query is still executed once per Account rather than once total, so it does nothing to reduce the query count and the same governor limit failure would still occur. Incorrect - D: wrapping the same unbulkified query-in-a-loop logic inside a Queueable class does not change how many times the query executes relative to the number of Accounts being processed - moving the problem to asynchronous Apex does not fix the underlying non-bulkified design, and Queueable jobs have their own (higher, but still finite) governor limits that the same per-record query pattern could eventually hit at larger scale.", "id": 260, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 23, "stem": "Which statement generates a list of Leads and Contacts that have a field with the phrase 'ACME'?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Map<sObject> searchList = [FIND '*ACME*' IN ALL FIELDS RETURNING Contact, Lead];"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "List<sObject> searchList = [FIND '*ACME*' IN ALL FIELDS RETURNING Contact, Lead];"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "List<List<sObject>> searchList = [SELECT Name, ID FROM Contact, Lead WHERE Name like '%ACME%'];"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "List<List<sObject>> searchList = [FIND '*ACME*' IN ALL FIELDS RETURNING Contact, Lead];"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Searching for a phrase across multiple unrelated object types (Contact and Lead here) is exactly what SOSL is for, expressed with FIND ... IN ALL FIELDS ... RETURNING; per the Apex Developer Guide, a SOSL query always returns a List<List<sObject>> - the outer list has one inner list per object type named in RETURNING, in the same order they were listed, so RETURNING Contact, Lead produces a two-element outer list: index 0 holding matching Contacts, index 1 holding matching Leads. Incorrect - A: a Map is not a valid return type for a SOSL query at all - Apex has no built-in mechanism to auto-key SOSL results into a Map, so this option is invalid syntax dressed up as a plausible-looking type. Incorrect - B: List<sObject> is the wrong shape for a multi-object SOSL result - it would only be appropriate if the search targeted a single object type; because two object types are being searched here, the compiler requires the nested List<List<sObject>> structure to keep each object type's results separate, and assigning to a flat List<sObject> would not compile. Incorrect - C: this is a SOQL query, not SOSL, and standard SOQL syntax cannot select FROM two different objects (Contact, Lead) in a single query the way this option attempts - SOQL always queries exactly one primary object (plus its relationships), so this statement is invalid regardless of the LIKE clause.", "id": 261, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 22, "stem": "Universal Containers (UC) is developing a process for their sales teams that requires all sales reps to go through a set of scripted steps with each new customer they create. In the first step of collecting information, UC's ERP system must be checked via a REST endpoint to see if the customer exists. If the customer exists, the data must be presented to the sales rep in Salesforce. Which two should a developer implement to satisfy the requirements?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Flow"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Invocable method"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Trigger"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Future method"}], "marked": ["A", "B"], "correct": ["A", "B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: A screen flow is Flow Builder's guided, multi-step, user-facing wizard type, purpose-built for exactly 'a set of scripted steps' that a sales rep walks through, including a screen that displays whatever customer data comes back from the ERP check. Correct - B: because a screen flow needs to synchronously call out to an external REST endpoint and get the response back in time to display on the very next screen, the flow calls an Apex-defined Action built from an @InvocableMethod - this is the documented mechanism for a flow to invoke custom Apex (here, performing the REST callout) and receive structured data back into flow variables for immediate display. Incorrect - C: a trigger fires automatically in response to DML on a record; it is not a user-facing, step-by-step interface and cannot present a guided series of scripted screens to a sales rep, so it does not fit the interactive nature of the requirement at all. Incorrect - D: a future method runs asynchronously, meaning the calling context does not wait for it to finish and has no way to receive a return value back into the same transaction - since the flow specifically needs to check the ERP and immediately show the result to the rep in the same interactive session, a fire-and-forget future method cannot deliver a response the flow can present on the next screen.", "id": 262, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 21, "stem": "Universal Containers wants to assess the advantages of declarative development versus programmatic customization for specific use cases in its Salesforce implementation. What are two characteristics of declarative development over programmatic customization?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Declarative development can be done using the Setup menu."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Declarative development has higher design limits and query limits."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Declarative development does not require Apex test classes."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Declarative code logic does not require maintenance or review."}], "marked": ["A", "C"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: declarative tools (Flow Builder, validation rules, page layouts, roll-up summaries, etc.) are configured entirely through point-and-click screens inside Setup, with no code editor or deployment package required, which is a defining, documented characteristic distinguishing declarative from programmatic (Apex/Visualforce/LWC) customization. Correct - C: because declarative automation is not Apex code, it is not subject to the 75% code-coverage requirement or the need for @isTest classes that gate the deployment of actual Apex to production - this is a real, practical advantage often cited when weighing declarative versus programmatic approaches. Incorrect - B: declarative tools do not receive higher governor or design limits than Apex - in fact, flows and other declarative automations are still bound by the same underlying platform limits (SOQL queries, DML rows, CPU time) within a transaction, and in various cases declarative tools actually have their own more restrictive element/design limits rather than more generous ones. Incorrect - D: this is simply false regardless of build method - declarative logic (complex flows, layered validation rules, process automation) absolutely requires ongoing maintenance and review just like code does, especially as business requirements change or multiple automations interact; the platform provides no exemption from maintenance just because something was built with clicks instead of code.", "id": 263, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 20, "stem": "A developer must implement a CheckPaymentProcessor class that provides check processing payment capabilities that adhere to what is defined for payments in the PaymentProcessor interface shown below. Which implementation is correct to use the PaymentProcessor interface class?", "code": "public interface PaymentProcessor {\n    void pay(Decimal amount);\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "public class CheckPaymentProcessor implements PaymentProcessor { public void pay(Decimal amount) { // functional code here } }"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "public class CheckPaymentProcessor extends PaymentProcessor { public void pay(Decimal amount); }"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "public class CheckPaymentProcessor implements PaymentProcessor { public void pay(Decimal amount); }"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "public class CheckPaymentProcessor extends PaymentProcessor { public void pay(Decimal amount) { // functional code here } }"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Apex Developer Guide's interface documentation, a class adopts an interface using the implements keyword, and it must then provide a full, concrete method body for every method the interface declares - here, a real implementation of pay(Decimal amount) with an actual method body, which is exactly what option A shows. Incorrect - B and D: extends is Apex's class-inheritance keyword, used only when a class inherits from another (virtual or abstract) class; it cannot be used to adopt an interface, so any option using extends PaymentProcessor is invalid syntax regardless of what else it does correctly. Incorrect - C: this correctly uses implements, but declares the pay method with a semicolon and no method body (public void pay(Decimal amount);) - that abstract-style signature-only declaration is only legal inside an interface or abstract class itself, not inside a concrete class that is supposed to actually implement the behavior; a concrete class implementing an interface must supply a real body, so this fails to compile.", "id": 264, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 19, "stem": "Universal Containers wants Opportunities to no longer be editable when reaching the Closed/Won stage. How should a developer accomplish this?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use a validation rule."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Mark fields as read-only on the page layout."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use Flow Builder."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Process Automation settings."}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: A validation rule with a formula such as ISPICKVAL(PRIORVALUE(StageName), 'Closed Won') (or checking ISCHANGED combined with the prior stage) evaluates on every save and can throw an error that blocks any further edit to a record once it was already Closed/Won in the database - because validation rules run on every save regardless of which UI, integration, or API performed the edit, this is the robust, universally enforced way to lock down a record's editability at the platform level. Incorrect - B: marking fields read-only on a page layout only affects users viewing that specific layout in the standard UI - it does nothing to prevent edits coming from a different page layout, the API, Data Loader, or an integration, so it is not a complete or reliable enforcement mechanism for 'no longer editable' as a hard business rule. Incorrect - C: while a flow could theoretically be built to detect an edit attempt on a Closed/Won Opportunity and throw a custom error, this is a heavier, more complex solution for what a single validation rule already solves natively and more simply - a validation rule is the standard, simplest tool documented for exactly this kind of save-time restriction. Incorrect - D: 'Process Automation settings' is not itself a mechanism that blocks edits to records - it refers to overall configuration/organization of automation tools (Flow Trigger Explorer, deprecated Workflow Rules, etc.), not a specific feature that can enforce a field/record from being edited.", "id": 265, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 18, "stem": "Universal Containers uses a custom object called Vendor. The Vendor custom object has a master-detail relationship with the standard Account object. Based on some internal discussions, the UC administrator tried to change the master-detail relationship to a lookup relationship, but was not able to do so. What is a possible reason that the change was not permitted?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The organization wide default for the Vendor object is Public Read/Write."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The Account object has a roll-up summary field on the Vendor object."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Some of the Vendor records have null for the Account field."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The Account object does not allow changing a field type for a custom field."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: Per Salesforce Help's documented restrictions on changing relationship types, you cannot convert a master-detail relationship to a lookup relationship while any roll-up summary field on the master object (here, Account) depends on that relationship - since a roll-up summary field only works with master-detail relationships, Salesforce blocks the conversion until every roll-up summary field referencing the Vendor relationship is deleted first; only then does the platform allow changing the relationship type. Incorrect - A: organization-wide defaults (sharing settings) control record visibility between users and have no bearing on whether a relationship type can be changed - a Public Read/Write OWD setting does not block or permit relationship-type conversions in any documented way. Incorrect - C: master-detail child records cannot have a null value in their master-detail field in the first place (the relationship is required by definition, unless it is one of the rare optional master-detail configurations) - existing null values would actually be the opposite direction of concern (converting lookup TO master-detail requires no nulls, not master-detail TO lookup), so this doesn't match the documented blocker for this specific conversion direction. Incorrect - D: this option references changing 'a field type for a custom field' on Account in general terms, which is unrelated to the specific, documented rule about roll-up summary fields blocking master-detail-to-lookup conversions - it does not describe an actual Salesforce restriction relevant to this scenario.", "id": 266, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 17, "stem": "A lead developer creates an Apex interface called Laptop. Consider the following code snippet. How can a developer use the Laptop interface within the SilverLaptop class?", "code": "public class SilverLaptop {\n    //code implementation\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "public class SilverLaptop implements Laptop"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "@Interface(class=\"Laptop\") public class SilverLaptop"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "public class SilverLaptop extends Laptop"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "@Extends(class=\"Laptop\") public class SilverLaptop"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Apex Developer Guide, a class adopts an interface's contract using the implements keyword followed by the interface name; SilverLaptop implements Laptop declares that SilverLaptop must provide concrete bodies for every method Laptop declares, which is the standard, documented syntax for interface adoption in Apex. Incorrect - C: extends is reserved for class inheritance from a virtual or abstract parent class, not for adopting an interface - Apex does not allow extends to be used with an interface name, so this would not compile. Incorrect - B and D: @Interface and @Extends are not real Apex annotations at all - Apex has no annotation-based mechanism for declaring interface implementation or class inheritance; both relationships are expressed with the plain keywords implements and extends directly in the class declaration line, making these two options entirely fabricated syntax.", "id": 267, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 16, "stem": "A developer needs to make a custom Lightning Web Component available in the Salesforce Classic user interface. Which approach can be used to accomplish this?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use a Visualforce page with a custom controller to invoke the Lightning Web Component using a call to an Apex method."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Embed the Lightning Web Component is a Visualforce Component and add directly to the page layout."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Wrap the Lightning Web Component in an Aura Component and surface the Aura Component as a Visualforce tab."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use the Lightning Out JavaScript library to embed the Lightning Web Component in a Visualforce page and add to the page layout."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per Salesforce's Lightning Out documentation, this feature is specifically designed to let Lightning components (including LWCs wrapped as needed) run outside Lightning Experience - by including the Lightning Out JavaScript library on a Visualforce page and calling $Lightning.use()/$Lightning.createComponent(), the component renders inside that Visualforce page, which can then itself be surfaced in Salesforce Classic (for example, added to a page layout or as a Visualforce tab) - this is the documented bridge that makes Lightning components visible in Classic. Incorrect - A: an Apex method call from a custom Visualforce controller can retrieve or manipulate data, but it has no capability to render a client-side Lightning Web Component's UI inside the page - invoking Apex is a data operation, not a UI-embedding mechanism. Incorrect - B: there is no supported way to 'embed a Lightning Web Component in a Visualforce Component' directly - Visualforce components and LWCs use entirely different rendering frameworks, and simply adding an LWC 'directly' to a page layout without going through Lightning Out is not a real, documented mechanism. Incorrect - C: wrapping an LWC inside an Aura component can be a valid pattern for using an LWC inside Lightning Experience (since Aura can contain LWCs), but merely wrapping it in Aura does nothing to make it visible inside Salesforce Classic - Aura components still require the same Lightning Out bridge to appear on a Visualforce page/Classic tab, so this option skips the step that actually solves the stated problem.", "id": 268, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 15, "stem": "What is a benefit of developing applications in a multi-tenant environment?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Preconfigured storage for big data"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Access to predefined computing resources"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Enforced unit testing and code coverage best practices"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Unlimited processing power and memory"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: because many customers (tenants) share the same underlying infrastructure in Salesforce's multi-tenant architecture, the platform must protect that shared infrastructure from any single tenant's code degrading performance for everyone else - this is why Salesforce enforces mandatory practices like the 75% Apex code-coverage requirement and governor limits, which push developers toward writing tested, bulk-safe, efficient code; this enforced discipline is a genuine, documented benefit of the multi-tenant model, even though it can feel restrictive. Incorrect - A: multi-tenancy does not mean storage is 'preconfigured for big data' - every org still operates within specific, finite data and file storage allocations tied to its edition/licenses, which must be actively managed, not simply granted as unlimited or pre-sized for big-data workloads. Incorrect - B: 'predefined computing resources' undersells and mischaracterizes the actual model - compute is shared and governed by limits (CPU time, heap size, etc.) rather than being handed to a tenant as a dedicated, predefined allocation the way a single-tenant VM might be. Incorrect - D: this is the direct opposite of reality - multi-tenancy is exactly why processing power and memory are NOT unlimited; governor limits exist precisely because compute must be rationed fairly across all tenants sharing the same infrastructure.", "id": 269, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 14, "stem": "Universal Containers has implemented an order management application. Each Order can have one or more Order Line items. The Order Line object is related to Order via a master-detail relationship. For each Order Line item, the total price is calculated by multiplying the Order Line item price with the quantity ordered. What is the best practice to get the sum of all Order Line item totals on the Order record?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Apex trigger"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Quick action"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Roll-up summary field"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Formula field"}], "marked": ["C"], "correct": ["C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - C: Because Order Line has a genuine master-detail relationship to Order, Salesforce's declarative roll-up summary field type (SUM) can total the child Order Line totals directly onto the Order with zero code, recalculating automatically whenever a child record is inserted, updated, or deleted - this is the documented best-practice choice whenever a true master-detail relationship exists, per Salesforce's architecture guidance to prefer declarative solutions over Apex when the platform already supports the requirement natively. Incorrect - A: an Apex trigger could replicate the same total, but it adds code that must be written, bulk-tested, and maintained (including handling recursion and governor limits) for a requirement the platform already solves out of the box - unnecessary engineering effort compared to the declarative roll-up. Incorrect - B: a quick action is a UI affordance for creating/updating a record or launching a flow from a button; it has no aggregation capability and would not maintain a running total automatically as line items change. Incorrect - D: a formula field only evaluates fields on the record itself (or, cross-object, a single parent's fields) - it cannot reach down and sum an arbitrary number of child records, so it is structurally incapable of producing an Order-level total across many Order Line children.", "id": 270, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 13, "stem": "A development team wants to use a deployment script to automatically deploy to a sandbox during their development cycles. Which two tools can they use to run a script that deploys to a sandbox?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Change Sets"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "SFDX CLI"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ant Migration Tool"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Developer Console"}], "marked": ["B", "C"], "correct": ["B", "C"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: the Salesforce CLI (SFDX) is explicitly documented as a scriptable command-line tool - commands like `sf project deploy start` can be invoked from a shell script, batch file, or CI job with no manual UI interaction, making it a natural fit for an automated deployment script. Correct - C: the Ant Migration Tool is Salesforce's long-standing Java/Ant-based deployment utility, driven entirely by an XML build file and command-line ant targets (deploy/retrieve) - it predates SFDX but is equally scriptable and still documented as a supported deployment mechanism for exactly this kind of automation. Incorrect - A: Change Sets are configured and submitted entirely through a point-and-click Setup UI in the source org, with no command-line or scriptable interface at all - there is no way to invoke a Change Set deployment from an automated script, which is precisely why it is excluded here. Incorrect - D: the Developer Console is an interactive, browser-based tool for writing Apex, running anonymous code, and inspecting logs within a single already-authenticated org; it has no deployment API or scripting interface and cannot push metadata between two orgs at all, let alone automatically.", "id": 271, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 12, "stem": "A developer wrote Apex code that calls out to an external system using REST API. How should a developer write the test to prove the code is working as intended?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Write a class that implements WebServiceMock."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Write a class that extends WebServiceMock."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Write a class that extends HTTPCalloutMock."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Write a class that implements HTTPCalloutMock."}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Apex Developer Guide's testing documentation for HTTP callouts, a test class must implement the HttpCalloutMock interface (providing a respond(HttpRequest) method that returns a fabricated HttpResponse) and then register it with Test.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, mockInstance) before invoking the code under test - since real callouts are blocked inside Apex tests, this mock interface is the documented, required mechanism for simulating a REST response. Incorrect - A and B: WebServiceMock is the equivalent mocking interface for SOAP-based callouts (generated from a WSDL2Apex stub), not REST/HTTP callouts - using it here targets the wrong callout style entirely, and 'implements' vs 'extends' doesn't fix that mismatch. Incorrect - C: interfaces in Apex are always adopted with implements, never extends (extends is reserved for class inheritance) - so 'extends HTTPCalloutMock' is invalid syntax on top of being conceptually a mock interface name being used incorrectly.", "id": 272, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 11, "stem": "A team of developers is working on a source-driven project that allows them to work independently, with many different org configurations. Which type of Salesforce orgs should they use for their development?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Scratch orgs"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Developer sandboxes"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Full Copy sandboxes"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Developer orgs"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: Per the Salesforce DX Developer Guide, scratch orgs are ephemeral, disposable orgs whose entire configuration is defined declaratively by a scratch-org definition file and the metadata pushed from source control - this makes them inherently source-driven (the org's state is fully reproducible from the repo) and lets each developer spin up their own org with whatever specific configuration variant they need, independent of any other developer's org, which is exactly the scenario described. Incorrect - B: Developer sandboxes are copies of a single shared production org's configuration - every developer's sandbox reflects the same underlying production metadata baseline, which does not give the flexibility of 'many different org configurations' that scratch orgs, defined by different scratch-org-definition JSON files, can provide. Incorrect - C: Full Copy sandboxes copy both configuration and all production data, are slow and resource-heavy to refresh, and organizations are typically limited to very few of them - they are used for staging/performance testing, not as a disposable, per-developer, source-driven workspace. Incorrect - D: standalone Developer Edition orgs are not tied to source control or reproducible from a project's metadata definition, and are not part of the documented Salesforce DX source-driven development model at all - they are just free, independent signup orgs.", "id": 273, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 10, "stem": "What should a developer use to script the deployment and unit test execution as part of continuous integration?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Salesforce CLI"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Developer Console"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Execute Anonymous"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "VS Code"}], "marked": ["A"], "correct": ["A"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - A: The Salesforce CLI is documented as the tool built for headless, scriptable automation - its deploy and `sf apex run test` commands can be chained together in a shell script or CI configuration file (Jenkins, GitHub Actions, etc.) with no human interaction, making it the standard choice for scripting deployment plus test execution in a CI pipeline. Incorrect - B: the Developer Console is a browser-based, point-and-click interactive tool intended for a human developer to manually run tests and inspect results; it exposes no command-line or programmatic interface a CI server could invoke. Incorrect - C: Execute Anonymous runs a one-off, unsaved block of Apex code interactively from the Developer Console or VS Code - it is not a mechanism for running the org's actual test classes or for deploying metadata, and has no scriptable CI entry point of its own. Incorrect - D: VS Code with the Salesforce Extension Pack is a desktop IDE meant for a human developer to write and deploy code interactively; while it uses the CLI under the hood, a CI server calls the CLI directly rather than launching a code editor, so VS Code itself is not the tool a pipeline scripts against.", "id": 274, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 9, "stem": "A developer at AW Computing is tasked to create the supporting test class for a programmatic customization that leverages records stored within the custom object, Pricing_Structure__c. AW Computing has a complex pricing structure with more than 500 records. Which two approaches can the developer use to ensure Pricing_Structure__c records are available when the test class is executed?", "type": "multi", "choose": 2, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Use a Test Data Factory class."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Use the @isTest(SeeAllData=true) annotation."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Use the Test.loadTest() method."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Use without sharing on the class declaration."}], "marked": ["B", "D"], "correct": ["A", "C"], "confidence": "verified-web", "flagged": true, "explanation": "Correct - A: Salesforce's testing best practices explicitly recommend a dedicated Test Data Factory class - a reusable Apex utility that programmatically constructs the needed Pricing_Structure__c records (even 500+ of them, in bulk-safe loops) - so tests create their own self-contained, deterministic data rather than depending on whatever happens to already exist in the org. Correct - C: for large or complex pre-built data sets, the Apex Developer Guide documents Test.loadData(Pricing_Structure__c.sObjectType, 'ResourceName'), which loads records from a CSV file uploaded as a static resource directly into a List<Pricing_Structure__c> inside the test - well suited to a 'complex pricing structure with more than 500 records' that might be easier to define once as a static resource than to generate programmatically. Incorrect - B (part of the screenshot's marked answer): @isTest(SeeAllData=true) makes existing org data visible to a test, but it does not create or guarantee any specific Pricing_Structure__c records exist, and relying on whatever data happens to be in the org is explicitly discouraged in Salesforce's own testing documentation as fragile and non-repeatable. Incorrect - D (the other half of the screenshot's marked answer): without sharing only changes whether the running class enforces record-level sharing/visibility rules for the current user - it has nothing to do with creating or making test data 'available'; a class running without sharing still sees zero Pricing_Structure__c records if none were inserted by the test itself.", "id": 275, "bullets": [], "code": null}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 8, "stem": "Which Lightning Web Component custom event property settings enable the event to bubble up the containment hierarchy and cross the Shadow DOM boundary?", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "bubbles: true, composed: false"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "bubbles: false, composed: true"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "bubbles: false, composed: false"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "bubbles: true, composed: true"}], "marked": ["D"], "correct": ["D"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - D: Per the Lightning Web Components Developer Guide's event documentation, the bubbles property controls whether a dispatched CustomEvent propagates upward through ancestor DOM nodes (bubbles: true is required for it to travel up the containment hierarchy at all), while composed independently controls whether the event is allowed to cross shadow DOM boundaries out of the component that fired it (composed: true is required to escape the component's shadow tree). Because the requirement explicitly needs BOTH behaviors simultaneously - bubbling up AND crossing shadow boundaries - both flags must be set to true together, which only option D provides. Incorrect - A: bubbles: true alone lets the event travel upward, but composed: false traps it at the shadow boundary of the component that fired it, so it would never be observable by an ancestor component living outside that shadow tree. Incorrect - B: composed: true alone lets the event cross shadow boundaries, but bubbles: false means it does not propagate up through ancestor elements at all - it would only be dispatched at the originating element itself, never bubbling to a listener higher in the DOM. Incorrect - C: with both flags false, the event neither bubbles nor crosses shadow boundaries, so only a listener attached directly to the exact element that dispatched it (not even its own immediate host, in most cases) could ever observe it - useless for the ancestor-to-descendant communication being asked about.", "id": 276, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 7, "stem": "Which three data types can a SOQL query return?", "type": "multi", "choose": 3, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Double"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Integer"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "sObject"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Long"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "List"}], "marked": ["B", "C", "E"], "correct": ["B", "C", "E"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - E: the most common SOQL result shape is a List of sObjects (e.g., List<Account>), returned whenever a query can match zero or more rows - this is the default, general-purpose return type for SOQL in Apex. Correct - C: a SOQL query can also be assigned directly to a single sObject variable (e.g., Account a = [SELECT Id FROM Account LIMIT 1];) when the developer expects at most one row back. Correct - B: aggregate SOQL functions such as COUNT() return a plain Integer directly (e.g., Integer c = [SELECT COUNT() FROM Account];), rather than a collection, when the query is written specifically as a COUNT() aggregate expression. Incorrect - A and D: Double and Long are valid Apex primitive data types used to hold field values (for example a field of type Double or Long once retrieved), but a SOQL query's own top-level return type - what the query expression itself evaluates to when assigned - is never a bare Double or Long; those types describe individual field values inside a record, not the shape SOQL hands back to Apex.", "id": 277, "bullets": [], "code": null, "flagged": false}, {"exam": "pd1", "number": 6, "stem": "Considering the following code snippet, when the code executes a DML exception is thrown. How should a developer modify the code to ensure exceptions are handled gracefully?", "code": "public static void insertAccounts(List<Account> theseAccounts) {\n    for (Account thisAccount : theseAccounts) {\n        if (thisAccount.website == null) {\n            thisAccount.website = 'https://www.demo.com';\n        }\n    }\n    update theseAccounts;\n}", "type": "single", "choose": 1, "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Remove null items from the list of Accounts."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Implement a try/catch block for the DML."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Implement the upsert DML statement."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Implement Change Data Capture."}], "marked": ["B"], "correct": ["B"], "confidence": "high", "explanation": "Correct - B: wrapping the `update theseAccounts;` statement in a try/catch block that catches DmlException is the standard, documented Apex pattern for handling a DML failure gracefully - it lets the code log the error, notify a user, or take a fallback action instead of letting an unhandled exception propagate and abort the whole transaction, which is exactly what 'handled gracefully' means in the Apex Developer Guide's exception-handling documentation. Incorrect - A: the method's own logic only assigns a default website value when it is null; it doesn't describe removing null Account list items at all, and even if some items in the list were null references, removing them addresses a completely different problem than gracefully handling a thrown DML exception - it does nothing to catch or manage the exception itself. Incorrect - C: switching from `update` to `upsert` changes whether records are matched/inserted-or-updated by Id (or an External ID) - it does not add any exception-handling behavior; an upsert call can throw a DmlException from invalid data just as easily as update, so swapping DML verbs does not by itself achieve graceful error handling. Incorrect - D: Change Data Capture is an entirely unrelated platform feature for publishing record-change events to external subscribers in near-real time - it has no role in catching or handling an exception thrown by a DML statement inside this method.", "id": 278, "bullets": [], "flagged": false}];
